window.TEST_2026 = [{"q":"A 48-year-old woman underwent right modified radical mastectomy with level II axillary clearance for carcinoma breast 3 weeks ago. On follow-up she is unable to raise her right arm fully above her head, and on pushing against a wall with her hands, the medial border of the right scapula protrudes posteriorly (winging of scapula). Which nerve has been injured?","o":["Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)","Thoracodorsal nerve","Intercostobrachial nerve","Medial pectoral nerve"],"a":0,"ex":"Winging of the scapula is pathognomonic of serratus anterior palsy from long thoracic nerve injury (C5-C7), which runs on the superficial surface of serratus anterior and is vulnerable during axillary clearance. Thoracodorsal nerve injury (latissimus dorsi) causes loss of adduction/internal rotation of the arm, not winging. Intercostobrachial nerve injury causes only sensory loss over the medial arm and axilla. Medial pectoral nerve injury weakens pectoralis muscles, sparing scapular mobility. Hook: 'winging = serratus = long thoracic' — also seen after neck dissection or trauma.","tp":"Brachial plexus & nerves of upper limb","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p1","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman presents with tingling and numbness in the thumb, index and middle fingers that wakes her at night and is relieved by shaking her hand (Flick sign). Examination reveals wasting of the thenar eminence. Which of the following structures does NOT pass through the carpal tunnel?","o":["Median nerve","Tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis","Tendons of flexor digitorum profundus","Ulnar nerve and artery"],"a":3,"ex":"The carpal tunnel contains the median nerve, four FDS tendons, four FDP tendons and the flexor pollicis longus tendon, all covered by synovial sheaths. The ulnar nerve and artery pass superficial to the flexor retinaculum through Guyon's canal, which is why ulnar nerve function is spared in carpal tunnel syndrome. Hook: 'Ulnar is OUT — Guyon's canal, never the carpal tunnel.'","tp":"Carpal tunnel & median nerve","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p2","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a motorcycle accident with a fracture of the neck of the right fibula. Three days later he develops foot drop, is unable to evert the foot, and complains of tingling over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve is injured?","o":["Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Common fibular (peroneal) nerve","Saphenous nerve"],"a":2,"ex":"The common fibular nerve winds around the neck of the fibula and is the most commonly injured nerve in fibular neck fractures. It divides into the deep branch (tibialis anterior — dorsiflexion) and superficial branch (peroneus longus/brevis — eversion), so injury causes foot drop with loss of eversion and sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. Tibial nerve injury affects plantar flexion. Sural and saphenous nerves are purely sensory. Hook: 'neck of fibula = foot drop; winds around the neck like a scarf.'","tp":"Sciatic nerve & its divisions","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p3","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old man develops milky, triglyceride-rich pleural fluid 4 days after transhiatal esophagectomy for carcinoma esophagus. Injury to the thoracic duct is suspected. Into which junction does the thoracic duct normally drain?","o":["Left brachiocephalic venous junction","Junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins (left venous angle)","Junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins","Junction with the azygos vein"],"a":1,"ex":"The thoracic duct ascends in the posterior mediastinum, crosses from the right to the left side behind the esophagus at the level of T5, arches above the left pleura and terminates at the left venous angle (junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins). Chyle leak produces milky pleural fluid with triglycerides >110 mg/dL. Hook: 'crosses at T5, ends at the left venous angle' — the right lymphatic duct drains the opposite angle.","tp":"Thoracic duct & mediastinum","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p4","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with hypertension presents with sudden painless loss of vision in the left half of the visual field of both eyes. MRI shows an infarct involving the right medial occipital lobe; the macular region of the visual cortex is spared. Occlusion of which artery explains these findings?","o":["Left posterior cerebral artery","Right middle cerebral artery","Right posterior cerebral artery","Right anterior cerebral artery"],"a":2,"ex":"The posterior cerebral artery supplies the occipital (visual) cortex; its occlusion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Macular sparing occurs because the macular representation at the occipital pole receives a dual supply from the PCA and MCA. MCA occlusion causes contralateral hemiplegia and hemisensory loss (with aphasia or neglect), and ACA occlusion causes contralateral leg-predominant weakness — neither produces isolated visual loss. Hook: 'PCA = posterior = visual field loss; macula has two patrons, PCA and MCA.'","tp":"Arterial supply of brain (circle of Willis)","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p5","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman squeezed a furuncle on her nose and 2 days later developed high-grade fever, proptosis, chemosis and ophthalmoplegia with dilated pupils. Cavernous sinus thrombosis is suspected. Which cranial nerve runs INSIDE the cavernous sinus (not in its lateral wall) and is therefore most directly compressed by an enlarging thrombus or aneurysm?","o":["Abducens nerve (CN VI)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1)"],"a":0,"ex":"Within the cavernous sinus lie the internal carotid artery and the abducens nerve (CN VI), while CN III, CN IV, V1 and V2 are embedded in its lateral wall. CN VI has the longest intracranial course and is the earliest and most commonly affected nerve in cavernous sinus thrombosis, producing failure of abduction with medial deviation of the eye. Infection spreads from the 'danger area' of the face via the ophthalmic veins. Hook: '6 inside, 4 on the wall: III, IV, V1, V2.'","tp":"Dural venous sinuses (cavernous sinus)","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p6","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman presents with acute onset of right-sided facial weakness with inability to close the right eye and flattening of the right nasolabial fold; the forehead muscles are also involved. She retains normal taste sensation and lacrimation. At which site is the facial nerve lesion?","o":["Geniculate ganglion","Internal auditory meatus","Stylomastoid foramen","Cerebellopontine angle"],"a":2,"ex":"A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen, as in classic Bell's palsy, paralyzes all muscles of facial expression but spares taste and lacrimation because the chorda tympani and greater petrosal nerve arise more proximally within the facial canal. Lesions at the geniculate ganglion or internal auditory meatus add loss of taste, hyperacusis (nerve to stapedius) and lacrimation, and often involve CN VIII. CP angle lesions additionally cause deafness and vertigo. Hook: 'Below the foramen = pure motor palsy; above it = taste, tears and sound join in.'","tp":"Facial nerve & parotid region","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p7","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 19-year-old man presents with 6 hours of pain initially felt around the umbilicus that has now shifted to the right iliac fossa, along with two episodes of vomiting and low-grade fever. The initial periumbilical pain is mediated by which of the following?","o":["Somatic afferents of the T12 spinal nerve","Visceral afferents accompanying the sympathetic supply (T10)","Visceral afferents accompanying the parasympathetic supply (L1)","Somatic afferents of the L1 spinal nerve"],"a":1,"ex":"The appendix is a midgut derivative, so early appendicular pain is visceral, carried by sympathetic afferents from T10 and referred to the periumbilical region — a diffuse, poorly localized pain. Once inflammation involves the parietal peritoneum, somatic afferents (T12, L1 via iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves) localize pain to McBurney's point. Hook: 'Visceral = T10 midline; somatic = where it hurts later, the RIF.'","tp":"Appendix & visceral referred pain","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p8","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with a small, irreducible swelling in the right groin, below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, appearing on standing and associated with mild colicky pain. A femoral hernia is diagnosed. Which ligament forms the medial boundary of the femoral ring, where strangulation of such a hernia occurs?","o":["Inguinal ligament","Pectineal (Cooper's) ligament","Femoral vein","Lacunar (Gimbernat's) ligament"],"a":3,"ex":"The femoral ring is bounded anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, posteriorly by the pectineal (Cooper's) ligament and pectineus, medially by the lacunar (Gimbernat's) ligament, and laterally by the femoral vein. A femoral hernia passes below the inguinal ligament, lies lateral to the pubic tubercle, and is more common in women; its narrow ring with rigid Gimbernat's ligament medially accounts for the high strangulation rate. Hook: 'Ring: ant-inguinal, post-Cooper, med-Gimbernat, lat-femoral vein.'","tp":"Femoral triangle & femoral hernia","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p9","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with sudden severe left lower abdominal pain and nausea; transvaginal ultrasound shows an enlarged left ovary with absent arterial flow, suggesting ovarian torsion, and emergency surgery is planned. From which artery does the ovarian artery normally arise?","o":["Internal iliac artery","Abdominal aorta (at L2)","External iliac artery","Common iliac artery"],"a":1,"ex":"The ovarian artery arises directly from the abdominal aorta at the level of L2, just below the renal arteries, and descends across the pelvic brim to reach the mesovarium; it anastomoses at the ovarian hilum with the uterine artery branch of the internal iliac artery. This dual supply explains why ovarian viability depends on the competence of both vessels when torsion is released. Hook: 'Gonads spring from the aorta at L2 — ovary and testis alike.'","tp":"Blood supply of ovary & pelvis","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p10","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old primigravida at 30 weeks gestation has polyhydramnios. Ultrasonography shows herniated bowel loops within the left hemithorax and the fetal stomach in the thorax. The defect causing this condition results from failure of fusion of which embryonic structure?","o":["Septum transversum","Pleuropericardial membrane","Cervical myotomes of the diaphragm","Pleuroperitoneal membrane"],"a":3,"ex":"Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek type) results from failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to close the posterolateral diaphragmatic defect, which is 4 times more common on the left, allowing abdominal viscera to enter the thorax. The septum transversum forms the central tendon of the diaphragm (its failure causes eventration of the diaphragm, not herniation of bowel), and the pleuropericardial membrane failure causes pericardial defects. Hook: 'Bochdalek = posterolateral = pleuroperitoneal membrane.'","tp":"Embryology of diaphragm","s":"Anatomy","qid":"p11","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with a 10-year history of alcohol use disorder presents with progressive dyspnea, pedal edema and palpitations for 3 weeks. Examination shows tachycardia, elevated jugular venous pressure, warm extremities with bounding pulses, and bilateral lateral rectus palsy. Blood gas shows lactic acidosis (lactate 6.2 mmol/L). Which enzyme is most likely to have markedly reduced activity in this patient?","o":["Pyruvate carboxylase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate kinase","Glutamate dehydrogenase"],"a":1,"ex":"This is wet beriberi (thiamine deficiency) with high-output cardiac failure and Wernicke encephalopathy (ophthalmoplegia). Thiamine is the cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenase and transketolase; PDH impairment diverts pyruvate to lactate, explaining the lactic acidosis. Pyruvate carboxylase is biotin-dependent, pyruvate kinase is a glycolytic enzyme with no cofactor requirement, and glutamate dehydrogenase uses NAD/NADP. Hook: 'Beriberi → Big heart, Bad brain, Blood lactate up.'","tp":"Vitamin B1 (thiamine) deficiency","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p12","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 5-day-old term neonate is detected on newborn screening to have blood phenylalanine of 1150 µmol/L (normal <120) with a normal tyrosine level. The infant is asymptomatic and feeding well. Before initiating dietary phenylalanine restriction, which investigation is essential to exclude a treatable cofactor deficiency that presents identically?","o":["Urine pterin analysis (biopterin and neopterin profile) with a tetrahydrobiopterin loading test","Serum ammonia level","Liver biopsy for enzyme histochemistry","RBC galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase assay"],"a":0,"ex":"Hyperphenylalaninemia is caused either by phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency (classical PKU) or by defects in tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis/regeneration, since BH4 is the cofactor for PAH. BH4 deficiency must be excluded before committing to dietary therapy because it requires BH4 supplementation and neurotransmitter precursors (L-DOPA, 5-hydroxytryptophan); phenylalanine restriction alone fails and risks neurologic damage. Serum ammonia and galactose assays address unrelated disorders, and liver enzyme assay is not the diagnostic approach in the newborn period.","tp":"Phenylketonuria / tetrahydrobiopterin deficiency","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p13","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 3-day-old term male neonate presents with poor feeding, lethargy progressing to coma and tachypnea. Blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis, and plasma ammonia is 790 µmol/L (normal <110). The infant is not jaundiced. Which is the most appropriate initial management of this infant?","o":["Protein restriction alone with monitoring of ammonia","Hemodialysis with intravenous sodium benzoate/phenylbutyrate and arginine","Lactulose enemas with rifaximin","Insulin and glucose infusion"],"a":1,"ex":"Severe neonatal hyperammonemia with respiratory alkalosis is a urea cycle disorder (most commonly X-linked ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency). Acute management is nitrogen scavengers (sodium benzoate, sodium phenylbutyrate), arginine supplementation (becomes an essential amino acid), and hemodialysis when ammonia exceeds ~500 µmol/L or with encephalopathy. Protein restriction alone is adjunctive and too slow for life-threatening hyperammonemia; lactulose targets acquired hyperammonemia of cirrhosis; insulin-glucose is for hyperkalemia or to suppress catabolism only as an adjunct. Hook: 'Newborn + coma + high ammonia + respiratory alkalosis = urea cycle.'","tp":"Urea cycle disorders","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p14","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"An 8-month-old infant is brought for abdominal distension and failure to thrive. Examination reveals a doll-like face, marked hepatomegaly, and a protuberant abdomen. After a 4-hour fast, blood glucose is 32 mg/dL with lactate 6.8 mmol/L; uric acid and triglycerides are elevated. Which test would confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Assay of glucose-6-phosphatase activity in a liver biopsy","Assay of muscle phosphorylase activity","Assay of acid α-glucosidase (acid maltase) activity","Assay of debranching enzyme activity"],"a":0,"ex":"This is von Gierke disease (GSD type I) — hepatomegaly, severe fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia due to glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency, which is confirmed by liver enzyme assay. Muscle phosphorylase deficiency is McArdle disease (GSD V, exercise-induced cramps/myoglobinuria), acid α-glucosidase deficiency is Pompe disease (GSD II, cardiomyopathy/hypotonia), and debranching enzyme deficiency is Cori disease (GSD III). Hook: 'von Gierke = hypoglycemia + lactate + gout + fat.'","tp":"Glycogen storage diseases","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p15","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy presents with tall stature, long slender fingers, and a 2-year history of a painless visual disturbance. Slit-lamp examination shows inferior (downward) dislocation of the lens in both eyes. He had a deep vein thrombosis after a minor leg injury and performs poorly at school. Which of the following is the most likely underlying defect?","o":["Deficiency of cystathionine β-synthase","Mutation in the fibrillin-1 gene","Deficiency of lysyl hydroxylase","Deficiency of homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase"],"a":0,"ex":"Downward (inferior) lens dislocation plus thromboembolism and intellectual disability points to homocystinuria from cystathionine β-synthase deficiency, causing elevated homocysteine with connective tissue and thrombotic manifestations. Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1 mutation) has upward (superior) lens dislocation, aortic dilatation, and normal intelligence; lysyl hydroxylase deficiency causes Ehlers-Danlos kyphoscoliotic type; homogentisate dioxygenase deficiency causes alkaptonuria with ochronosis and dark urine. Hook: 'Lens goes DOWN with homocystinuria, UP with Marfan.'","tp":"Homocystinuria","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p16","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman is admitted with severe colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and anxiety. Two days ago she took a barbiturate-containing sleeping pill for insomnia. Examination is normal apart from mild tachycardia; serum sodium is 128 mEq/L. Her urine, which was amber on voiding, has turned dark red after standing in light. Which enzyme deficiency best explains her presentation?","o":["Porphobilinogen deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase)","Uroporphyrinogen III cosynthase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Ferrochelatase"],"a":0,"ex":"This is acute intermittent porphyria — neurovisceral attacks (abdominal pain, psychiatric symptoms, neuropathy, SIADH-related hyponatremia) with urine that darkens on standing due to porphobilinogen, precipitated by barbiturates which induce ALA synthase. AIP is caused by porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency. Uroporphyrinogen III cosynthase deficiency causes congenital erythropoietic porphyria (severe photosensitivity, red urine since birth); uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency causes porphyria cutanea tarda (blistering, no neurovisceral attacks); ferrochelatase deficiency causes erythropoietic protoporphyria (immediate painful photosensitivity).","tp":"Porphyrias","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p17","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 20-year-old male medical student is found incidentally to have serum bilirubin of 3.1 mg/dL with a conjugated fraction of 0.2 mg/dL. He reports mild yellowish discoloration of eyes during examination stress and after skipping meals, with no other symptoms. His hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, LFTs and peripheral smear are normal. What is the most likely underlying abnormality?","o":["Reduced activity of hepatic UDP-glucuronosyltransferase","Defective canalicular export pump (MRP2) for conjugated bilirubin","Deficiency of glucose-6-phosphatase","Reduced bile salt export pump activity"],"a":0,"ex":"This is Gilbert syndrome — benign unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal hemolysis and LFTs, worsened by fasting and stress, due to reduced (approximately 30%) UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity; it needs no treatment. Dubin-Johnson syndrome (MRP2 defect) gives conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with a black liver, and bile salt export pump defects (PFIC type 2) cause progressive cholestasis in infancy, not this picture. Hook: 'Gilbert = young, jaundiced after fasting, everything else normal.'","tp":"Gilbert syndrome","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p18","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 5-day-old breastfed neonate develops poor feeding, lethargy and vomiting. On examination, the infant is hypotonic with episodic opisthotonus. The mother reports a sweet, burnt-sugar smell of the urine and diapers. Urine dipstick shows ketones. Plasma amino acid analysis shows markedly elevated leucine, isoleucine and valine. Which enzyme is deficient?","o":["Branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase","3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase"],"a":0,"ex":"Maple syrup urine disease results from branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase deficiency, causing accumulation of leucine, isoleucine and valine with a classic maple-syrup odor and ketosis; leucine is neurotoxic causing the encephalopathy. Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase deficiency (methylmalonic acidemia) and propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency (propionic acidemia) cause severe metabolic acidosis with hyperammonemia but not this odor; HMG-CoA lyase deficiency presents with fasting hypoglycemia, metabolic acidosis and no organic aciduria of this pattern. Hook: 'Sweet-smelling neonate with ketoacidosis = MSUD.'","tp":"Maple syrup urine disease","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p19","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p20","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A patient presents with a neck mass causing tracheal and esophageal compression, and histology shows cell nests with pink extracellular amyloid. What is the tumor’s cell of origin?","o":["Parafollicular C cells","Askanazy cells","Hurthle cells","Follicular cells"],"a":0,"ex":"The histopathological description of \"cell nests and pink extracellular amyloid stroma\" is the pathognomonic finding for Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (MTC). The amyloid material consists of deposited calcitonin precursor proteins. MTC is a neuroendocrine tumor that arises from the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid gland. These cells are responsible for producing the hormone calcitonin. Follicular cells (D) give rise to papillary and follicular thyroid carcinomas. Hurthle cells or Askanazy cells (B, C) are eosinophilic, mitochondrion-rich follicular cells that form Hürthle cell adenomas and carcinomas. The presence of amyloid is the key feature pointing specifically to a tumor of C-cell origin.","tp":"Muscle Contraction (Tetanizing Frequency)","s":"Pathology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is rescued from a house fire with smoke inhalation. He is confused, tachypneic, and hypotensive. Arterial blood gas shows pH 7.08, lactate 11 mmol/L; co-oximetry shows carboxyhemoglobin 8% and a high mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2 82%). Despite oxygen supplementation, his lactic acidosis worsens. Which component of the electron transport chain is directly inhibited in this patient?","o":["NADH dehydrogenase (complex I)","Succinate dehydrogenase (complex II)","Cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV)","ATP synthase (complex V)"],"a":2,"ex":"Cyanide gas from burning synthetic materials inhibits cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV), blocking electron transfer to oxygen; cells cannot use O2, so venous blood remains oxygenated (arterialized, high SvO2) with severe lactic acidosis and carboxyhemoglobin often low — the key clue differentiating cyanide from CO poisoning. Treatment is hydroxocobalamin (or sodium thiosulfate). Complex I inhibition causes lactic acidosis from metformin; complex II and V are not cyanide targets. Hook: 'Cyanide = tissues suffocate in an ocean of oxygen.'","tp":"Electron transport chain / cyanide poisoning","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p21","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman on category I anti-tubercular therapy for the past 3 months develops progressive tingling, numbness and burning pain in both hands and feet, with reduced ankle jerks. Which micronutrient deficiency is responsible, and what should be added to her regimen?","o":["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","Niacin (vitamin B3)","Riboflavin (vitamin B2)","Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5)"],"a":0,"ex":"Isoniazid inactivates pyridoxine by forming a hydrazone with pyridoxal phosphate, producing a peripheral neuropathy, especially in slow acetylators and malnourished patients; pyridoxine (10–25 mg/day) is co-administered for prevention. Niacin deficiency causes pellagra (dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia) — also possible with INH but does not cause this isolated neuropathy; riboflavin and pantothenic acid deficiencies do not produce this picture. Hook: 'INH steals B6 → neuroparesthesias.'","tp":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine)","s":"Biochemistry","qid":"p22","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy (left ventricular ejection fraction 28%, NYHA class III) presents with worsening exertional dyspnoea. Compared with a healthy heart, his left ventricular Frank-Starling curve is shifted downward and to the right. Which physiological change best explains this shift?","o":["Chronic volume overload causing sarcomere stretch beyond optimal length","Sustained increase in afterload from elevated systemic vascular resistance","Reduced intrinsic myocardial contractility from loss of functioning myocytes","Downregulation of myocardial beta-adrenergic receptors"],"a":2,"ex":"The Frank-Starling curve plots stroke work against preload (LV end-diastolic volume/pressure). Reduced myocardial contractility — from myocyte loss and impaired actin-myosin interaction — shifts the entire curve down and to the right, so that even high preloads generate subnormal stroke work; this is the hallmark of systolic heart failure. Chronic volume overload moves the operating point along the curve, not the curve itself, and in intact human hearts the descending limb is not reached. Beta-receptor downregulation is a consequence of chronic sympathetic activation and further depresses contractility but is not the primary cause of the initial curve shift. Memory hook: 'Contractility shifts the curve; preload and afterload move the operating point along it.'","tp":"Heart failure physiology","s":"Physiology","qid":"p23","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman develops sudden dyspnoea and pleuritic chest pain 5 days after a long-haul flight. Arterial blood gas on room air: pH 7.48, PaCO2 32 mmHg, PaO2 58 mmHg. CTPA confirms bilateral segmental pulmonary emboli. Which mechanism best explains the hypoxaemia in this patient?","o":["Alveolar hypoventilation causing a parallel rise in PaCO2","A large increase in physiological dead space with redistribution of blood flow to low V/Q units","A pure right-to-left shunt from collapsed alveolar units","Diffusion limitation across a thickened alveolar-capillary membrane"],"a":1,"ex":"Pulmonary embolism creates ventilated but unperfused alveoli (increased physiological dead space), and blood is redirected to remaining well-ventilated, overperfused units producing V/Q mismatch — the dominant cause of hypoxaemia here. The low PaCO2 with respiratory alkalosis (pH 7.48) rules out alveolar hypoventilation, which would raise PaCO2. A pure shunt would not show significant improvement with 100% oxygen and is not the primary mechanism of acute PE; diffusion limitation characterises chronic interstitial lung disease. Memory hook: 'PE = ventilated but not perfused (dead space) — and V/Q mismatch causes the hypoxaemia.'","tp":"Ventilation-perfusion mismatch","s":"Physiology","qid":"p24","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and CKD stage 3a (eGFR 62 mL/min/1.73 m2, urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 450 mg/g) is started on empagliflozin to slow progression of diabetic nephropathy. Which renal physiological mechanism underlies the renoprotective effect of SGLT2 inhibitors?","o":["SGLT2 inhibition dilates the afferent arteriole, increasing GFR","Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa restores tubuloglomerular feedback, constricting the afferent arteriole and reducing intraglomerular hypertension","Reduced Na-K-2Cl cotransport in the thick ascending limb lowers medullary osmolality","Inhibition of angiotensin II synthesis by proximal tubular cells"],"a":1,"ex":"In hyperglycaemia, heavy proximal tubular reabsorption of glucose and sodium leaves reduced NaCl delivery to the macula densa, which suppresses tubuloglomerular feedback and causes afferent arteriolar vasodilation, intraglomerular hypertension and hyperfiltration. SGLT2 inhibitors block proximal Na-glucose cotransport, restoring NaCl delivery to the macula densa and re-activating TGF, which constricts the afferent arteriole, lowers intraglomerular pressure and reduces albuminuria — the physiological basis of their renoprotection in DAPA-CKD/EMPA-KIDNEY. Afferent dilation (option A) is the diabetic pathophysiological state, not the drug effect; the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter is the loop diuretic site, and the drug does not inhibit local angiotensin II synthesis. Memory hook: 'SGLT2i = more NaCl past the macula densa = the afferent arteriole gets a brake.'","tp":"Tubuloglomerular feedback / diabetic kidney","s":"Physiology","qid":"p25","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has been on prednisolone 20 mg/day for 18 months and is now being tapered off. Before completing steroid withdrawal, which single investigation is the best screening test for recovery of the hypothalamo-pituitary-adrenal axis?","o":["Early morning (8 AM) serum cortisol","Random serum cortisol","24-hour urinary free cortisol","Plasma ACTH after ovine corticotropin-releasing hormone"],"a":0,"ex":"An early-morning (8 AM) serum cortisol above approximately 13-14 mcg/dL (350-400 nmol/L) reliably predicts an intact HPA axis and allows safe steroid discontinuation, while a very low morning cortisol (<5 mcg/dL) indicates suppression warranting formal testing (ACTH stimulation test or insulin tolerance test). Random cortisol levels are uninterpretable because of circadian variation and stress; 24-hour urinary free cortisol reflects total daily secretion, not functional reserve; and oCRH stimulation is a specialised test, not a screening tool. Memory hook: 'Morning cortisol first — ACTH stimulation only when morning cortisol is equivocal or low.'","tp":"HPA axis / steroid withdrawal","s":"Physiology","qid":"p26","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with recurrent duodenal ulcers and chronic watery diarrhoea has serum gastrin of 850 pg/mL, and imaging reveals a pancreatic gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Which cell-mediator pathway predominantly drives the marked gastric acid hypersecretion in this patient?","o":["G cells releasing gastrin that directly stimulates parietal cells","D cells losing somatostatin inhibition of parietal cells","ECL cells releasing histamine that acts on H2 receptors on parietal cells","Chief cells secreting pepsinogen that potentiates H+ secretion"],"a":2,"ex":"Gastrin's acid-stimulating effect is largely indirect: it acts on enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells, which release histamine that stimulates parietal cell H2 receptors — the ECL cell is the 'amplifier' of acid secretion. The clinical efficacy of H2 receptor antagonists in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome confirms the dominance of this pathway. G cells are the source, not the target, of the excess gastrin; D cells are inhibited (somatostatin loss contributes but is not the primary driver); chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which does not stimulate H+ secretion. Memory hook: 'Gastrin → ECL → histamine → H2 receptor: acid's amplifier chain.'","tp":"Gastric secretion physiology","s":"Physiology","qid":"p27","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetic kidney disease (eGFR 25 mL/min/1.73 m2) presents with serum potassium 6.9 mmol/L and tall, peaked T waves on ECG. Which electrophysiological change best explains the initial membrane hyperexcitability of myocardial cells in hyperkalaemia?","o":["Hyperpolarisation of the resting membrane potential away from threshold","Depolarisation of the resting membrane potential toward the threshold potential","Prolongation of the absolute refractory period","Blockade of voltage-gated sodium channels at rest"],"a":1,"ex":"Resting membrane potential is governed by the potassium equilibrium potential (EK), and raising extracellular K+ makes EK less negative, depolarising the resting membrane potential toward threshold. Cells therefore fire more readily — seen as tall, peaked T waves — before severe hyperkalaemia later causes reduced excitability and conduction block. Hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia, hyperpolarises the membrane; prolonged refractory periods and sodium-channel inactivation occur only at extreme, late-stage elevations. Memory hook: 'HyperK+ = closer to threshold; HypoK+ = farther from threshold.'","tp":"Membrane potential / potassium","s":"Physiology","qid":"p28","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigable ptosis and diplopia that worsen through the day. Anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies are positive. Which step of neuromuscular transmission is affected by the disease process?","o":["Synthesis and packaging of acetylcholine in the motor nerve terminal","Calcium-dependent quantal release of acetylcholine from presynaptic terminals","Binding of acetylcholine to postsynaptic nicotinic receptors at the motor end-plate","Hydrolysis of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase in the synaptic cleft"],"a":2,"ex":"Myasthenia gravis is an antibody-mediated loss of postsynaptic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate, so fewer ACh molecules can bind and the end-plate potential margin for triggering muscle action potentials shrinks — hence fatigable weakness. Presynaptic synthesis is intact, and presynaptic release defects characterise Lambert-Eaton syndrome (and botulism); acetylcholinesterase is the target of organophosphates, and its inhibition by pyridostigmine is the treatment that relieves MG by prolonging ACh action. Memory hook: 'MG is postsynaptic, LEMS is presynaptic.'","tp":"Neuromuscular transmission","s":"Physiology","qid":"p29","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p30","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A young man presents with skin lesions as shown in the image. Identify the cause.","o":["Sporothrix schenckii","Staphylococcus aureus","Nocardia asteroides","Mycobacterium marinum"],"a":1,"ex":"The image shows multiple erythematous, tender nodular skin lesions with pustular and crusted centers clustered over the limb. This appearance is typical of furuncles or carbuncles, which are deep follicular infections most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus. Staphylococcus aureus is a gram-positive coccus that commonly infects hair follicles, leading to localized abscess formation. The lesions may begin as painful red papules and progress to fluctuant nodules with purulent discharge. In contrast: 1. Sporothrix schenckii causes sporotrichosis, which typically spreads along lymphatics in a linear pattern (sporotrichoid spread). 2. Nocardia asteroides may cause cutaneous abscesses but often presents with draining sinuses. 3. Mycobacterium marinum produces swimming pool granuloma, characterized by nodular lesions along lymphatic channels. Since the lesions shown are clustered abscess-like nodules consistent with furunculosis, the most likely causative organism is Staphylococcus aureus.","tp":"Placenta Accreta","s":"Dermatology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with small cell lung cancer has serum sodium 120 mmol/L, plasma osmolality 245 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 650 mOsm/kg and urine sodium 60 mmol/L, with clinical euvolemia. Which physiological abnormality best explains this picture?","o":["Primary renal sodium wasting with secondary water retention","Adrenal insufficiency with cortisol and aldosterone deficiency","Excessive water intake exceeding maximum renal water excretion","Impaired renal water excretion due to persistent ADH action producing inappropriately concentrated urine"],"a":3,"ex":"In this syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion, persistently secreted ADH (from the tumour) forces the kidneys to make concentrated urine (urine osmolality far above 100 mOsm/kg) despite plasma hypotonicity, so ingested water cannot be excreted and dilutional hyponatremia results while the patient stays euvolemic with high urine sodium. Renal sodium wasting and adrenal insufficiency produce hypovolemia with hypotension and, in the latter, hyperkalemia; primary polydipsia yields maximally dilute urine (low urine osmolality) because ADH is suppressed. Memory hook: 'Hyponatremia with urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg = a water-excretion defect, i.e. ADH.'","tp":"SIADH / water balance","s":"Physiology","qid":"p31","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 24-year-old man with sepsis has a temperature of 39.2 deg C and is shivering with cold, pale extremities. Which of the following best describes the physiological state during this chill phase of fever?","o":["Core temperature has risen above the hypothalamic set point, activating heat-loss mechanisms","Endogenous pyrogens directly inhibit shivering thermogenesis","Cutaneous vasodilation is dominant, with sweating occurring despite shivering","The hypothalamic set point is elevated above the actual core temperature, so heat-gain mechanisms are activated"],"a":3,"ex":"Pyrogens (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) induce PGE2 synthesis in the preoptic hypothalamus, raising the temperature set point. During the chill phase the core temperature is still below the new set point, so heat-gain effectors — shivering, cutaneous vasoconstriction (cold, pale skin) and nonshivering thermogenesis — are activated until core temperature matches the set point. Heat-loss mechanisms (sweating, vasodilation) dominate during defervescence, when the set point falls, and pyrogens stimulate rather than inhibit shivering. Memory hook: 'Fever up = shiver; fever down = sweat.'","tp":"Fever / thermoregulation","s":"Physiology","qid":"p32","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old trekker reaches an altitude of 4,200 m in the Himalayas and within hours develops breathlessness on exertion, a headache and tachycardia. Which compensatory response occurs within the first hours of ascent?","o":["An increase in ventilation mediated by peripheral chemoreceptor stimulation by hypoxia","A rise in haemoglobin concentration from erythropoietin-induced marrow stimulation","An increase in red cell 2,3-DPG shifting the oxygen dissociation curve rightward","Renal retention of bicarbonate to compensate for respiratory alkalosis"],"a":0,"ex":"Acute hypobaric hypoxia stimulates the carotid body peripheral chemoreceptors, producing the hypoxic ventilatory response — hyperventilation within minutes to hours of ascent — which is the first and most important acute acclimatisation mechanism (the resulting respiratory alkalosis then limits further ventilation). Erythropoietin-driven red cell mass expansion takes weeks; the 2,3-DPG rise occurs over days; and renal bicarbonate excretion (not retention) slowly compensates the respiratory alkalosis over days. Memory hook: 'Seconds-hours: ventilation; hours-days: 2,3-DPG and renal compensation; weeks: EPO and red cells.'","tp":"High-altitude physiology","s":"Physiology","qid":"p33","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 24-year-old man presents with fever, gum bleeding and fatigue for 3 weeks. Hb 8.2 g/dL, WBC 48,000/μL with 60% blasts showing abundant Auer rods and granulocytic maturation, platelets 25,000/μL. Bone marrow aspirate shows myeloblasts with bilobed nuclei. Which cytogenetic abnormality is most likely in this patient?","o":["t(15;17) PML-RARA","t(8;21) RUNX1-RUNX1T1","inv(16) CBFB-MYH11","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1"],"a":1,"ex":"t(8;21), producing the RUNX1-RUNX1T1 (AML1-ETO) fusion, defines AML with myeloblastic maturation (M2) with abundant Auer rods and bilobed ('salmon-pink') blasts; it is a favorable-risk AML. t(15;17) causes APL with DIC, faggot cells (multiple Auer rods in bundles) and responds to ATRA — unlikely without coagulopathy. inv(16) marks M4Eo with abnormal eosinophils. t(9;22) is CML, excluded by the marrow picture. Hook: granulocytic maturation + Auer rods = t(8;21).","tp":"AML cytogenetics","s":"Pathology","qid":"p34","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman presents with a 2-month history of vague right breast thickening; mammogram shows architectural distortion without a discrete mass. Core biopsy shows single-file cords of small discohesive cells infiltrating desmoplastic stroma, with occasional signet-ring cells. Immunohistochemistry is negative for E-cadherin. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Invasive ductal carcinoma (NST)","Mucinous carcinoma","Invasive lobular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma"],"a":2,"ex":"Invasive lobular carcinoma (ILC) is the classic cause of a diffuse breast thickening with no discrete mass, showing 'Indian file' single-cell cords and loss of E-cadherin (mutation in CDH1); it has a higher rate of bilateral disease and metastasizes to meninges, GI tract and ovaries. Invasive ductal carcinoma forms glandular tubules and is E-cadherin positive. Mucinous and medullary carcinomas have circumscribed gelatinous/syncytial architecture. Hook: 'lobular = linear (single file), lost E-cadherin.'","tp":"Invasive lobular carcinoma","s":"Pathology","qid":"p35","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 60-year-old man presents with melena and fatigue. CT abdomen shows a 7 cm exophytic, well-defined mass arising from the gastric wall. Biopsy shows interlacing fascicles of spindle cells with elongated nuclei. Immunohistochemistry: KIT (CD117) positive, DOG1 positive, desmin negative, S100 negative. Which of the following predicts response to imatinib therapy in this patient?","o":["Desmin positivity","S100 positivity","KIT exon 11 mutation","MDM2 amplification"],"a":2,"ex":"This is a GIST; KIT exon 11 mutations (most common, ~60-70%) predict excellent response to imatinib, an oral tyrosine kinase inhibitor. Exon 9 KIT mutations need higher imatinib doses; PDGFRA D842V and SDH-deficient (wild-type) GISTs are imatinib-resistant. Desmin and S100 positivity would favor leiomyoma and neural tumors respectively, and are negative here. MDM2 amplification is seen in dedifferentiated liposarcoma. Hook: 'KIT exon 11 = imatinib bliss.'","tp":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","s":"Pathology","qid":"p36","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman presents with progressive cervical and mediastinal lymphadenopathy, low-grade fever, night sweats and pruritus. Excisional biopsy shows nodular architecture with collagen bands surrounding nodules of lymphoid tissue; within them are large cells with pale, multilobated nuclei and small nucleoli (lacunar cells) plus classic Reed-Sternberg cells (CD15+, CD30+, CD45−). Which subtype of Hodgkin lymphoma is most likely?","o":["Mixed cellularity","Lymphocyte depleted","Lymphocyte predominant (NLPHL)","Nodular sclerosis"],"a":3,"ex":"Nodular sclerosis HL is the most common subtype, affecting young women with mediastinal masses; it is defined by collagen band sclerosis and lacunar cells (Reed-Sternberg variants retracted in formalin). Mixed cellularity is more common in older/HIV patients with abdominal disease. Lymphocyte-depleted is rare, aggressive, in older men. NLPHL shows 'popcorn' (LP) cells that are CD20+ and CD15/CD30 negative — different from classical RS cells. Hook: 'Young woman + mediastinal mass + lacunar cells = nodular sclerosis; front-line therapy is ABVD.'","tp":"Hodgkin lymphoma subtypes","s":"Pathology","qid":"p37","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has back pain, fatigue and weight loss. Investigations: Hb 9.8 g/dL, ESR 120 mm/hr, serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL, calcium 9.0 mg/dL, serum IgG kappa M-protein 2.1 g/dL, bone marrow shows 35% clonal plasma cells, and MRI shows no focal lesions. Per IMWG SLiM-CRAB criteria, which single finding, if present, would classify him as active myeloma needing treatment even without end-organ damage?","o":["Serum M-protein of 2 g/dL","Clonal bone marrow plasma cells of 60% or more","Hemoglobin of 7.5 g/dL","Generalized osteopenia on X-ray"],"a":1,"ex":"The 'Si' of SLiM-CRAB: ≥60% clonal marrow plasma cells alone defines active myeloma regardless of CRAB features (also >1 focal MRI lesion ≥5 mm, or free light chain ratio ≥100). An M-protein of 2 g/dL is below the usual 3 g/dL diagnostic threshold and is not a standalone SLiM criterion. Anemia and osteopenia are CRAB (end-organ) features — the patient here lacks them, so he remains smoldering myeloma. Hook: 'Sixty percent, Light chain ratio ≥100, more than one MRI lesion — SiLiM defines myeloma without CRAB.'","tp":"Multiple myeloma (SLiM-CRAB)","s":"Pathology","qid":"p38","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 15 years has hypertension and urine protein 3.4 g/day with creatinine 2.1 mg/dL. Renal biopsy shows eosinophilic nodular mesangial lesions, hyaline arteriolosclerosis and mild interstitial fibrosis. Which of the following is the EARLIEST morphologic change in diabetic nephropathy, preceding the nodules seen here?","o":["Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules","Fibrin caps","Diffuse mesangial matrix expansion with GBM thickening","Capsular drops"],"a":2,"ex":"Diffuse mesangial matrix expansion with glomerular basement membrane thickening is the earliest change in diabetic nephropathy, correlating with microalbuminuria. Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (the patient's biopsy) are pathognomonic but represent advanced disease. Fibrin caps (insudative hyaline in capillary loops) and capsular drops (Bowman capsule) are exudative lesions of late disease. Hook: 'Diffuse first, nodular later, exudative last.'","tp":"Diabetic nephropathy","s":"Pathology","qid":"p39","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p40","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Which of the following acts by the mechanism illustrated in the given image?","o":["Vibrio cholerae","Streptococcus pyogenes","Clostridium difficile","Clostridium botulinum"],"a":1,"ex":"The image illustrates the mechanism of action of a superantigen. Unlike conventional antigens that are processed and presented in the peptide-binding groove of an MHC molecule, a superantigen binds externally to the MHC Class II molecule on an antigen-presenting cell and to the Vβ region of the T-cell receptor on a T-cell. This cross-linking bypasses the need for specific antigen recognition and leads to the massive, non-specific activation of a large fraction (up to 20%) of the T-cell population. This results in a \"cytokine storm\" and can cause systemic inflammation and shock. Streptococcus pyogenes produces several superantigens, such as the streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxins (e.g., SpeA, SpeC), which are responsible for the clinical manifestations of scarlet fever and streptococcal toxic shock syndrome. Cholera toxin, botulinum toxin, and C. difficile toxins have different mechanisms of action (e.g., ADP-ribosylation, SNARE protein cleavage).","tp":"Respiratory Physiology","s":"Ophthalmology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 28-year-old man develops painless gross hematuria within 24 hours of a sore throat; he has no edema or hypertension, BP 130/80 mmHg, serum C3 normal. Urinalysis shows dysmorphic RBCs, RBC casts and protein 0.5 g/day. Renal biopsy shows mesangial proliferation with granular IgA deposits in the mesangium on immunofluorescence. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","IgA nephropathy","Membranous nephropathy","Thin basement membrane disease"],"a":1,"ex":"IgA nephropathy (Berger disease) classically causes synpharyngitic hematuria — bleeding concurrent with an upper respiratory infection — with mesangial IgA deposits and normal complement. Post-streptococcal GN occurs 1-3 weeks after infection, has low C3 and edema/hypertension. Membranous nephropathy is nephrotic, and thin basement membrane disease causes persistent microscopic hematuria without RBC casts or IgA deposits. Hook: 'Synpharyngitic (same time) = IgA; post-pharyngitic (1-3 weeks) = PSGN.'","tp":"IgA nephropathy","s":"Pathology","qid":"p41","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has fever, confusion, and petechiae for 2 days. Hb 7.0 g/dL, platelets 18,000/μL, creatinine 2.2 mg/dL, LDH 1500 U/L, and peripheral smear shows schistocytes. ADAMTS13 activity is 4% with a high-titer inhibitor. What is the most appropriate initial therapy?","o":["Platelet transfusion","Splenectomy","Therapeutic plasma exchange with plasma replacement","High-dose corticosteroids alone"],"a":2,"ex":"Severe ADAMTS13 deficiency with inhibitor confirms immune TTP; urgent therapeutic plasma exchange (TPE) replaces the enzyme and removes antibody — it cut mortality from ~90% to <20% and must not be delayed. Platelet transfusion is discouraged (worsens thrombosis, and ADAMTS13 <10% excludes it in modern scoring). Splenectomy and steroids are later/adjunctive options. Hook: 'TTP triad of fever + neuro + renal with schistocytes = pheresis, never platelets.'","tp":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","s":"Pathology","qid":"p42","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has pallor, jaundice and splenomegaly; she had neonatal jaundice and now pigment gallstones. Hb 9.0 g/dL, MCV 88 fL, MCHC 38 g/dL, reticulocyte count 8%. Peripheral smear shows spherocytes, Coombs test negative. Which test would best confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Direct Coombs test","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Bone marrow biopsy","Osmotic fragility test"],"a":3,"ex":"Hereditary spherocytosis (membrane defect, usually spectrin/ankyrin) is confirmed by increased osmotic fragility: spherocytes with reduced surface-to-volume ratio lyse in less dilute saline. The elevated MCHC (>36 g/dL) with spherocytes and a negative Coombs test is a strong clue separating it from autoimmune hemolysis. Hb electrophoresis would detect thalassemia/sickle disease, and marrow biopsy is not diagnostic. Splenectomy is curative in severe cases. Hook: 'MCHC high + spherocytes + Coombs negative = spectrin problem, not antibody problem.'","tp":"Hereditary spherocytosis","s":"Pathology","qid":"p43","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea, weight loss and iron-deficiency anemia for 8 months. IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase antibody is 10 times the upper limit of normal. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Metronidazole","Lifelong gluten-free diet","Mesalamine","Azathioprine"],"a":1,"ex":"The biopsy (Marsh 3c: villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytosis) with high-titer IgA anti-tTG confirms celiac disease; a lifelong gluten-free diet produces clinical and histologic remission and reduces lymphoma risk. Metronidazole and mesalamine treat giardiasis and inflammatory bowel disease respectively — neither causes this serologic/biopsy pattern. Azathioprine is reserved for refractory celiac. Hook: 'Celiac triad — tTG, Marsh 3, villous atrophy — treated with the fork, not the pharmacy.'","tp":"Celiac disease","s":"Pathology","qid":"p44","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 65-year-old man, chronic smoker, has a 2-month history of cough, weight loss and confusion. Serum sodium 118 mEq/L, urine osmolality 420 mOsm/kg, plasma osmolality 255 mOsm/kg. CT chest shows a central 6 cm lung mass with bulky mediastinal nodes; transbronchial biopsy shows sheets of small cells with scant cytoplasm, nuclear molding and crush artifact. Which paraneoplastic syndrome is most characteristic of this tumor?","o":["Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH)","Hypercalcemia","Polycythemia","Acromegaly"],"a":0,"ex":"Small cell lung carcinoma (SCLC) is a neuroendocrine tumor that most classically causes SIADH (as here — hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine) and ectopic ACTH (Cushing); Lambert-Eaton syndrome is another association. Hypercalcemia from PTHrP is the signature paraneoplastic syndrome of squamous cell carcinoma. Polycythemia (erythropoietin) marks renal cell carcinoma and hepatocellular carcinoma; acromegaly (GHRH) can occur in carcinoid. Hook: 'Small cell = small sodium; squamous = big calcium.'","tp":"Small cell lung carcinoma / paraneoplastic","s":"Pathology","qid":"p45","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has persistent epigastric discomfort; stool antigen test is positive for Helicobacter pylori. Upper GI endoscopy shows antral erythema; biopsy of the gastric body shows a dense diffuse infiltrate of small CD20+ B cells forming lymphoepithelial lesions within glandular epithelium. What is the most appropriate initial management?","o":["R-CHOP chemotherapy","H. pylori eradication therapy","Total gastrectomy","Radiotherapy"],"a":1,"ex":"This is gastric extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma, which arises from H. pylori-driven chronic gastritis; eradication therapy alone achieves remission in 70-80% of localized cases and is the standard first-line treatment. R-CHOP is reserved for H. pylori-negative, advanced or t(11;18)-positive MALT that fails eradication. Surgery has been replaced by organ-preserving approaches. Hook: 'Gastric MALT: treat the germ first — antibiotics, not chemotherapy, unless t(11;18) fails to respond.'","tp":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","s":"Pathology","qid":"p46","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy and NYHA class II heart failure has an LVEF of 30% on echocardiography. He is optimally treated with sacubitril/valsartan 100 mg twice daily, bisoprolol 5 mg, and spironolactone 25 mg. His eGFR is 58 mL/min/1.73 m² and HbA1c is 6.2% (he is not diabetic). According to current heart failure guidelines, which drug should be added next?","o":["Empagliflozin 10 mg once daily","Verapamil 240 mg once daily","Pioglitazone 15 mg once daily","Digoxin 0.25 mg once daily"],"a":0,"ex":"Empagliflozin is correct. The 2022 AHA/ACC and 2023 ESC HF guidelines mandate an SGLT2 inhibitor as the fourth 'pillar' of HFrEF therapy (with ARNI/ACEi, beta-blocker and MRA) with a class I recommendation regardless of diabetes status — benefit on CV death and HF hospitalisation was shown in the EMPEROR-Reduced and DAPA-HF trials, which enrolled predominantly non-diabetic patients. Verapamil is a negative inotrope and contraindicated in HFrEF; pioglitazone causes fluid retention and can precipitate/exacerbate heart failure; digoxin improves symptoms and reduces HF hospitalisations but has no mortality benefit and is not the next guideline-directed add-on. Hook: '4 pillars of HFrEF — ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i (even if not diabetic).'","tp":"Heart failure pharmacotherapy","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p47","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 64-year-old man started unfractionated heparin 7 days ago for a proximal deep vein thrombosis. Today his platelet count has fallen from 220,000 to 40,000/µL and he develops a new contralateral DVT. Heparin-PF4 antibodies are positive. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Start argatroban infusion and stop all heparin","Switch to subcutaneous enoxaparin 1 mg/kg twice daily","Start warfarin 5 mg daily with a heparin overlap","Continue unfractionated heparin but reduce the dose by 50%"],"a":0,"ex":"Argatroban is correct. This is heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) type II, an immune-mediated reaction in which anti-PF4-heparin antibodies cause platelet activation and paradoxical thrombosis — the diagnostic 4Ts here (thrombocytopenia ~day 7, thrombosis, no alternative cause) are high. All heparin (including LMWH) must be stopped and an alternative non-heparin anticoagulant started; argatroban is a direct thrombin inhibitor and the drug of choice. LMWH cross-reacts with heparin antibodies in vitro and is contraindicated; warfarin alone is dangerous because it can cause venous limb gangrene/skin necrosis if started before platelet recovery (start only after platelets exceed 150,000/µL, with overlap); continuing heparin is fatal in HIT. Hook: 'HIT — stop heparin, give a direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban); warfarin only after platelet recovery.'","tp":"Anticoagulant adverse effects","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p48","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with non-valvular atrial fibrillation (CHA2DS2-VASc score 5) has a creatinine clearance of 25 mL/min (Cockcroft-Gault), serum creatinine 1.8 mg/dL and weighs 58 kg. Which oral anticoagulant regimen is most appropriate?","o":["Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily","Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily","Rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily","Edoxaban 60 mg once daily"],"a":1,"ex":"Apixaban is correct — it is the DOAC of choice in chronic kidney disease (approved down to CrCl 15 mL/min) and the dose is reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when at least two of three criteria are met: age ≥80 years, weight ≤60 kg, or serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL (this patient meets two). Dabigatran is contraindicated when CrCl <30 mL/min because of accumulation and bleeding risk; rivaroxaban 20 mg requires CrCl >50 mL/min (dose would be 15 mg for CrCl 15–50); edoxaban 60 mg is only for CrCl >50 mL/min (30 mg for 15–50). Hook: 'Advanced CKD + AF = apixaban; remember the apixaban 2.5 mg dose-reduction rule: age ≥80, weight ≤60 kg, creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL — any two.'","tp":"Anticoagulants in CKD","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p49","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p50","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Comment on the diagnosis from the ECG shown below.","o":["First degree heart block","Second-degree infra-nodal heart block","Complete heart block","AV dissociation"],"a":1,"ex":"On the ECG strip, there are regular P waves occurring at a constant rate, but not every P wave is followed by a QRS complex. Some atrial impulses fail to conduct to the ventricles, resulting in dropped QRS complexes. The PR intervals of the conducted beats appear constant rather than progressively lengthening. This pattern is characteristic of Mobitz type II second-degree AV block, which usually occurs below the AV node, in the His-Purkinje system (infra-nodal level). In contrast, first-degree heart block shows prolonged PR interval but no dropped beats. Complete heart block demonstrates total AV dissociation, where atrial and ventricular rhythms are independent. AV dissociation can occur in various settings and does not necessarily imply complete block. Mobitz type II is clinically significant because it can progress to complete heart block and often requires pacemaker insertion. The presence of sudden non-conducted P waves with constant PR intervals in conducted beats supports the diagnosis of second-degree infra-nodal (Mobitz type II) heart block.","tp":"Occupational Lung Diseases","s":"Ophthalmology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with acute pyelonephritis has a history of anaphylaxis (angioedema and shock) to amoxicillin. Urine culture grows Escherichia coli resistant to ampicillin. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","o":["Cefuroxime 750 mg IV thrice daily","Imipenem-cilastatin 500 mg IV six-hourly","Aztreonam 1 g IV eight-hourly","Ampicillin-sulbactam 1.5 g IV six-hourly"],"a":2,"ex":"Aztreonam is correct. It is a monobactam with no cross-reactivity with penicillins (its side chain is identical only to ceftazidime, not to penicillin/cephalosporins), making it a safe alternative in a patient with true penicillin anaphylaxis needing Gram-negative coverage. Cephalosporins such as cefuroxime carry about a 2% cross-reaction risk and are contraindicated after penicillin anaphylaxis; carbapenems also cross-react (about 1% risk) and are likewise avoided after anaphylaxis; ampicillin-sulbactam is both allergenic and the isolate is resistant. Hook: 'Penicillin anaphylaxis + Gram-negative cover = aztreonam; cross-reacts only with ceftazidime.'","tp":"Beta-lactam cross-reactivity","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p51","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infection is diagnosed with acute pyelonephritis. Urine culture grows extended-spectrum beta-lactamase (ESBL)-producing Escherichia coli, resistant to ceftriaxone and ciprofloxacin. She is febrile with flank tenderness. Which antibiotic is the most appropriate choice?","o":["Nitrofurantoin 100 mg orally four times daily","Cefixime 200 mg orally twice daily","Ertapenem 1 g IV once daily","Fosfomycin trometamol 3 g single oral dose"],"a":2,"ex":"Ertapenem is correct. ESBL-producing Enterobacterales hydrolyse penicillins and cephalosporins; carbapenems are the treatment of choice for invasive infections such as pyelonephritis, with ertapenem covering this E. coli as a first-line agent. Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin do not achieve adequate tissue/blood levels and are restricted to uncomplicated cystitis, not pyelonephritis; cefixime would be hydrolysed by the ESBL enzyme and therapy would fail. Hook: 'ESBL = carbapenem; nitrofurantoin/fosfomycin are bladder-only drugs.'","tp":"Carbapenems / ESBL infections","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p52","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with stage IV non-small cell lung cancer has been on pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1) for 4 months. He presents with 2 weeks of dry cough and exertional dyspnoea; SpO2 is 93% on room air and CT chest shows bilateral ground-glass opacities. Bronchoalveolar lavage is negative for infection. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Withhold pembrolizumab and start oral prednisone 1 mg/kg/day","Continue pembrolizumab and add inhaled bronchodilators","Permanently discontinue pembrolizumab and give methylprednisolone 1 g IV pulse","Continue pembrolizumab and add azithromycin empirically"],"a":0,"ex":"Withholding pembrolizumab and starting prednisone 1 mg/kg/day is correct — this is grade 2 immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) pneumonitis (symptomatic, limited to one lobe or <50% of lung, no hypoxia requiring support), and ASCO/ESMO guidelines recommend withholding the ICI plus corticosteroids; resumption can be considered after resolution on a steroid taper. Permanent discontinuation with pulse methylprednisolone (1–2 mg/kg IV, escalating to infliximab if refractory) is reserved for grade 3–4 pneumonitis; continuing the ICI, adding bronchodilators, or empirical antibiotics (infection was excluded on BAL) are all incorrect. Hook: 'ICI pneumonitis — grade 2: hold + prednisone 1 mg/kg; grade 3–4: permanent stop + high-dose steroids.'","tp":"Immune checkpoint inhibitor toxicity","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p53","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus for 8 years (HbA1c 7.8%) is on metformin 1000 mg twice daily. Her eGFR has fallen from 60 to 35 mL/min/1.73 m² (serum creatinine 1.9 mg/dL) over the past year. Per current ADA guidelines, what is the appropriate adjustment?","o":["Continue metformin 1000 mg twice daily with 6-monthly monitoring","Reduce metformin dose to a maximum of 1000 mg/day","Stop metformin and start basal insulin immediately","Switch to glyburide 5 mg daily"],"a":1,"ex":"Reducing metformin to a maximum of 1000 mg/day is correct. ADA Standards of Care: at eGFR 30–45 mL/min/1.73 m² metformin may be continued but the starting dose is limited and the maximum dose reduced to 1000 mg/day (it should not be newly initiated at this eGFR); below eGFR 30 it is contraindicated. Continuing 1000 mg BD exceeds the reduced ceiling and increases lactic acidosis risk; insulin is not required yet (reserved for eGFR <30 where metformin is stopped); glyburide is an inappropriate choice in renal impairment because of prolonged action and severe hypoglycaemia risk. Hook: 'Metformin and eGFR: >45 normal, 30–45 reduce to 1000 mg/day, <30 stop.'","tp":"Metformin in CKD","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p54","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 5 years (HbA1c 8.1% on metformin 2000 mg/day) has a BMI of 33 kg/m² and suffered a myocardial infarction 2 years ago (eGFR 75 mL/min/1.73 m²). According to current ADA/ESC guidance, which add-on agent is preferred for this patient?","o":["Pioglitazone 15 mg daily","Glimepiride 2 mg daily","Semaglutide 1 mg subcutaneous weekly","Sitagliptin 100 mg daily"],"a":2,"ex":"Semaglutide is correct. In patients with established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) and obesity, guidelines preferentially recommend a GLP-1 receptor agonist with proven cardiovascular benefit (SUSTAIN-6 showed reduced MACE with semaglutide); it also provides substantial weight loss, which is particularly desirable at this BMI. Pioglitazone increases heart failure risk and weight; glimepiride causes weight gain and hypoglycaemia with no CV benefit; sitagliptin is cardiovascular-neutral and weight-neutral, so it would not be the preferred first add-on in this high-risk, obese patient. Hook: 'ASCVD + obesity + diabetes → GLP-1 RA (semaglutide); SGLT2i also good but weight-loss edge goes to GLP-1.'","tp":"GLP-1 receptor agonists","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p55","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with Parkinson disease on levodopa-carbidopa develops persistent nausea with bloating and early satiety suggestive of gastroparesis. Which antiemetic/prokinetic is most appropriate in this patient?","o":["Metoclopramide 10 mg thrice daily","Prochlorperazine 5 mg thrice daily","Haloperidol 2 mg at bedtime","Domperidone 10 mg thrice daily"],"a":3,"ex":"Domperidone is correct. It is a peripheral D2-receptor antagonist that does not cross the blood-brain barrier in significant amounts, so it does not worsen Parkinsonism. Metoclopramide, prochlorperazine and haloperidol all cross the blood-brain barrier and block central dopamine receptors, which would worsen parkinsonian symptoms (metoclopramide also carries an FDA black-box warning for tardive dyskinesia, ; UK MHRA advises a 5-day maximum, while the FDA black-box warning caps continuous use at 12 weeks). Hook: 'Parkinson + nausea = domperidone (peripheral D2 blocker); metoclopramide is central D2 — contraindicated.'","tp":"Prokinetics and antiemetics","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p56","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis on methotrexate 15 mg once weekly mistakenly took the drug daily for 2 weeks. She now has severe oral mucositis, fever, Hb 7 g/dL, total leucocyte count 800/µL and platelets 40,000/µL. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Leucovorin (folinic acid) 15 mg IV six-hourly","Folic acid 5 mg orally once daily","Supportive care with blood transfusions alone","Stop methotrexate and observe for 2 weeks"],"a":0,"ex":"Leucovorin is correct. This is life-threatening methotrexate toxicity (pancytopenia with mucositis from daily instead of weekly dosing); leucovorin (folinic acid) is the reduced, active form of folate that bypasses the DHFR blockade caused by methotrexate and rescues normal rapidly dividing cells, and it must be given promptly in high doses. Folic acid is ineffective because it requires DHFR to be reduced — the very enzyme methotrexate inhibits; transfusions are supportive but not definitive; observation alone risks fatal aplasia and sepsis. Hook: 'MTX toxicity → leucovorin (reduced folate), never folic acid — it cannot bypass the DHFR block.'","tp":"Methotrexate toxicity","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p57","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman undergoing wrist surgery under an infraclavicular brachial plexus block with 40 mL of 0.5% bupivacaine develops generalised seizures 10 minutes after the block, followed by ventricular tachycardia and hypotension (BP 70/40 mm Hg). What is the most important immediate treatment?","o":["Intravenous amiodarone 300 mg bolus","Intravenous sodium bicarbonate 1 mEq/kg","Intravenous 20% lipid emulsion (1.5 mL/kg bolus followed by infusion)","Propofol infusion titrated for seizure control"],"a":2,"ex":"Intravenous 20% lipid emulsion is correct — this is local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), and lipid emulsion is the specific antidote: the bolus 1.5 mL/kg with a 0.25 mL/kg/min infusion creates a 'lipid sink' that binds bupivacaine and reverses both neurotoxicity and cardiotoxicity. Amiodarone is not recommended (it can worsen LA-induced arrhythmias, and vasopressin is specifically harmful); sodium bicarbonate is the treatment for tricyclic antidepressant toxicity, not LAST; propofol only suppresses seizures transiently and does not reverse cardiotoxicity. Hook: 'LA toxicity → Lipid Emulsion — the three Ls; bupivacaine is the most cardiotoxic local anaesthetic.'","tp":"Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p58","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 42-year-old man started atorvastatin 40 mg daily 6 weeks ago for dyslipidaemia. He now presents with severe myalgia and dark-coloured urine; serum CK is 12,000 U/L and urinalysis shows myoglobinuria. After stopping the statin and starting IV fluids, which of the following actions is most appropriate under India's pharmacovigilance framework?","o":["Report the suspected ADR only to the hospital ethics committee","No reporting is required because statin-induced rhabdomyolysis is already well documented","Report the suspected ADR only to the drug manufacturer","Report the serious adverse drug reaction to PvPI through the nearest ADR Monitoring Centre"],"a":3,"ex":"Reporting to PvPI (Pharmacovigilance Programme of India) through the nearest ADR Monitoring Centre is correct — CDSCO's PvPI, coordinated by the National Coordination Centre at the Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission, Ghaziabad, relies on spontaneous reporting by healthcare professionals, and serious ADRs such as rhabdomyolysis must be reported irrespective of how well known the reaction is. Causality assessment is done by PvPI, not the reporting clinician; reporting to the manufacturer, ethics committee, or withholding reporting because an ADR is 'well documented' does not satisfy the national pharmacovigilance requirement and contributes to the widespread problem of under-reporting. Hook: 'Any suspected serious ADR → report to PvPI/nearest AMC — never assume it is not worth reporting.'","tp":"Pharmacovigilance","s":"Pharmacology","qid":"p59","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p60","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Identify the condition in the given image.","o":["Actinomyces israelii","Blastomyces dermatitidis","Clostridium perfringens","Eumycetoma"],"a":3,"ex":"The clinical photograph displays a chronically infected, grossly swollen and deformed foot, a condition known as mycetoma or Madura foot. The image shows the characteristic clinical triad of mycetoma : massive tumefaction (swelling), the formation of multiple nodules and abscesses, and the presence of numerous draining sinus tracts on the skin surface. These sinuses typically discharge purulent fluid containing \"grains,\" which are macroscopic colonies of the causative organism. Mycetoma is classified based on its etiology. When caused by true fungi, it is called eumycetoma. When caused by filamentous bacteria (actinomycetes), it is called actinomycetoma. Actinomyces israelii (A) causes actinomycosis, which typically affects the cervicofacial region. Blastomyces (B) and Clostridium (C) cause different types of infections. The presentation is classic for mycetoma, and eumycetoma is a specific type of this condition.","tp":"Smoking Cessation","s":"Surgery","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman developed watery diarrhoea (8 stools/day) one week after finishing a 10-day course of amoxicillin-clavulanate for pneumonia. Stool is positive for glutamate dehydrogenase and toxins A and B. She has no prior CDI episodes, is haemodynamically stable, and has no ileus. Per current IDSA guidelines, which is the preferred treatment for this first episode?","o":["Metronidazole 500 mg thrice daily for 10 days","Fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily for 10 days","Vancomycin 125 mg four times daily for 14 days","Oral budesonide with metronidazole for 21 days"],"a":1,"ex":"This is a first episode of Clostridioides difficile infection after antibiotic exposure. The 2021 IDSA/SHEA update states that for an initial episode, fidaxomicin is preferred over vancomycin (both are acceptable; fidaxomicin reduces recurrence). Metronidazole is no longer preferred for adults, even in non-severe disease, and is only used when neither vancomycin nor fidaxomicin is available. Vancomycin remains acceptable but is no longer the preferred choice. Budesonide has no role in CDI. Memory hook: '2021 update: fidaxomicin first — fewer relapses, preferred over vancomycin; metronidazole is out.'","tp":"Clostridioides difficile infection","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p61","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 24-year-old man presents with 3 weeks of cough, night sweats and weight loss. Chest X-ray shows a right upper lobe cavity. Sputum CBNAAT reports 'Mycobacterium tuberculosis detected, rifampicin resistant' and culture confirms MDR-TB (resistant to rifampicin and isoniazid, susceptible to fluoroquinolones). He has never been treated for TB. As per current WHO and NTEP recommendations, which regimen should be started?","o":["Standard 2HRZE/4HR (6-month first-line regimen)","BPaLM: bedaquiline + pretomanid + linezolid + moxifloxacin for 6 months","Kanamycin-based 8-month injectable regimen","24-month regimen with pyrazinamide, ethambutol and second-line injectables"],"a":1,"ex":"For rifampicin-resistant/MDR-TB, WHO (2022) recommends the 6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) as the preferred option; NTEP India has adopted it, replacing injectable and longer regimens. Standard first-line HRZE fails because the isolate is already R-resistant. Injectable kanamycin regimens are obsolete (ototoxicity/renal toxicity). BPaLM is preferred over the longer 18-24 month regimen. Memory hook: 'BPaLM = 6 months all-oral MDR-TB; injectables are dead, HRZE is no help when R is resistant.'","tp":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (MDR-TB)","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p62","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents on day 3 of fever with retro-orbital pain, severe myalgia and a flush-like rash. Her platelet count is 120,000/µL and haematocrit 42%. Serum NS1 antigen is positive while dengue IgM is negative. Which statement regarding this patient is correct?","o":["A negative dengue IgM rules out dengue infection, and the fever is likely viral fever","NS1 positivity confirms dengue; she can be managed as outpatient with paracetamol, oral fluids and monitoring for warning signs","She needs immediate hospital admission and prophylactic platelet transfusion because NS1 is positive","Dengue is excluded because IgM appears from day 2 onwards and its absence at day 3 is incompatible"],"a":1,"ex":"NS1 antigen is detectable from day 1 to day 5-7 of illness, while IgM appears only from day 4-5, so an NS1-positive/IgM-negative result at day 3 is the classic early dengue pattern and confirms infection. With no warning signs (abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, mucosal bleed, lethargy, hepatomegaly, rising HCT with falling platelets), WHO 2009/2011 criteria allow outpatient management with paracetamol, oral fluids and close monitoring. Prophylactic platelet transfusion is not indicated in dengue without significant bleeding. Memory hook: 'NS1 = early antigen (day 1-5), IgM = late (day 4+); NS1+ rules dengue IN, not out.'","tp":"Dengue","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p63","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 20-year-old man from Chhattisgarh presents with fever every alternate day, chills and anaemia. Peripheral smear shows ring forms and gametocytes of Plasmodium vivax. G6PD assay confirms deficiency (activity 0.8 U/g Hb). Which regimen provides radical cure with the least haemolytic risk?","o":["Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days plus primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily for 14 days","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days plus single-dose tafenoquine 300 mg","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days plus primaquine 0.75 mg/kg once weekly for 8 weeks","Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days alone, without any hypnozoitocidal drug"],"a":2,"ex":"Radical cure of P. vivax needs a hypnozoitocidal drug to prevent relapse. In G6PD deficiency, daily primaquine risks severe haemolysis; WHO (2023) recommends supervised primaquine 0.75 mg/kg once weekly for 8 weeks, which is better tolerated. The standard daily 0.25 mg/kg × 14 days is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency. Tafenoquine is also contraindicated in G6PD deficiency and in pregnancy. Omitting radical cure leaves a >30% relapse risk. Memory hook: 'G6PD deficient: weekly primaquine 8 weeks; G6PD normal: daily 14 days.'","tp":"Malaria","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p64","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old man in the ICU develops ventilator-associated pneumonia 8 days after abdominal surgery. Sputum culture grows Klebsiella pneumoniae resistant to meropenem (MIC >32 µg/mL); combination-disk EDTA synergy and mCIM confirm NDM-type metallo-beta-lactamase. Which antimicrobial strategy is most appropriate?","o":["Ceftazidime-avibactam alone at standard doses","Ceftazidime-avibactam plus intravenous aztreonam","Meropenem-vaborbactam 4 g IV 8-hourly","Doubling the meropenem dose with prolonged infusion"],"a":1,"ex":"NDM is a metallo-beta-lactamase that hydrolyses ceftazidime-avibactam and meropenem-vaborbactam (both active only against serine carbapenemases like KPC). Aztreonam resists MBLs but is degraded by co-produced ESBLs/AmpC, so combining aztreonam with ceftazidime-avibactam (avibactam protects aztreonam from the serine enzymes) is the reference therapy for NDM Enterobacterales. Meropenem-vaborbactam covers KPC, not MBL, and high-dose meropenem fails against NDM. Memory hook: 'NDM = metallo → needs aztreonam; CTZ-avi + aztreonam = the NDM combo.'","tp":"Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p65","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old man in the ICU with a central venous catheter develops fever 6 days after admission. Blood cultures grow a germ-tube-negative yeast, identified by MALDI-TOF as Candida auris. He is haemodynamically stable. Which is the most appropriate management?","o":["Remove the catheter and start an echinocandin (e.g. micafungin), with strict contact precautions","Start oral fluconazole 800 mg daily after catheter removal","Start amphotericin B deoxycholate and discontinue contact precautions after 48 hours","Repeat blood cultures without antifungal therapy, as C. auris is a coloniser"],"a":0,"ex":"Candida auris is a multidrug-resistant emerging pathogen: most isolates are resistant to fluconazole, and amphotericin B resistance is frequent, so echinocandins are first-line. Catheter removal is essential (catheter-associated candidemia), and strict contact precautions are mandatory because C. auris persists on environmental surfaces and causes outbreaks. Fluconazole monotherapy and amphotericin B deoxycholate are unreliable; treating as coloniser risks candidaemia progression. Memory hook: 'C. auris: echinocandin + line out + contact isolation; fluconazole is usually useless.'","tp":"Candida auris","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p66","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with epigastric pain undergoes endoscopy showing antral erythema; rapid urease test is positive. He was treated with clarithromycin-based triple therapy 2 years ago. The local clarithromycin resistance rate is known to be >20%. Per Maastricht VI/2022 recommendations, which regimen should be prescribed?","o":["Repeat the same clarithromycin triple therapy for 14 days","Bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole) for 14 days","Levofloxacin-based triple therapy for 7 days","PPI plus amoxicillin dual therapy for 5 days"],"a":1,"ex":"When clarithromycin resistance exceeds 15%, clarithromycin-based regimens should not be used, and bismuth quadruple therapy (PPI + bismuth + tetracycline + metronidazole, 14 days) is the recommended first-line option per Maastricht VI/2022 and Indian consensus. Repeating clarithromycin triple after prior failure has low efficacy. Levofloxacin triple is a second-line rescue choice, not first-line, and 7-day duration is inadequate. High-dose dual therapy is still experimental in this setting. Memory hook: 'Resistance >15% → bismuth quadruple; clarithromycin triple is dead in high-resistance areas.'","tp":"Helicobacter pylori","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p67","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 16 weeks of gestation presents for routine antenatal check-up. She is asymptomatic. Urine culture grows Escherichia coli at 10^5 CFU/mL; the isolate is sensitive to cephalexin and nitrofurantoin. Which is the correct management?","o":["No treatment, as the patient is asymptomatic","Oral cephalexin for 7 days with a test-of-cure culture","Oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days","Cotrimoxazole 960 mg twice daily for 3 days"],"a":1,"ex":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy must be screened and treated because it increases the risk of pyelonephritis, preterm labour and low birth weight (ACOG/IDSA). Beta-lactams such as cephalexin are the preferred safe option; nitrofurantoin is also acceptable but avoided near term and in G6PD deficiency. Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin) are contraindicated in pregnancy (cartilage toxicity), and cotrimoxazole is avoided in the first and third trimesters (teratogenicity, kernicterus risk). A test-of-cure culture is recommended after therapy. Memory hook: 'ASB in pregnancy: treat with cephalexin — never ciprofloxacin, never ignore.'","tp":"Urinary tract infection in pregnancy","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p68","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman at 10 weeks of gestation presents with a 2-week history of painless posterior cervical lymphadenopathy and mild fever. Serology shows positive Toxoplasma IgM and IgG with low IgG avidity. Which is the most appropriate management at this stage of pregnancy?","o":["No treatment is needed since the infection is mild","Start pyrimethamine plus sulfadiazine with folinic acid","Start spiramycin 1 g thrice daily to reduce vertical transmission","Advise immediate medical termination of pregnancy"],"a":2,"ex":"Low IgG avidity with positive IgM indicates acute primary toxoplasmosis acquired in pregnancy. Before fetal infection is documented, spiramycin is given to reduce transplacental transmission; it does not cross the placenta well, hence is safe in the first trimester. Pyrimethamine-sulfadiazine is used only after fetal infection is confirmed (amniotic fluid PCR) and is teratogenic in early pregnancy. Termination is not automatic — spiramycin with serial amniocentesis for fetal PCR is standard. Memory hook: 'First trimester toxo: spiramycin first (prevents transmission), pyrimethamine-sulfa only if fetus infected.'","tp":"Toxoplasmosis in pregnancy","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p69","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p70","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"In the provided image, which cell is being depicted?","o":["Gaucher cell","Sickle cell","Target cell","Hairy cell"],"a":3,"ex":"The image provided is a peripheral blood smear showing a lymphocyte with distinct morphological features. The cell has a round to indented nucleus, moderately abundant pale-blue cytoplasm, and, most characteristically, fine, irregular, hair-like cytoplasmic projections that extend around its entire circumference. This unique appearance is the pathognomonic feature of a hairy cell, the neoplastic cell found in Hairy Cell Leukemia (HCL). HCL is a rare, indolent B-cell lymphoproliferative disorder. The presence of these cells on a peripheral smear, along with pancytopenia and splenomegaly, is highly suggestive of the diagnosis, which is then typically confirmed by flow cytometry and bone marrow biopsy showing a characteristic \"fried-egg\" appearance. Gaucher cells (A) are lipid-laden macrophages found in the bone marrow with a \"wrinkled tissue paper\" appearance. Sickle cells (B) and target cells (C) are abnormally shaped red blood cells.","tp":"Clostridioides difficile Infection","s":"Pathology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 19-year-old student develops nausea and projectile vomiting 2.5 hours after eating reheated fried rice from a hostel canteen. He has no fever or diarrhoea. Which foodborne illness best explains this presentation?","o":["Clostridium perfringens type A enterotoxin disease","Salmonella enterica gastroenteritis","Bacillus cereus emetic-type illness due to cereulide toxin","Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxin B from contaminated rice"],"a":2,"ex":"The short incubation (1-6 h), vomiting-predominant picture and the classic vehicle of reheated fried rice point to Bacillus cereus emetic-type food poisoning, caused by preformed heat-stable cereulide toxin (survives reheating). C. perfringens causes watery diarrhoea 6-24 h after meat dishes (colonic enterotoxin). Salmonella has a 12-72 h incubation with fever and invasive diarrhoea. S. aureus enterotoxin also causes rapid vomiting but from dairy/pastry handled-food, not typically reheated rice, and its toxin is heat-stable (preformed, survives reheating). Memory hook: 'Reheated rice + 2-hour vomiting = B. cereus cereulide; meat + 8-hour diarrhoea = C. perfringens.'","tp":"Food poisoning (Bacillus cereus)","s":"Microbiology","qid":"p71","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old man is found dead in a locked room at 8 AM. At examination at 9 AM, his rectal temperature is 35.1°C (ambient room temperature 25°C); rigor mortis is present in the jaw, facial muscles and neck, but not in the limbs; postmortem lividity on the back blanches on firm finger pressure. Based on these findings, what is the most likely time since death?","o":["Less than 30 minutes","About 2–4 hours","About 8–12 hours","More than 24 hours"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: B. In the first 12 hours the body cools at approximately 0.8°C/hour in a moderate ambient temperature, so a drop from 37°C to 35.1°C indicates roughly 2.5 hours since death. Supporting evidence: rigor mortis begins in the eyelids/jaw within 1–2 hours and spreads to limbs over the next 3–6 hours, and lividity that still blanches on pressure is early (within ~6 hours of death). At 8–12 hours rigor would be fully developed in all limbs and lividity fixed; beyond 24 hours rigor would have disappeared with onset of decomposition. Memory hook: algor mortis 1.5°F (~0.8°C) per hour; rigor jaw-first, 'face at 1–2 hours, limbs by 6–12 hours, gone by 24–48 hours'.","tp":"Postmortem changes","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p72","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman, married 3 years ago, dies in her matrimonial home after sustaining 90% antemortem flame burns. Her mother alleges she was harassed for dowry. At autopsy the death is certified as burn injuries. Under the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023, the FIR for dowry death should be registered under which section, which also attracts the statutory presumption of dowry death?","o":["Section 103 BNS (punishment for murder)","Section 80 BNS (dowry death)","Section 105 BNS (culpable homicide not amounting to murder)","Section 108 BNS (abetment of suicide)"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: B. Section 80 BNS (old Section 304B IPC) defines dowry death: death by burns or other bodily injury within 7 years of marriage, if it is shown that she was subjected to cruelty/harassment for dowry shortly before death; the presumption then shifts the burden onto the husband/relatives. Because the death occurred within 7 years of marriage with documented dowry harassment, this special provision takes precedence over generic murder under Section 103 BNS, which would additionally require proof of intention to kill. Sections 105 (culpable homicide) and 108 (abetment of suicide) are inapplicable on these facts. Memory hook: 'Dowry death = 80 BNS (304B IPC) — 7 years, cruelty, presumption; murder 103 (302), culpable homicide 105 (304), abetment of suicide 108 (306)'; all criminal laws were replaced on 1 July 2024.","tp":"New criminal laws (BNS 2023)","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p73","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old man consumed locally brewed illicit liquor at a party. About 12 hours later he presents with headache, giddiness, abdominal pain and blurred vision. On examination, both pupils are dilated and sluggishly reacting; fundoscopy shows hyperemic optic discs. ABG shows pH 7.12 with high anion gap metabolic acidosis. What is the most appropriate antidote to administer?","o":["Naloxone 0.4 mg IV","Fomepizole IV","Atropine 2 mg IV","Sodium thiosulfate IV"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: B. The picture of visual disturbance with bilateral optic nerve involvement, abdominal pain and high anion gap metabolic acidosis after illicit liquor is classic methanol poisoning — methanol is metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase to formic acid, which is both the cause of the acidosis and the optic nerve toxin. Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole) is the first-line inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase; IV ethanol is the alternative where fomepizole is unavailable, along with sodium bicarbonate for severe acidosis and folate to enhance formate metabolism. Naloxone is the antidote for opioids, atropine for organophosphate poisoning, and sodium thiosulfate for cyanide poisoning — none fit these findings. Memory hook: 'Blind drunk — methanol → formic acid → optic atrophy + high anion gap acidosis; fomepizole blocks ADH before the toxin forms'.","tp":"Toxicology: methanol poisoning","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p74","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman is found suspended by a dupatta from a ceiling fan with her feet just touching the floor. At autopsy, the ligature mark is situated 1 cm above the thyroid cartilage, is oblique, V-shaped with the apex at the knot behind the left ear, and is non-continuous; petechiae are absent and the hyoid bone is intact. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Typical hanging (self-suspension)","Ligature strangulation","Garrotting","Smothering"],"a":0,"ex":"Correct: A. The findings are characteristic of hanging: the ligature mark lies high, above the thyroid cartilage, is oblique, V-shaped (apex at the knot) and interrupted at the knot. Death in partial hanging (feet touching the ground) occurs through compression of neck vessels — carotid/vertebral occlusion causing cerebral hypoxia — so full body suspension is not required. Petechiae are typically scanty or absent in hanging, whereas they are prominent in strangulation; the hyoid is usually intact in hanging but frequently fractured in manual/ligature strangulation. A strangulation mark would be low (below the thyroid cartilage), horizontal and completely encircling the neck, with a broad fabric or finger-pressure pattern; smothering and garrotting produce no such ligature mark. Memory hook: 'Hanging — high, oblique, V, interrupted; strangulation — low, horizontal, complete circle; petechiae favour strangulation'.","tp":"Asphyxial deaths: hanging","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p75","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with severe traumatic brain injury has been on mechanical ventilation for 24 hours. He has GCS 3/15, bilaterally fixed and dilated pupils, absent corneal and oculocephalic reflexes, absent gag and cough, and no spontaneous respiratory effort. The family gives written consent for organ donation. As per the Transplantation of Human Organs Act and Rules, what is the next appropriate step before declaring brain-stem death?","o":["Certify brain-stem death by a board of four doctors (treating doctor, registered medical practitioner in charge of the hospital, neurologist/neurosurgeon, and a nominee of the medical superintendent) after confirming apnoea by the apnoea test, with a second examination 6 hours later","Certify brain death after demonstrating a flat electroencephalogram for 30 minutes","Obtain certification of brain death by two independent doctors not involved in the transplant team","Obtain written consent for medicolegal autopsy before proceeding"],"a":0,"ex":"Correct: A. Under the THOA 1994 (amended 2011) rules, brain-stem death must be certified by a board of four doctors — the treating consultant, the RMO in charge of the hospital, a neurologist/neurosurgeon, and a medical officer authorised by the medical superintendent — after the apnoea test (no respiratory effort despite PaCO2 > 60 mmHg), with certification repeated after 6 hours. EEG is not mandatory for certification of brain-stem death in India, so a flat-trace EEG alone is insufficient; the two-physician standard is not the Indian legal requirement. Medicolegal autopsy consent is not needed for organ donation once brain-stem death is certified and consent obtained. Memory hook: 'Brain death in India = 4 doctors + apnoea test × 2, 6 hours apart; EEG optional'.","tp":"Brain death & organ donation","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p76","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman reports being sexually assaulted about 18 hours earlier. The investigating officer asks the examining doctor to 'insert two fingers into her vagina to determine whether she is habituated to sexual intercourse'. What should the doctor do?","o":["Perform the two-finger test and record the findings as part of the routine examination","Refuse the two-finger test as it violates her dignity and privacy, examine her for injuries and collect vaginal, oral and perianal swabs for evidence within the window period","Perform the test only after obtaining her written informed consent","Refer her to a gynecologist for the test as it is a specialist procedure"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: B. The Supreme Court (Lillu v. State of Haryana, 2013) banned the two-finger (virginity) test, holding it violates the victim's right to privacy and dignity and has no scientific basis; it stigmatises the victim and must not be performed regardless of consent or police request. The doctor should instead conduct a dignified examination, document injuries, and collect vaginal, oral and anal swabs with proper labelling and chain of custody — ideally within 96 hours of the assault. Consent from the victim (and parent/guardian if a minor) is required for the examination itself, but consent cannot legitimise the banned test; referral to a gynecologist would also be wrong because the test remains impermissible. Memory hook: 'Two-finger test banned 2013 — virginity is a social concept, not a forensic finding; swabs within 96 hours, chain of custody'.","tp":"Sexual offences: examination","s":"Forensic Medicine","qid":"p77","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl is brought to the school health clinic. Her mother asks about the HPV vaccine, which has been launched in her district's school immunization program for adolescent girls. As per the current Indian Academy of Pediatrics (IAP) and WHO recommendations for girls aged 9-14 years, what is the correct HPV vaccination schedule?","o":["Two doses, 6 months apart","Three doses, 0, 2 and 6 months","Single dose only","Two doses, 3 months apart"],"a":0,"ex":"For girls aged 9-14 years, a 2-dose schedule (0 and 6 months) of HPV vaccine is recommended because immunogenicity in this age group is excellent; a 3-dose schedule (0, 2, 6 months) is only for those initiating vaccination at ≥15 years of age or immunocompromised individuals. WHO's 2022 SAGE single-dose option exists for operational flexibility, but the standard 2-dose schedule remains the recommendation in India's adolescent vaccination programs. Memory hook: '9-14 years = 2 doses; ≥15 years = 3 doses'.","tp":"Immunization","s":"PSM","qid":"p78","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 27-year-old man presents with fever, severe headache, and a vesiculopustular rash beginning on his face and spreading to the palms and soles, with tender bilateral inguinal lymphadenopathy. He returned 10 days ago from a trip to the Democratic Republic of Congo, where an outbreak of the disease linked to a new clade (Ib) was declared a Public Health Emergency of International Concern by WHO in August 2024. Which investigation provides the definitive diagnosis?","o":["PCR of lesion swab (skin lesion fluid)","NS1 antigen test on serum","IgM antibody test on serum collected on day 2 of rash","Blood culture for bacterial pathogens"],"a":0,"ex":"PCR (molecular testing) of skin lesion material is the confirmatory test for mpox (formerly monkeypox); swabs from lesion roofs/exudate are the preferred specimen. The clade Ib outbreak in DRC spread primarily via sexual and close household contact, prompting the August 2024 PHEIC declaration. NS1 is for dengue, IgM serology is unreliable in early disease (antibodies appear only after ~5 days), and blood cultures are irrelevant. Memory hook: 'Pox = PCR of lesion; PHEIC 2024 = mpox clade Ib'.","tp":"Emerging infections","s":"PSM","qid":"p79","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p80","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A 36-week neonate develops respiratory distress shortly after birth, and the chest X-ray is shown. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Respiratory Distress Syndrome","Transient Tachypnea of Newborn","Neonatal Pneumonia","Meconium Aspiration Syndrome"],"a":1,"ex":"The key clues are the gestational age (36 weeks, late preterm), timing (distress soon after birth), and the CXR findings. The X-ray shows perihilar streaking, fluid in the horizontal fissure, and mild hyperinflation, but not the classic \"ground-glass\" appearance. (A) Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS): Less likely in a 36-weeker, and the CXR is not the classic \"ground-glass.\" (B) Transient Tachypnea of Newborn (TTN): This is the most likely diagnosis. It's caused by retained fetal lung fluid, is common in late preterm infants, and presents immediately at birth. The CXR findings of \"streaky,\" \"hazy\" lungs with fluid in the fissures are classic for TTN. (C) Neonatal Pneumonia: Possible, but TTN is more common and fits the picture better. (D) Meconium Aspiration Syndrome: No history of meconium-stained fluid is given, and the CXR shows coarse, patchy infiltrates, which is different from the image.","tp":"Multiple Pregnancy","s":"Paediatrics","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis is confirmed to have rifampicin-resistant TB (RR-TB) on cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification test; drug susceptibility testing shows susceptibility to fluoroquinolones. She is treatment-naive for second-line drugs. As per the current NTEP (2024) guidelines, which regimen is the preferred first-line treatment for her?","o":["BPaLM (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) for 6 months","9-month all-oral shorter MDR regimen (bedaquiline, levofloxacin, linezolid, clofazimine, cycloserine, ethambutol)","18-month conventional regimen with an injectable (kanamycin)","6-month daily regimen of HRZE (first-line drugs)"],"a":0,"ex":"NTEP adopted WHO's all-oral BPaLM regimen (6 months) as the preferred treatment for MDR/RR-TB in patients with fluoroquinolone-susceptible disease, replacing the older 9-month and 18-month regimens. Injectable agents (kanamycin) are obsolete due to high ototoxicity and poor outcomes. First-line HRZE would fail since the isolate is rifampicin-resistant. Memory hook: 'MDR-TB 2024 = BPaLM 6 months; FQ-resistant or intolerance → BPaL or longer regimens'.","tp":"Tuberculosis control","s":"PSM","qid":"p81","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A screening programme for diabetes mellitus uses a point-of-care test with 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity. The prevalence of diabetes in the target population is 2%. If a person tests positive, what is the approximate probability that they actually have diabetes (positive predictive value)?","o":["27%","90%","18%","95%"],"a":0,"ex":"PPV = (sensitivity × prevalence) / [(sensitivity × prevalence) + (1 − specificity) × (1 − prevalence)] = (0.90 × 0.02) / [(0.90 × 0.02) + (0.05 × 0.98)] = 0.018/0.067 ≈ 27%. Despite the test's high sensitivity and specificity, the low prevalence (2%) means most positive results are false positives — PPV falls with falling prevalence. 90% is the sensitivity (trap), 1.8% is prevalence × sensitivity, 95% is specificity. Memory hook: 'Rare disease → low PPV, no matter how good the test'.","tp":"Screening tests","s":"PSM","qid":"p82","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman visits a well-woman clinic for cervical cancer screening. She was previously screened with VIA twice, with normal results. Based on the current WHO (2021) and updated Indian guidance recommending HPV DNA-based screening, which approach should she now receive?","o":["HPV DNA testing, and if negative, repeat after 5 years","Pap smear every 3 years from age 25","VIA every 3 years, as in the earlier programme","Annual Pap smear and HPV co-testing from now until menopause"],"a":0,"ex":"WHO 2021 guidance and India's revised screening strategy recommend HPV DNA testing as the primary screening test for women aged 30-65 years, at 5-year intervals (self-sampling is allowed). VIA is being phased out because of operator dependence and low reproducibility, and cytology (Pap) is resource-intensive; India's earlier programme used VIA 3-yearly, which is now outdated. Annual screening of any type is unnecessary — HPV-negative women have very low cancer risk for ≥5 years. Memory hook: 'HPV DNA primary, 30-65 years, 5-yearly; VIA is history'.","tp":"Cancer screening","s":"PSM","qid":"p83","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"In a case-control study of lung cancer and smoking, 300 cases and 300 controls were enrolled: 210 of the 300 cases were smokers, while only 90 of the 300 controls were smokers. What is the odds ratio for lung cancer in smokers compared with non-smokers?","o":["5.4","2.3","1.7","0.4"],"a":0,"ex":"OR = (a × d)/(b × c) where a = exposed cases (210), b = unexposed cases (90), c = exposed controls (90), d = unexposed controls (210): OR = (210 × 210)/(90 × 90) = 5.44 ≈ 5.4. In a case-control study the relative risk cannot be calculated directly because the population denominators are unknown, so the OR (a valid approximation of RR for rare diseases) is the effect measure of choice. Smokers have ~5.4 times the odds of lung cancer. Memory hook: 'Case-control = odds ratio only, always (a×d)/(b×c)'.","tp":"Study designs","s":"PSM","qid":"p84","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 74-year-old farmer, already a beneficiary of Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (PM-JAY) with his family, asks whether he is eligible for additional health cover now that he is 70 years old. As per the Union Cabinet decision of September 2024, what applies to him?","o":["All citizens aged 70 years and above are eligible for PM-JAY with a fresh card and an additional top-up of ₹5 lakh per year, irrespective of their socioeconomic status","No additional cover; only BPL families above 70 years are covered","The existing family card automatically doubles to ₹10 lakh per year for households with a member above 70","He must first join the National Pension Scheme to qualify for senior-citizen cover"],"a":0,"ex":"In September 2024 the Union Cabinet expanded PM-JAY to cover all citizens aged 70 years and above with a separate, dedicated card and a top-up health cover of ₹5 lakh per year, regardless of income or SECC status. This is on top of any existing family card cover. There is no pension linkage, and the ₹10-lakh doubling is incorrect — the cover is an additional ₹5 lakh. Memory hook: 'PM-JAY 2024: 70+ = own card, extra ₹5 lakh, everyone'.","tp":"Health insurance","s":"PSM","qid":"p85","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A state's demographic report shows that the Net Reproduction Rate (NRR) of the population has reached exactly 1.0. Which of the following is the most accurate interpretation of this finding?","o":["The population has attained replacement-level fertility: each generation of women is exactly replacing itself after accounting for female mortality","The population will double in one generation","Fertility is below replacement level and the population is rapidly shrinking","Mortality has been eliminated in the reproductive age group"],"a":0,"ex":"NRR = 1.0 means each woman, on average, produces exactly one daughter who survives to reproductive age — the definitive indicator of replacement-level fertility, as it accounts for both fertility and mortality. A Gross Reproduction Rate of 1.0 does NOT indicate replacement because not all female infants survive to childbearing age (NRR = GRR × survival probability). TFR of ~2.1 is the rough replacement value only when mortality is low. Memory hook: 'NRR 1.0 = replacement; GRR ignores death'.","tp":"Demography","s":"PSM","qid":"p86","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman at 22 weeks of gestation is diagnosed on anomaly scan to have a fetus with Down syndrome (trisomy 21). She requests medical termination of pregnancy. Under the Medical Termination of Pregnancy (Amendment) Act 2021, what is required to legally terminate her pregnancy at this gestational age?","o":["Opinion of two registered medical practitioners","Opinion of one registered medical practitioner","Opinion of a Medical Board constituted by the state government","Written consent of her husband alone"],"a":0,"ex":"Under the MTP (Amendment) Act 2021, termination between 20 and 24 weeks requires the opinion of two registered medical practitioners (for specified categories including fetal anomalies); beyond 24 weeks, a state-constituted Medical Board is needed, but that does not apply here. Up to 20 weeks needs only one doctor. The woman's own consent is paramount — spousal consent is not required. Memory hook: '20-24 weeks = 2 doctors; >24 weeks = Medical Board'.","tp":"MTP Act 2021","s":"PSM","qid":"p87","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"An 8-month-old boy is brought with wasting, bilateral pitting edema of the feet, a distended abdomen, and sparse, discolored hair. His weight-for-height Z-score is −3.5 SD. He is diagnosed with edematous severe acute malnutrition (kwashiorkor). What is the correct initial therapeutic feeding?","o":["Admit and start starter formula F-75","Start catch-up formula F-100 immediately","Start ready-to-use therapeutic food (RUTF) at full dose","Encourage home-based complementary feeding and review in 2 weeks"],"a":0,"ex":"Edematous SAM is a medical emergency requiring inpatient care. Feeding begins with F-75 starter formula (low protein, low energy) to stabilize the compromised gut and enzyme systems, advancing to F-100/RUTF (high energy) in the catch-up phase after appetite returns. Starting F-100 or RUTF immediately risks refeeding syndrome and electrolyte shifts. Home management is only for uncomplicated, non-edematous SAM with good appetite. Memory hook: 'F-75 first (stabilize), F-100 next (catch-up); edematous SAM = admit'.","tp":"Nutrition","s":"PSM","qid":"p88","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"In a tertiary care hospital, which of the following items should be discarded into the RED-coloured container as per the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016?","o":["Used IV fluid bottles and saline infusion tubing","Amputated limb from a gangrenous toe","Used hypodermic needles","Microbiological cultures and stocks of microorganisms"],"a":0,"ex":"The red container holds recyclable contaminated plastic waste such as IV sets, tubing, urine bags and bottles — these are sent for recycling after disinfection. Human anatomical waste (amputated limbs) goes in yellow bags; used needles and sharps go in white puncture-proof containers (with pre-treatment, e.g., needle-cutters); microbiological cultures and infected lab waste go in yellow. Memory hook: 'Red = recyclable plastics; Yellow = anatomical/contaminated; White = sharps'.","tp":"Biomedical waste","s":"PSM","qid":"p89","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p90","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A male patient who has ingested an unknown substance now exhibits profuse sweating, excessive tearing, diarrhea, muscle weakness, and tachycardia. What is the correct management for this condition?","o":["Calcium gluconate","Pralidoxime","Obidoxime","Atropine"],"a":3,"ex":"The patient's symptoms—diaphoresis (sweating), lacrimation (tearing), diarrhea, and muscle weakness—are classic features of a cholinergic crisis. This is most commonly caused by poisoning with acetylcholinesterase inhibitors, such as organophosphate or carbamate insecticides. These toxins cause an excess of acetylcholine at both muscarinic and nicotinic synapses. The immediate, life-saving antidote is atropine. Atropine is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors. It works by blocking the excessive parasympathetic effects, thereby drying up secretions (bronchorrhea, salivation), increasing the heart rate (if bradycardic), and reducing bronchospasm. Large and repeated doses are often required. Pralidoxime (B) is an oxime used as an adjunct in organophosphate (but not carbamate) poisoning to reactivate the cholinesterase enzyme and treat nicotinic symptoms like muscle weakness, but atropine is the first-line and most critical intervention.","tp":"HELLP Syndrome","s":"Orthopaedics","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy is brought with loud snoring, mouth breathing, restless sleep, and intermittent bed-wetting for 8 months. He had recurrent tonsillitis in the past. Tonsils are 3+/4 (kissing) and overnight polysomnography shows an AHI of 7. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line management?","o":["Nocturnal CPAP","Adenotonsillectomy","Weight reduction alone","Intranasal steroid spray with review in 6 months"],"a":1,"ex":"Adenotonsillectomy is first-line treatment for paediatric OSA with adenotonsillar hypertrophy, and this child has moderate OSA (AHI 5-10) with 3+ tonsils. CPAP (A) is reserved for residual OSA after surgery or when surgery is contraindicated. Weight reduction (C) helps when obesity is the dominant factor but is inadequate alone for adenotonsillar obstruction. Intranasal steroids (D) are useful in mild OSA with allergic rhinitis, not in moderate OSA with kissing tonsils. Hook: 'Snoring child + big tonsils + AHI >5 = take the tonsils out.'","tp":"Paediatric obstructive sleep apnoea","s":"ENT","qid":"p91","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman woke up 2 days ago with sudden hearing loss and ringing in her right ear after a presumed viral illness. She has no vertigo, no ear discharge, and otoscopy is normal. Pure-tone audiometry shows right-sided sensorineural hearing loss averaging 65 dB (0.5-4 kHz) with a normal left ear. She has well-controlled type 2 diabetes. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?","o":["Oral prednisolone 60 mg/day with a tapering course","Intravenous acyclovir","MRI of the internal auditory meatus before any treatment","Wait-and-watch with repeat audiometry in 4 weeks"],"a":0,"ex":"This is sudden sensorineural hearing loss; the AAO-HNS guideline recommends prompt systemic corticosteroids (prednisolone 60 mg daily, tapered) within 2 weeks of onset, ideally within 72 hours, and treatment must not be delayed for imaging; in diabetics, glucose should be monitored during the course. Antivirals (B) have no proven benefit in SSNHL. MRI (C) is important to exclude retrocochlear lesions (vestibular schwannoma) but is performed without delaying steroids. Watchful waiting (D) risks irreversible cochlear hair-cell loss. Hook: 'SSNHL: treat first (steroids), image after (MRI), salvage with intratympanic steroids.'","tp":"Sudden sensorineural hearing loss","s":"ENT","qid":"p92","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old hypertensive man presents with profuse right-sided epistaxis that has continued for 40 minutes despite a properly placed anterior nasal pack. Flexible endoscopy shows blood streaming from behind the posterior choana. A posterior nasal pack is inserted, but oozing continues over the next 12 hours. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Repeat posterior packing with a Foley catheter","Endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation","External carotid angiography with embolisation","Septoplasty and cauterisation of Little's area"],"a":1,"ex":"Persistent posterior epistaxis after failed packing is managed by endoscopic sphenopalatine artery ligation (SPAL), which is definitive, highly effective, and carries minimal morbidity. Repeat packing (A) is a temporising measure already tried and adds aspiration and infection risk. Embolisation (C) is an alternative but is preferred when SPAL fails or is contraindicated, as it carries stroke risk and higher cost. Septoplasty with cautery (D) targets Little's area, an anterior bleed, and does not address a posterior source. Hook: 'Posterior bleed + packing fails = SPAL.'","tp":"Epistaxis","s":"ENT","qid":"p93","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has had foul-smelling discharge from his left ear for 5 years with progressive hearing loss. Otoscopy shows an attic perforation with white, pearly keratin debris and granulations. Pure-tone audiometry reveals a 45 dB conductive loss. Which of the following is the most appropriate next investigation to guide surgical planning?","o":["High-resolution CT of the temporal bone","MRI of the brain with contrast","Plain X-ray of the mastoids (Schuller's view)","Repeat audiometry after 6 weeks of topical antibiotic drops"],"a":0,"ex":"Attic perforation with keratin debris indicates cholesteatoma (unsafe CSOM); high-resolution CT of the temporal bone defines disease extent and critical complications such as ossicular erosion, tegmen or facial canal dehiscence, and labyrinthine fistula, directly guiding surgery. MRI with DWI (B) is complementary for soft-tissue versus cholesteatoma differentiation in recurrent disease, not the primary planning tool. Plain mastoid X-ray (C) has no role in modern practice. Topical drops alone (D) are wrong because cholesteatoma is a surgical disease, not medically curable. Hook: 'Unsafe CSOM: clinical diagnosis, CT for extent, surgery for cure.'","tp":"Cholesteatoma (unsafe CSOM)","s":"ENT","qid":"p94","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 11%, in ketoacidosis) presents with fever, left facial pain, and nasal obstruction for 4 days. Endoscopy shows a black necrotic eschar over the left middle turbinate, and CT shows soft-tissue opacification with subtle erosion of the lamina papyracea. KOH mount of a biopsy sample reveals broad, non-septate hyphae. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","o":["Oral itraconazole for 6 weeks with outpatient follow-up","IV liposomal amphotericin B with urgent endoscopic debridement","IV broad-spectrum antibiotics with nasal saline irrigation alone","Systemic corticosteroids with antifungals to reduce inflammation"],"a":1,"ex":"This is acute invasive fungal rhinosinusitis (mucormycosis) — a combined surgical and medical emergency: urgent endoscopic debridement, IV liposomal amphotericin B (isavuconazole is an alternative), and reversal of the predisposing state by correcting ketoacidosis. Oral azoles (A) are used in chronic invasive disease, not the acute fulminant form. Antibiotics alone (C) ignore the fungal pathogen and delay surgery. Corticosteroids (D) are contraindicated as they worsen immunosuppression. Hook: 'Black eschar + DKA = mucormycosis: cut (debride) + cure (liposomal amphotericin B).'","tp":"Invasive fungal rhinosinusitis","s":"ENT","qid":"p95","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman reports recurrent episodes of spontaneous vertigo lasting 2-4 hours with nausea, photophobia, phonophobia, and throbbing headache during some episodes. She has a history of migraine without aura since adolescence and motion sickness in childhood. Otoscopy, audiometry, and caloric testing are normal; positional testing is negative. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?","o":["Betahistine 16 mg thrice daily","Epley's repositioning manoeuvre","Lifestyle modification and migraine prophylaxis (e.g., propranolol)","Intratympanic gentamicin instillation"],"a":2,"ex":"Episodic vertigo with migrainous features (headache, photophobia, phonophobia) and a normal ENT work-up fits vestibular migraine (Bárány/AAO-HNS criteria); management is migraine-based — trigger avoidance, lifestyle measures, and prophylaxis (propranolol, amitriptyline, flunarizine), with triptans for acute attacks. Betahistine (A) is used for Meniere's-type symptoms and is ineffective here. Epley (B) treats BPPV, excluded by negative positional testing. Gentamicin (D) is ablative therapy for disabling Meniere's disease and would be harmful. Hook: 'Vertigo + migraine history = vestibular migraine — treat the migraine, not the ear.'","tp":"Vestibular migraine","s":"ENT","qid":"p96","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a 2-year history of fluctuating right-sided hearing loss with roaring tinnitus, aural fullness, and attacks of incapacitating vertigo lasting 1-2 hours. Audiometry shows low-frequency sensorineural hearing loss that partially recovers between attacks. Despite 6 months of salt restriction and a diuretic, he has 3 disabling attacks per month. Which of the following is the most appropriate next intervention?","o":["Intratympanic corticosteroid injection","Intratympanic gentamicin ablation","Endolymphatic sac decompression surgery","Labyrinthectomy"],"a":0,"ex":"This is definite Meniere's disease: after oral medical therapy (salt restriction, diuretics) fails, the AAO-HNS 2020 guideline supports intratympanic corticosteroid injection as the next non-ablative step; gentamicin (B) is reserved for failure of steroid injections or when the patient prefers ablative therapy. Endolymphatic sac decompression (C) is a non-ablative surgical option for those failing intratympanic therapy, not the first escalation. Labyrinthectomy (D) is a destructive last resort for non-serviceable hearing and is never first-line. Hook: 'Meniere's ladder: diet → diuretic → intratympanic steroid → intratympanic gentamicin → surgery.'","tp":"Meniere's disease","s":"ENT","qid":"p97","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman presents with sudden, painless loss of vision in the right eye that occurred 8 hours ago. She also reports jaw claudication while eating, scalp tenderness on combing her hair, and low-grade fever for 3 weeks. Best corrected visual acuity is perception of light in the right eye and 6/6 in the left. Examination shows a pale, swollen optic disc on the right with a relative afferent pupillary defect. ESR is 98 mm/hr and CRP is 45 mg/L. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Immediate temporal artery biopsy, and start steroids only after histopathological confirmation","Oral prednisolone 40 mg daily and temporal artery biopsy within 48 hours","Intravenous methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days followed by oral prednisolone; temporal artery biopsy may be deferred up to 1 week","Aspirin 150 mg daily with close observation, as steroids can mask the biopsy result"],"a":2,"ex":"This is arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (AAION) due to giant cell arteritis — sudden visual loss with jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, and markedly elevated ESR/CRP with a pale swollen disc. High-dose systemic corticosteroids (IV methylprednisolone 1 g/day or 60-80 mg oral prednisolone) must be started immediately to protect the fellow eye; temporal artery biopsy can be performed within 7 days of starting steroids without losing diagnostic yield and must not delay therapy. Low-dose prednisolone is inadequate, the diagnosis is clinical so treatment never awaits biopsy confirmation, and aspirin is only adjunctive. Memory hook: 'Biopsy is confirmatory, not a gatekeeper — never let the fellow eye wait.'","tp":"Ischaemic optic neuropathy","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p98","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus of 12 years' duration presents with gradual blurring of vision in both eyes. HbA1c is 8.6%. Visual acuity is 6/24 in the right eye and 6/12 in the left. Fundus examination shows multiple microaneurysms, hard exudates and intraretinal haemorrhages in both eyes with thickening of the right macula. OCT shows centre-involving diabetic macular oedema (central subfield thickness 420 μm) in the right eye. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment for the right eye?","o":["Pars plana vitrectomy","Oral corticosteroids with strict glycaemic control","Focal/grid macular laser photocoagulation","Intravitreal anti-VEGF agent (ranibizumab)"],"a":3,"ex":"Centre-involving diabetic macular oedema with reduced vision (6/24) is treated with intravitreal anti-VEGF agents (ranibizumab, aflibercept or bevacizumab) as first line — this replaced focal/grid laser as the standard of care following DRCR.net Protocol T. Laser remains an option only for non-centre-involving oedema or as an adjunct, vitrectomy is for non-resolving vitreous haemorrhage or tractional detachment, and glycaemic control alone cannot reverse established oedema. Memory hook: 'Laser is OUT for centre-involving DME, anti-VEGF is IN.'","tp":"Diabetic retinopathy","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p99","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p100","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"What is the probable diagnosis based on the finding given in the CT image?","o":["Concha bullosa","Pneumatized superior turbinate","Onodi cell","Haller cell"],"a":1,"ex":"The CT scan (coronal view of paranasal sinuses) shows air-filled expansion within the superior turbinate, indicating pneumatization of this turbinate. A pneumatized turbinate appears as a well-defined air density within the turbinate bone. Differentiation from other options: 1. Concha bullosa refers specifically to pneumatization of the middle turbinate, not the superior turbinate. 2. Onodi cell is a posterior ethmoid air cell located superolateral to the sphenoid sinus. 3. Haller cell is an infraorbital ethmoid air cell located along the orbital floor. Since the air cavity is located within the superior turbinate itself, the correct diagnosis is pneumatized superior turbinate (Option B).","tp":"Hodgkin Lymphoma","s":"Pharmacology","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with Graves' disease (euthyroid on methimazole, a current smoker) has progressive proptosis, eyelid retraction and new-onset diplopia for 6 months. Clinical activity score is 5/7. She has not responded adequately to a course of intravenous methylprednisolone. Which of the following is the targeted therapy now approved for active, moderate-to-severe thyroid eye disease?","o":["Teprotumumab (IGF-1 receptor inhibitor)","Rituximab (anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody)","Infliximab (anti-TNF-alpha antibody)","Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor antibody)"],"a":0,"ex":"Teprotumumab, an insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF-1R) inhibitor, is the first approved targeted therapy for active moderate-to-severe thyroid eye disease and reduces both proptosis and clinical activity score — it is the option of choice in steroid-refractory or steroid-intolerant disease. Rituximab and tocilizumab have been used only off-label in small trials and are not approved for TED; infliximab has no established role. Memory hook: 'TED responds to Teprotumumab — the IG-F(1)R antibody.' Smoking cessation and euthyroidism remain essential adjuncts.","tp":"Thyroid eye disease","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p101","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman underwent uneventful phacoemulsification with IOL implantation in the left eye 3 days ago. She now complains of severe pain, redness and worsening vision. Examination shows lid oedema, marked conjunctival congestion, corneal oedema, a fibrinous anterior chamber reaction with a 2 mm hypopyon, and vision reduced to hand movements. B-scan ultrasonography shows vitreous echoes. What is the most appropriate initial management?","o":["Immediate pars plana vitrectomy with silicone oil tamponade","Oral ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily with topical moxifloxacin hourly","Vitreous tap/biopsy followed by intravitreal injection of vancomycin and ceftazidime","Topical atropine and dexamethasone alone with observation"],"a":2,"ex":"This is acute postoperative bacterial endophthalmitis (onset within days of cataract surgery): acute severe pain, hypopyon and vitreous involvement mandate vitreous sampling (tap or biopsy) with immediate intravitreal antibiotics — vancomycin for Gram-positive cover plus ceftazidime for Gram-negative cover. Systemic and topical antibiotics alone cannot achieve therapeutic vitreous levels; immediate vitrectomy is reserved for eyes presenting with no light perception; steroids are added only after antibiotics are administered.","tp":"Endophthalmitis","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p102","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 25-year-old contact lens wearer presents with severe pain, tearing and photophobia in the left eye that is disproportionate to the clinical findings. He admits to rinsing his lenses in tap water. Slit-lamp examination shows a ring-shaped corneal infiltrate with a central epithelial defect and radial perineural infiltrates. What is the most appropriate initial medical therapy?","o":["Topical natamycin 5%","Topical fortified cefazolin and tobramycin","Oral acyclovir with topical trifluridine","Topical polyhexamethylene biguanide (PHMB) 0.02% with propamidine isethionate"],"a":3,"ex":"Severe pain out of proportion to signs with a ring infiltrate and radial perineuritis in a contact lens wearer who used tap water is classic Acanthamoeba keratitis, treated with a biguanide (PHMB or chlorhexidine) plus a diamidine (propamidine). Natamycin is for fungal keratitis, fortified antibiotics for bacterial keratitis (which would show a dense suppurative infiltrate without the ring pattern), and antivirals for herpetic disease with dendritic lesions. Memory hook: 'Tap water + contact lens = Acanthamoeba = PHMB.'","tp":"Microbial keratitis","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p103","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A preterm infant born at 32 weeks' gestation (birth weight 1.4 kg) received supplemental oxygen for 2 weeks in the NICU and is now 3 weeks old. According to current Indian (NNF/NPCI) screening recommendations, when should the first retinal screening examination for retinopathy of prematurity be performed?","o":["At term-equivalent age (40 weeks postmenstrual age)","At 6 months of age, when fundus examination can be done under anaesthesia","Within 24 hours of birth, since oxygen exposure mandates urgent screening","At 4 weeks of postnatal age or 30-31 weeks postmenstrual age, whichever is later"],"a":3,"ex":"Indian guidelines (NNF/NPCI 2023) recommend ROP screening for all preterm infants with birth weight ≤2000 g or gestational age ≤34 weeks (plus older infants with risk factors such as oxygen therapy), with the first examination at 4 weeks of postnatal age or 30-31 weeks postmenstrual age, whichever is later. Screening within 24 hours of birth is not required in the absence of severe systemic instability, and delaying to term equivalence or later misses the therapeutic window (laser or anti-VEGF) for threshold disease. Memory hook: 'Screen ROP at 4 weeks of age or 31 weeks PMA — whichever comes later.'","tp":"Retinopathy of prematurity","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p104","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with hypertension and atrial fibrillation (not on anticoagulants) develops sudden, painless, complete loss of vision in the left eye while watching television 1 hour ago. Vision is no perception of light, there is a relative afferent pupillary defect, and fundus examination shows a cherry-red spot with retinal whitening of the posterior pole. Blood pressure is 150/88 mmHg. According to the current AHA scientific statement, what is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Reassurance and review in the outpatient clinic in 2 weeks","Ocular massage with anterior chamber paracentesis to dislodge the embolus","Immediate laser photocoagulation of the occluded artery","Urgent evaluation and management as an acute ischaemic stroke, including consideration of intravenous thrombolysis within the window"],"a":3,"ex":"Central retinal artery occlusion is now managed as a stroke equivalent: the 2021 AHA statement mandates emergent evaluation (carotid imaging, cardiac/rhythm assessment) and consideration of IV thrombolysis within 4.5 hours of onset. Digital ocular massage and anterior chamber paracentesis are no longer recommended as they rarely restore perfusion and delay stroke workup; laser photocoagulation has no role in acute CRAO. The cherry-red spot represents the thin fovea showing through ischaemic, oedematous retina. Memory hook: 'Cherry-red spot = retinal infarction = treat like a stroke, not a massage.'","tp":"Retinal artery occlusion","s":"Ophthalmology","qid":"p105","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old man, 3 weeks after an anterior wall STEMI, presents with exertional dyspnoea (NYHA class II). Echocardiography shows left ventricular ejection fraction 30% with anteroapical hypokinesia. He is on aspirin, ticagrelor, atorvastatin, ramipril, metoprolol succinate and spironolactone, with normal renal function and potassium. Which drug is the most appropriate next addition to his regimen?","o":["Dapagliflozin 10 mg once daily","Ivabradine 5 mg twice daily","Verapamil 40 mg thrice daily","Digoxin 0.25 mg once daily"],"a":0,"ex":"Answer: Dapagliflozin. SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin/empagliflozin) are class I recommendations in all patients with HFrEF (EF ≤40%) irrespective of diabetes, added to ACEi/ARNI + beta-blocker + MRA, reducing HF hospitalisation and CV death. Verapamil and other non-dihydropyridine CCBs are contraindicated in HFrEF (negative inotropy). Ivabradine is reserved for persistent sinus rhythm ≥70 bpm despite maximal beta-blockade, and digoxin only for refractory symptoms or AF — both after SGLT2i. Memory hook: '4 pillars' of HFrEF = ACEi/ARNI, BB, MRA, SGLT2i.","tp":"Heart failure","s":"Medicine","qid":"p106","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a 5-year history of hypertension has clinic BP 154/96 mm Hg on maximum doses of telmisartan 80 mg, amlodipine 10 mg and chlorthalidone 25 mg daily, with confirmed good adherence. Home BP readings are similar; he has no snoring, normal serum potassium and eGFR 88 mL/min. Which is the most appropriate fourth drug to add?","o":["Metoprolol succinate 50 mg","Doxazosin 4 mg","Clonidine 0.1 mg","Spironolactone 25 mg"],"a":3,"ex":"Answer: Spironolactone. This is resistant hypertension (BP uncontrolled on 3 drugs including a diuretic at maximal tolerated doses); AHA 2018/ESH 2023 guidelines and the PATHWAY-2 trial establish mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (spironolactone 25-50 mg) as the preferred fourth-line agent. Beta-blockers are not a standard 4th step; doxazosin is a 5th-line option; clonidine is reserved for refractory cases given CNS side effects. Rule out secondary causes (OSA, renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism) and pseudo-resistance before adding drugs.","tp":"Hypertension","s":"Medicine","qid":"p107","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes of 8 years duration has HbA1c 8.2%, eGFR 32 mL/min/1.73 m² (CKD G3b), urine albumin-creatinine ratio 300 mg/g and mild exertional dyspnoea with an echocardiographic EF of 45%. She is on metformin 1000 mg daily. Which add-on agent is most appropriate for her glycaemic and cardio-renal management?","o":["Glimepiride 2 mg","Dapagliflozin 10 mg","Pioglitazone 15 mg","Sitagliptin 100 mg"],"a":1,"ex":"Answer: Dapagliflozin. SGLT2 inhibitors are class I for T2DM with CKD (KDIGO: initiate at eGFR ≥20 mL/min) and in heart failure across the EF spectrum, providing renal and CV protection independent of glycaemia. Pioglitazone causes fluid retention and is avoided in HF. Glimepiride has hypoglycaemia risk with no organ-protection data. DPP-4 inhibitors are cardiorenal-neutral and not preferred when an SGLT2i is indicated. Continue metformin (dose reduction at eGFR <45, stop at <30), and monitor for euglycaemic DKA and genital mycotic infections on SGLT2i.","tp":"Diabetes mellitus","s":"Medicine","qid":"p108","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with diabetic kidney disease (eGFR 24 mL/min/1.73 m²) has Hb 9.0 g/dL, MCV 82 fL, ferritin 90 ng/mL, transferrin saturation 16%, and normal B12 and folate. He is asymptomatic apart from fatigue. Per current KDIGO recommendations for anaemia of CKD, what is the most appropriate first step?","o":["Start darbepoetin alfa 40 mcg weekly","Transfuse packed red cells to Hb 10 g/dL","Give a trial of iron therapy (oral or IV)","Start erythropoietin and IV iron simultaneously"],"a":2,"ex":"Answer: Trial of iron therapy. KDIGO 2024 recommends a trial of iron when TSAT ≤30% and ferritin ≤500 ng/mL — this patient's TSAT 16% confirms iron-deficient erythropoiesis despite normal ferritin (ferritin is an acute-phase reactant, unreliable in CKD). IV iron is preferred in dialysis patients and when ESA is used; oral iron acceptable in non-dialysis CKD. ESA is added only if Hb remains low once iron-replete. Transfusion is reserved for symptomatic/severe anaemia. Hook: 'Iron first, ESA second — never withhold iron in CKD anaemia.'","tp":"Chronic kidney disease","s":"Medicine","qid":"p109","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p110","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"How would you diagnose a patient with an ABCA1 gene mutation, low HDL levels, and tonsils appearing as shown in the image?","o":["Tangier's disease","Antiauthoritarianism","Hyperpigmentation","Wolman's disease"],"a":0,"ex":"The combination of clinical and genetic findings is pathognomonic for Tangier's disease. This is a rare autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the ABCA1 gene. The ABCA1 transporter is crucial for the efflux of cholesterol from cells to Apolipoprotein A1, which is the first step in the formation of high-density lipoprotein (HDL). A defective ABCA1 transporter leads to a near-complete absence of HDL in the plasma and the accumulation of cholesteryl esters in various tissues, especially the reticuloendothelial system. This accumulation in the tonsils gives them a characteristic and striking enlarged, yellow-orange appearance, as seen in the image. Other features include hepatosplenomegaly and peripheral neuropathy. Abetalipoproteinemia (B) affects LDL and VLDL formation. Wolman's disease (D) is a lysosomal acid lipase deficiency. The specific tonsillar appearance and the ABCA1 mutation are definitive for Tangier's disease.","tp":"Septic Arthritis","s":"ENT","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has newly diagnosed smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis; Xpert MTB/RIF confirms sensitivity to rifampicin. Chest X-ray shows no cavitation, and he is HIV-negative. Per the current WHO-endorsed strategy adopted by India's NTEP, which regimen should be prescribed?","o":["4-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen (2HPZM/2HPM)","6-month regimen of 2HRZE/4HR","9-month regimen of 2HRZE/7HR","12-month regimen for multidrug-resistant TB"],"a":0,"ex":"Answer: 4-month 2HPZM/2HPM. WHO 2022 (adopted by NTEP in 2025) recommends this shortened daily regimen — rifapentine plus moxifloxacin replaces rifampicin and ethambutol — for drug-susceptible pulmonary TB in eligible patients (HIV-negative, no cavitation, no RIF resistance). It cuts treatment time by a third with comparable efficacy and lower total pill burden. The 6-month HRZE regimen remains an alternative where the new regimen is unavailable or ineligible. Longer regimens are for drug-resistant TB. Hook: '2025: DS-TB default = 4 months, 4 drugs, daily.'","tp":"Tuberculosis","s":"Medicine","qid":"p111","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a mechanical mitral valve prosthesis (inserted 3 years ago for rheumatic mitral stenosis) develops new-onset atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response. Which anticoagulation strategy is most appropriate?","o":["Apixaban 5 mg twice daily","Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily","Warfarin targeting INR 2.5-3.5","Rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily"],"a":2,"ex":"Answer: Warfarin. DOACs are contraindicated in mechanical heart valves — the RE-ALIGN trial showed excess thromboembolic and bleeding events with dabigatran. Warfarin with target INR 2.5-3.5 (3.0-4.0 for mitral position per some societies, though 2.5-3.5 is standard for modern valves with bridge dosing) is required. In non-valvular AF DOACs are preferred over warfarin, but the mechanical prosthesis overrides this. Add a rate-control agent for symptom control; consider ablation/valve-related discussion as per valvular AF algorithms.","tp":"Atrial fibrillation","s":"Medicine","qid":"p112","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman presents with sudden breathlessness and pleuritic chest pain. BP 82/60 mm Hg, pulse 120/min, JVP raised. ECG shows sinus tachycardia; echocardiography reveals a dilated, hypokinetic right ventricle; CTPA confirms bilateral proximal pulmonary embolism. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["IV unfractionated heparin alone","Insertion of an inferior vena cava filter","Catheter-directed thrombolysis with reduced-dose alteplase","Systemic thrombolysis with alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours"],"a":3,"ex":"Answer: Systemic thrombolysis. This is high-risk (massive) PE with shock — 2019 ESC guidelines give class I recommendation for immediate systemic thrombolysis; heparin alone cannot relieve obstruction fast enough in the setting of hypotension. Catheter-directed therapy is reserved for intermediate-high-risk PE (normotensive with RV dysfunction) after failed/contraindicated systemic lysis. An IVC filter alone does not treat the clot and is indicated for recurrent embolism despite adequate anticoagulation or when anticoagulation is contraindicated.","tp":"Venous thromboembolism","s":"Medicine","qid":"p113","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with small-cell lung cancer on chemotherapy develops hyponatraemia: serum Na 122 mEq/L. He is euvolemic, urine osmolality 480 mOsm/kg, urine sodium 60 mEq/L, TSH and cortisol normal. He is asymptomatic apart from mild fatigue. Which is the most appropriate first-line management?","o":["Tolvaptan 15 mg once daily","Fluid restriction to 1 L/day","Demeclocycline 300 mg twice daily","Bolus of 150 mL 3% saline"],"a":1,"ex":"Answer: Fluid restriction. This is SIADH (euvolemic, urine osmolality >100 mOsm/kg, urine Na >40 mEq/L, normal thyroid/adrenal function) — first-line treatment is fluid restriction to 0.5-1 L/day per the 2023 European hyponatraemia guideline. Hypertonic saline is reserved for severe symptoms (seizures, coma) or Na <120 with symptoms, with slow correction to avoid osmotic demyelination. Tolvaptan is second-line and contraindicated in liver disease and hypovolemia; demeclocycline is obsolete. Treating the underlying SCLC also addresses the cause.","tp":"Hyponatremia","s":"Medicine","qid":"p114","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 67-year-old postmenopausal woman has received denosumab 60 mg SC 6-monthly for osteoporosis for 5 years. Her DXA T-score is now -2.9 and she has had no fractures, but she wants to stop the injections. What is the most appropriate advice?","o":["Transition to oral alendronate at the time of her next scheduled denosumab dose","Stop denosumab and monitor bone density after a 2-year drug holiday","Switch to teriparatide 20 mcg daily","Switch to raloxifene 60 mg daily"],"a":0,"ex":"Answer: Transition to a bisphosphonate. Denosumab has NO drug holiday — discontinuation causes rebound osteoclast activity with rapid bone loss and spontaneous vertebral fractures. Patients should receive a bisphosphonate (alendronate or zoledronate) at the next scheduled dose time to maintain suppression. The 'drug holiday' concept applies only to bisphosphonates. Teriparatide after denosumab causes transient BMD loss (anabolic agent first suppresses after denosumab), and raloxifene is inferior for nonvertebral protection. Hook: 'Denosumab = lifelong or bridge to bisphosphonate.'","tp":"Osteoporosis","s":"Medicine","qid":"p115","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has migraine without aura on 12-14 days per month. She failed propranolol and amitriptyline, and developed nephrolithiasis on topiramate. She is otherwise healthy. Which preventive therapy is most appropriate next?","o":["Sumatriptan 100 mg at onset of each attack","Botulinum toxin A 155 units every 12 weeks","Topiramate 25 mg twice daily","Erenumab 140 mg subcutaneously monthly"],"a":3,"ex":"Answer: Erenumab. Anti-CGRP monoclonal antibodies (erenumab, galcanezumab, fremanezumab, eptinezumab) are recommended for episodic migraine after failure of ≥2 oral preventives, per AHS/EFNS positions updated through 2023-24. Topiramate caused nephrolithiasis (a known adverse effect — carbonic anhydrase inhibition), so it is contraindicated. Sumatriptan is abortive, not preventive. Botulinum toxin A is licensed only for chronic migraine (≥15 headache days/month). Hook: 'Two failed orals → anti-CGRP mAb; Botox only if ≥15 days.'","tp":"Migraine","s":"Medicine","qid":"p116","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old never-smoker woman presents with stage IV lung adenocarcinoma (bilateral pulmonary nodules, malignant pleural effusion). NGS shows EGFR exon 19 deletion; PD-L1 expression is 90%. Which is the most appropriate first-line therapy?","o":["Pembrolizumab monotherapy","Osimertinib 80 mg once daily","Platinum doublet chemotherapy with pembrolizumab","Crizotinib 250 mg twice daily"],"a":1,"ex":"Answer: Osimertinib. In NSCLC with an activating EGFR mutation, a targeted TKI is the standard of care regardless of PD-L1 status — checkpoint inhibitors are inferior and carry a risk of hyperprogression in this subgroup (FLAURA trial; NCCN 2024). PD-L1-guided pembrolizumab applies only to driver-negative tumours. Crizotinib targets ALK/ROS1, not EGFR. Hook: 'Driver mutation always trumps PD-L1 — test NGS first.'","tp":"Lung cancer","s":"Medicine","qid":"p117","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 48-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis and tense ascites develops fever and abdominal pain. Diagnostic paracentesis shows ascitic fluid PMN count 350/mm³; culture is pending. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Observe and repeat paracentesis in 48 hours","IV cefotaxime 2 g 8-hourly plus IV albumin 1.5 g/kg on day 1","Oral ciprofloxacin 500 mg twice daily","IV metronidazole 500 mg 8-hourly alone"],"a":1,"ex":"Answer: IV cefotaxime plus albumin. Ascitic PMN ≥250/mm³ establishes spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and mandates empiric third-generation cephalosporin (cefotaxime/ceftriaxone). Adding IV albumin 1.5 g/kg on day 1 (1 g/kg on day 3) prevents hepatorenal syndrome and reduces mortality in high-risk patients (bilirubin >4 mg/dL, BUN >30, INR >1.5). Oral ciprofloxacin is used only for SBP prophylaxis (prior SBP, variceal bleeding). Observation is inappropriate — antibiotic delay worsens outcome. Metronidazole lacks gram-negative coverage, the main SBP pathogens (E. coli, Klebsiella).","tp":"Cirrhosis","s":"Medicine","qid":"p118","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve prosthesis is scheduled for an invasive dental extraction. Per current ESC 2023 guidelines, which is the appropriate infective endocarditis prophylaxis?","o":["Amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before the procedure","No antibiotic prophylaxis","IV clindamycin 600 mg administered 24 hours after the procedure","IV vancomycin with gentamicin in all such procedures"],"a":0,"ex":"Answer: Amoxicillin 2 g PO. ESC 2023 recommends IE prophylaxis before high-risk dental procedures for patients with prosthetic valves (including TAVR), previous IE, and high-risk congenital heart disease (unrepaired/palliated cyanotic CHD, or CHD repaired with prosthetic material) — mechanical aortic prosthesis qualifies. No prophylaxis applies to routine native-valve patients, mitral valve prolapse, or low-risk groups. Clindamycin 600 mg is the penicillin-allergic alternative given 30-60 min before, not 24 h after. Vancomycin is reserved for specific high-risk settings (penicillin allergy with oral intake impossible), not routine use. Hook: 'ESC 2023: prosthetic valve + dental procedure = amoxicillin 2 g.'","tp":"Infective endocarditis","s":"Medicine","qid":"p119","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p120","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Which of the following is a feature of Pap smear procedure? a.\t Lithotomy position b.\t Vaginal examination c.\t Speculum examination d.\t Sample taken using Ayre's spatula","o":["a, b, d","a, c, d","a, c","b, a, c"],"a":1,"ex":"A Pap smear (or Pap test) is a cervical cancer screening procedure. The process involves several key steps. First, the patient is placed in the (a) Lithotomy position (lying on her back with feet in stirrups) to allow for proper visualization and access to the cervix. Next, a (c) Speculum examination is performed. A speculum (e.g., Cusco's speculum) is inserted into the vagina and opened to visualize the cervix clearly. This step is essential and distinct from a bimanual vaginal examination (b), which is a tactile exam of the uterus and adnexa and is not the primary step for sample collection. Once the cervix is visualized, the sample is collected from the transformation zone (squamocolumnar junction) using a sampling device. The classic device for this is the (d) Ayre's spatula, which is used to scrape the ectocervix, often in combination with a cytobrush for the endocervix. Therefore, the correct features of the procedure are (a) Lithotomy position, (c) Speculum examination, and (d) Sample collection with an Ayre's spatula.","tp":"Airway Devices","s":"OBG","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a 30 pack-year smoking history and COPD has FEV1 45% predicted, mMRC grade 2, CAT score 16 and two moderate exacerbations in the past year. Blood eosinophil count is 380/µL. According to GOLD 2024, what is the most appropriate initial inhaler therapy?","o":["LAMA monotherapy (tiotropium)","LABA monotherapy (formoterol)","Triple therapy: LAMA + LABA + ICS","ICS monotherapy (budesonide)"],"a":2,"ex":"Answer: Triple therapy (LAMA+LABA+ICS). GOLD 2024 groups patients by exacerbation risk: this man is group E (≥2 moderate exacerbations) — initial therapy is LAMA+LABA, and with blood eosinophils ≥300/µL an ICS is added (ICS indicated at ≥300; considered at 100-299). Hence LAMA+LABA+ICS. LAMA monotherapy suits group A/B. LABA alone and ICS alone are never appropriate in COPD (ICS monotherapy increases pneumonia risk). Blood eosinophil count is the key biomarker guiding ICS use/de-escalation. Hook: 'E-group + eos ≥300 → triple.'","tp":"COPD","s":"Medicine","qid":"p121","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman at 12 weeks of gestation (first trimester) has known primary hypothyroidism on levothyroxine 75 mcg daily. TSH is 5.8 mIU/L, free T4 normal. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Increase levothyroxine dose and recheck TSH in 4 weeks","Continue the same dose and recheck TSH after 8 weeks","Stop levothyroxine during pregnancy","Add propylthiouracil 100 mg thrice daily"],"a":0,"ex":"Answer: Increase levothyroxine. Pregnancy raises LT4 requirement by ~30-50%, and ATA 2017 targets trimester-specific TSH goals (first 0.1-2.5; second 0.2-3.0; third 0.3-3.0) — TSH 5.8 is above goal, so the dose must be increased with TSH rechecked every 4 weeks until stable. Untreated hypothyroidism in pregnancy risks fetal neurocognitive impairment and pregnancy loss. Stopping LT4 is dangerous; PTU is for Graves' thyrotoxicosis, not hypothyroidism. Hook: 'Pregnancy: LT4 dose goes UP 30-50%, TSH goal trimester-specific.'","tp":"Thyroid disorders","s":"Medicine","qid":"p122","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old previously healthy man presents with 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain, fever (38.6°C), and a positive Murphy sign. WBC 15,000/µL, CRP 180 mg/L. Ultrasound shows a thick-walled (8 mm) gallbladder with multiple gallstones; the common bile duct is not dilated. He is hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Emergency laparoscopic cholecystectomy","Percutaneous cholecystostomy","Intravenous antibiotics followed by interval cholecystectomy at 6-8 weeks","Open cholecystectomy with routine intraoperative cholangiogram"],"a":0,"ex":"Emergency (early) laparoscopic cholecystectomy is the treatment of choice for Grade I acute cholecystitis per the Tokyo Guidelines 2018 — it is safe, shortens hospital stay, and prevents recurrence. Percutaneous cholecystostomy is reserved for unstable/high-surgical-risk (Grade II/III) patients as a temporizing measure. Routine interval cholecystectomy after antibiotic therapy is an outdated practice that is no longer recommended for fit patients. Open cholecystectomy is not preferred without specific indications, and routine cholangiography is unnecessary with a non-dilated CBD.","tp":"Acute cholecystitis","s":"Surgery","qid":"p123","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 19-year-old man presents with 24 hours of colicky abdominal pain that migrated to the right lower quadrant, fever 38.2°C, and tenderness at McBurney point. WBC 12,000/µL, CRP 28 mg/L. Ultrasound shows a non-compressible appendix 8 mm in diameter, with no fecolith. Which finding, if present, would make non-operative management with antibiotics more likely to fail?","o":["Age under 25 years","CRP of 28 mg/L","Appendix diameter of 8 mm on ultrasound","Presence of an appendicolith"],"a":3,"ex":"The CODA and APPAC trials (2020) showed non-operative antibiotic therapy is a reasonable option in uncomplicated appendicitis, but the presence of an appendicolith (fecolith) is the strongest predictor of failure of antibiotic therapy and late recurrence — such patients are best treated with appendicectomy. A mildly elevated CRP and 8 mm appendiceal diameter are typical of uncomplicated appendicitis and do not contraindicate non-operative care. Age alone is not a validated failure predictor.","tp":"Acute appendicitis","s":"Surgery","qid":"p124","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old man on regular NSAIDs for arthritis develops sudden, severe epigastric pain 3 hours ago. Abdomen is rigid and board-like with absent bowel sounds. Erect chest X-ray shows free air under the right hemidiaphragm. HR 120/min, BP 100/70 mm Hg. What is the best next step?","o":["Upper GI endoscopy to confirm the site of perforation","CT-guided percutaneous drainage of intraperitoneal air","Emergency laparotomy with omental (Graham) patch repair","Keep NPO, start NG suction and IV PPI, observe for 48 hours"],"a":2,"ex":"Clinical peritonitis with pneumoperitoneium in an NSAID user indicates a perforated peptic ulcer; urgent laparotomy (open or laparoscopic) with omental patch closure is the definitive management. Endoscopy is contraindicated in perforation because air insufflation enlarges the leak. Percutaneous drainage of free air is not a recognized therapy. Selective non-operative management is reserved for a few stable patients with contained perforations confirmed on water-soluble contrast study — not a rigid patient with generalized peritonitis.","tp":"Perforated peptic ulcer","s":"Surgery","qid":"p125","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has painless progressive jaundice for 3 weeks with pale stools, dark urine, and 4 kg weight loss. Gallbladder is palpable and nontender (Courvoisier sign). USG: CBD dilated to 14 mm with a distal stricture. Pancreatic-protocol CT shows a 3.2 cm hypodense pancreatic head mass with tumor abutment of the superior mesenteric vein involving less than 180° of its circumference; no distant metastases. EUS-FNA confirms adenocarcinoma. CA 19-9 is 420 U/mL. What is the best management?","o":["Upfront pancreaticoduodenectomy (Whipple procedure)","Palliative biliary stenting with best supportive care","Neoadjuvant chemotherapy (e.g., FOLFIRINOX) followed by reassessment for surgery","Definitive chemoradiotherapy without surgery"],"a":2,"ex":"Tumor-vein interface <180° (abutment) without unreconstructable encasement defines borderline resectable pancreatic cancer; per NCCN, these patients receive neoadjuvant chemotherapy (FOLFIRINOX-based) with restaging, and surgery is offered to those who remain resectable — this improves R0 resection rates. Upfront Whipple is for clearly resectable tumors. Palliative stenting alone is for metastatic/unresectable disease, and definitive chemoradiotherapy without surgery is not curative.","tp":"Pancreatic cancer","s":"Surgery","qid":"p126","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B) presents with two episodes of hematemesis, HR 110/min, BP 96/60 mm Hg, and mild icterus. An acute variceal bleed is suspected. Which of the following is recommended in ALL patients with suspected acute variceal hemorrhage?","o":["Immediate placement of a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube","Prophylactic intravenous antibiotics (e.g., ceftriaxone)","Prophylactic lactulose enemas","Elective TIPS before endoscopy"],"a":1,"ex":"Per Baveno VII/AASLD guidance, prophylactic antibiotics (ceftriaxone 1 g IV daily) are given to every cirrhotic patient with suspected variceal bleeding because they reduce bacterial infections, early rebleeding, and mortality. Standard care also includes vasoactive drugs (terlipressin/octreotide) and endoscopic band ligation after resuscitation. Balloon tamponade is a rescue measure for uncontrolled bleeding, not routine first-line therapy. TIPS is reserved for refractory/rebleeding cases and is not placed electively before endoscopy.","tp":"Variceal bleeding","s":"Surgery","qid":"p127","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has had progressive erythema, warmth, and enlargement of the right breast with peau d'orange skin for 4 weeks, without fever or a discrete lump, and without improvement despite two courses of oral antibiotics for presumed mastitis. Ultrasound shows skin thickening with no abscess. What is the best next step?","o":["Continue antibiotics for another 2 weeks and review","Core needle biopsy of the breast skin and parenchyma","Upfront modified radical mastectomy","Reassurance with repeat ultrasound in 3 months"],"a":1,"ex":"The picture suggests inflammatory breast cancer (IBC), an aggressive form usually presenting without a lump and without systemic sepsis; the correct first step is tissue diagnosis by core needle biopsy, looking for dermal lymphatic invasion. Antibiotics are stopped once IBC is suspected because mastitis unresponsive to treatment is the classic mimic. Surgery is NOT the initial treatment in IBC — neoadjuvant chemotherapy is given first, followed by mastectomy and radiation. Reassurance and observation would be dangerously delayed management.","tp":"Breast cancer","s":"Surgery","qid":"p128","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a 1.2 cm solitary left thyroid nodule. USG shows a TIRADS 4 nodule; FNA cytology is Bethesda VI (papillary thyroid carcinoma). There is no extrathyroidal extension and no cervical lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is an acceptable initial surgical management per current ATA guidelines?","o":["Total thyroidectomy with prophylactic bilateral central neck dissection in all cases","Radioactive iodine ablation as primary therapy","Percutaneous ethanol injection of the nodule","Thyroid lobectomy (hemithyroidectomy) alone"],"a":3,"ex":"ATA 2015/2024 guidance permits thyroid lobectomy alone as initial surgery for intrathyroidal papillary carcinoma measuring 1-4 cm without nodal metastases or adverse features, as recurrence and survival outcomes are comparable to total thyroidectomy with less morbidity (hypoparathyroidism, RLN injury). Prophylactic central neck dissection is not mandatory. Radioiodine is used for ablation after surgery, not as primary therapy, and ethanol injection is for benign cystic nodules or recurrent disease in special situations.","tp":"Thyroid carcinoma","s":"Surgery","qid":"p129","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p130","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"What is the association between a filling defect and the condition observed in the MRCP image?","o":["Stricture","Choledocholithiasis","Cholecystitis","Stones"],"a":1,"ex":"The image provided is from a Magnetic Resonance Cholangiopancreatography (MRCP), a non-invasive imaging technique that visualizes the biliary and pancreatic ducts. The image clearly shows the common bile duct (CBD), which appears dilated. Within the distal portion of this dilated CBD, there is a distinct, dark, ovoid signal void, which represents a filling defect. In this clinical and anatomical context, such a filling defect is the characteristic appearance of a stone located within the common bile duct. This condition is known as choledocholithiasis. The presence of this stone is causing an obstruction, which has led to the proximal dilation of the bile duct. A stricture (A) would appear as a smooth or irregular narrowing of the duct, not a discrete intraluminal defect. Cholecystitis (C) is inflammation of the gallbladder, which would show different findings.","tp":"Semen Stains","s":"Dermatology","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with atrial fibrillation (not on anticoagulation) develops sudden left leg pain 4 hours ago. The leg is pale and cold below the knee with absent femoral, popliteal, and pedal pulses; sensation is reduced over the foot with mild motor weakness. What is the best management?","o":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis alone","IV heparin with urgent Fogarty embolectomy","Oral anticoagulation with elective revascularization in one week","Immediate above-knee amputation"],"a":1,"ex":"Acute embolic limb ischemia (AF with sudden occlusion, viable limb at 4 hours) is managed with immediate systemic heparin followed by surgical embolectomy via the Fogarty catheter — the 4-6 hour golden window preserves the limb. Catheter-directed thrombolysis is preferred for acute thrombotic (in-situ) occlusion rather than embolism. Delaying revascularization risks irreversible ischemia, and amputation is reserved for limbs with irreversible damage (rigor, fixed mottling, complete anesthesia).","tp":"Acute limb ischemia","s":"Surgery","qid":"p131","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 71-year-old male smoker is found on screening ultrasound to have an asymptomatic 5.8 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm. He has good functional status. What is the best management?","o":["Repeat ultrasound in 12 months","Defer repair until the aneurysm reaches 6.5 cm","Elective repair (EVAR or open surgery)","Endovascular coiling of the aneurysm sac"],"a":2,"ex":"The repair threshold for an asymptomatic infrarenal AAA is 5.5 cm (or rapid expansion >1 cm/year or symptoms); at 5.8 cm, elective repair is indicated and is safe in a patient with good functional status. Surveillance alone is appropriate below 5.5 cm. There is no evidence to wait until 6.5 cm. Endovascular coiling has no role in AAA — EVAR (endograft) or open repair are the options.","tp":"Abdominal aortic aneurysm","s":"Surgery","qid":"p132","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 25-year-old man sustains a closed midshaft tibial fracture in a road traffic accident and is placed in a cast. Eight hours later he has escalating pain despite analgesia; the leg is tense and passive toe extension causes severe pain, though distal pulses are palpable. What is the best next step?","o":["Remove the cast and observe for 24 hours","Perform fasciotomy only when distal pulses disappear","MRI of the leg before any intervention","Immediate four-compartment fasciotomy of the leg"],"a":3,"ex":"Pain out of proportion, tense compartments, and pain on passive stretch are early signs of acute compartment syndrome; distal pulses remain present until very late because compartment pressure rarely exceeds arterial pressure, so waiting for pulselessness delays diagnosis. Definitive treatment is emergency four-compartment fasciotomy (confirmed by pressure >30 mm Hg or delta pressure <30 mm Hg if measured). Cast removal alone is inadequate, and MRI wastes critical time.","tp":"Compartment syndrome","s":"Surgery","qid":"p133","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a 1.2 mm Breslow-thickness melanoma (T2a) on his back that was removed by excisional biopsy with a 2 mm margin. Clinical examination shows no palpable regional lymph nodes. What is the next appropriate surgical step?","o":["Wide local excision with a 1-2 cm margin and sentinel lymph node biopsy","Wide local excision alone with clinical follow-up of nodes","Elective ipsilateral completion lymphadenectomy","Mohs micrographic surgery alone"],"a":0,"ex":"For melanoma ≥1 mm Breslow thickness (and T1b with adverse features), sentinel lymph node biopsy is indicated for staging, and wide local excision with margins of 1-2 cm (for 1-2 mm depth) completes the primary treatment. Omitting SLNB loses the most powerful prognostic information, while elective (prophylactic) lymphadenectomy without a positive sentinel node causes unnecessary morbidity. Completion lymphadenectomy is performed only when the sentinel node is positive. Mohs surgery does not address depth-related node staging.","tp":"Melanoma","s":"Surgery","qid":"p134","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has intermittent purulent perianal discharge for 3 months with no pain. The external opening is 3 cm from the anal verge. Examination under anesthesia reveals a fistula track traversing the upper half of the external anal sphincter (high trans-sphincteric fistula). What is the best management?","o":["Lay open the entire track (fistulotomy)","Seton placement","Fistulectomy with immediate primary sphincter repair in the same sitting","Antibiotics and sitz baths alone"],"a":1,"ex":"High trans-sphincteric fistulas involve a large portion of the external sphincter, so laying open the track (fistulotomy) risks major fecal incontinence; a seton (loose or staged cutting) preserves continence while controlling sepsis and allowing gradual division of the sphincter. Fistulotomy is safe for low (intersphincteric or low trans-sphincteric) fistulas. Primary sphincter repair is used in selected anterior fistulas in women, not routinely here, and antibiotics alone do not cure a chronic fistula.","tp":"Anal fistula","s":"Surgery","qid":"p135","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 70 kg man sustains flame burns involving 40% total body surface area (mixed partial and full thickness). Using the Parkland formula, what volume of Ringer lactate should he receive, and in what time frame?","o":["5600 mL in the first 8 hours","2800 mL in the first 8 hours","11200 mL in the first 8 hours","11200 mL over 24 hours, with half given in the first 16 hours"],"a":0,"ex":"Parkland formula: 4 mL × body weight (kg) × %TBSA = 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL crystalloid over 24 hours; the first half (5600 mL) is given in the first 8 hours from the time of burn (not arrival), and the remainder over the next 16 hours. Giving less risks under-resuscitation and acute kidney injury; giving the entire volume in 8 hours risks fluid overload and compartment syndromes. Ringer lactate is the fluid of choice.","tp":"Burns","s":"Surgery","qid":"p136","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman (G2P1) presents at 6 weeks of amenorrhea with 4 days of painless spotting. Serum beta-hCG is 3,480 mIU/mL. Transvaginal sonography shows no intrauterine gestational sac, a 2.8 cm echogenic right adnexal mass, no free fluid in the pouch of Douglas, and no fetal cardiac activity. She is hemodynamically stable. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Expectant management with serial beta-hCG monitoring","Single-dose methotrexate 50 mg/m² intramuscularly","Laparoscopic right salpingostomy","Repeat transvaginal ultrasound after one week"],"a":1,"ex":"An hCG >2,500–3,500 mIU/mL with no intrauterine sac on good-quality TVS indicates a nonviable or ectopic pregnancy; the adnexal mass confirms ectopic. Single-dose MTX is appropriate as she is stable, unruptured, sac <3.5 cm, no fetal cardiac activity, and hCG <5,000. Expectant management requires a low and falling hCG (<1,500–2,000), while salpingostomy is reserved for medical failure or surgical preference. Delaying by repeating the scan risks tubal rupture.","tp":"Ectopic pregnancy","s":"OBG","qid":"p137","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old primigravida at 32 weeks presents with headache-free hypertension: BP 148/96 mmHg on two readings 6 hours apart, urine protein +2 on dipstick, platelets 1,80,000/mm³, normal liver enzymes, and a reassuring non-stress test. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Immediate cesarean delivery at 32 weeks","Induction of labor at 34 weeks","Outpatient follow-up with weekly BP monitoring until 40 weeks","Admission for close fetomaternal surveillance with planned delivery at 37 weeks"],"a":3,"ex":"This is preeclampsia without severe features (no neurological symptoms, normal platelets/LFTs) — managed expectantly with admission, serial BP, urine protein, and fetal surveillance until 37 weeks, when delivery is planned. Immediate delivery is reserved for severe features, eclampsia, or fetal compromise; induction at 34 weeks is not indicated, and outpatient care is unsafe. Magnesium sulfate and antihypertensives are added only when severe features develop.","tp":"Preeclampsia","s":"OBG","qid":"p138","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a spontaneous vaginal delivery and develops uterine atony with blood loss of 1,200 mL. She has received uterine massage, oxytocin infusion, ergometrine, carboprost, and tranexamic acid, yet bleeding continues at 200 mL per 10 minutes with a persistently boggy uterus. What is the next best step?","o":["Intrauterine balloon tamponade (Bakri/condom catheter)","Uterine compression sutures (B-Lynch) via laparotomy","Emergency hysterectomy","Repeat intramuscular carboprost"],"a":0,"ex":"The PPH cascade is: medical (uterotonics + tranexamic acid) → mechanical (intrauterine balloon tamponade) → surgical (compression sutures, arterial ligation, hysterectomy). After maximum medical therapy fails in an atonic uterus, the balloon is the correct next step, being minimally invasive and highly effective. Compression sutures and hysterectomy are second/third-line rescue, and repeating carboprost has already been covered by the maximum uterotonic regimen.","tp":"Postpartum hemorrhage","s":"OBG","qid":"p139","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p140","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"What is the appropriate initial management for a 9-month-old infant who has a respiratory rate of 48 breaths per minute but shows no chest retractions or other symptoms?","o":["No treatment needed","Send back home after starting oral antibiotics","Hospitalize and start oral antibiotics","Hospitalize and start IV antibiotics"],"a":0,"ex":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines for the Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI), the diagnosis of pneumonia in infants aged 2 to 12 months is based on the presence of fast breathing or chest in drawing. For this age group, fast breathing is defined as a respiratory rate of 50 breaths per minute or more. This infant's respiratory rate is 48 breaths per minute, which is within the normal range. Furthermore, the infant shows no danger signs like chest retractions, grunting, lethargy, or inability to feed. In the absence of both fast breathing (tachypnea) and any danger signs, the child does not meet the criteria for pneumonia. The condition is likely a simple cough or cold that does not require antibiotic treatment. Therefore, the most appropriate management is to reassure the parents and advise on supportive care at home, with no specific medical treatment needed.","tp":"Vaccines","s":"Surgery","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman (BMI 30) with oligomenorrhea since menarche, hirsutism (Ferriman–Gallwey score 12), and bilateral polycystic ovaries on ultrasound has been trying to conceive for 2 years. Her partner's semen analysis is normal and she is confirmed anovulatory. What is the most appropriate first-line ovulation induction agent?","o":["Metformin 1,500 mg/day","Letrozole 2.5–5 mg on days 3–7 of the cycle","Clomiphene citrate 50–100 mg for 5 days","Laparoscopic ovarian drilling"],"a":1,"ex":"Letrozole is now the preferred first-line ovulation induction in PCOS (PALM trial and current ASRM/ESHRE guidance show higher live birth rates and lower multiple pregnancy than clomiphene). Clomiphene is second-line, metformin is used only for impaired glucose tolerance/metabolic correction, and ovarian drilling is reserved for clomiphene-resistant anovulation in lean women.","tp":"PCOS","s":"OBG","qid":"p141","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with bilateral ovarian endometriomas (right 4.8 cm, left 3.1 cm) and severe dysmenorrhea has been trying to conceive for 1 year with no other infertility factor identified. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Laparoscopic excision of the endometriomas followed by fertility treatment","Immediate in vitro fertilization without prior surgery","GnRH agonist for 6 months followed by spontaneous conception attempt","Ultrasound-guided aspiration of the cyst"],"a":0,"ex":"For endometriomas ≥4 cm, ESHRE guidance recommends laparoscopic cystectomy before ART — it improves access to follicles and reduces infection risk at retrieval, while awaiting spontaneous conception is reasonable post-surgery. IVF without surgery is an option for smaller cysts; GnRH agonist suppresses but does not remove the cyst, and aspiration has high recurrence with infection risk and is not recommended.","tp":"Endometriosis","s":"OBG","qid":"p142","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with a last menstrual period 14 months ago complains of 8–10 hot flushes daily with night sweats disturbing her sleep. She has no history of venous thromboembolism, breast cancer, or liver disease. She is normotensive and has a body mass index of 24. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Vaginal estrogen cream alone","Escitalopram 10 mg daily","Black cohosh with lifestyle measures only","Systemic combined estrogen-progestogen therapy"],"a":3,"ex":"A healthy woman <60 years (and within 10 years of menopause) with bothersome vasomotor symptoms and an intact uterus is an ideal candidate for systemic combined MHT — the 'window of opportunity' where cardiovascular benefits outweigh risks. Vaginal estrogen treats genitourinary symptoms only, SSRIs are second-line when MHT is declined or contraindicated, and herbal remedies lack evidence. Progestogen is added to protect the endometrium.","tp":"Menopause and HRT","s":"OBG","qid":"p143","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman (G2P1) is diagnosed with gestational diabetes at 26 weeks by 75 g OGTT (fasting 98 mg/dL, 1-hour 182 mg/dL, 2-hour 150 mg/dL). After 2 weeks of medical nutrition therapy and exercise, her fasting capillary glucose remains 108–115 mg/dL on multiple readings. Per ACOG guidance, what is the first-line pharmacotherapy?","o":["Continue medical nutrition therapy alone","Metformin 500 mg twice daily","Subcutaneous insulin","Glyburide 5 mg daily"],"a":2,"ex":"With fasting glucose persistently above the target of <95 mg/dL despite medical nutrition therapy, pharmacotherapy is required. ACOG recommends insulin as first-line because it does not cross the placenta; metformin is an acceptable alternative in some settings but is considered second-line, and glyburide is not recommended due to transplacental transfer and increased macrosomia risk.","tp":"Gestational diabetes","s":"OBG","qid":"p144","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old primigravida at 31 weeks presents with regular uterine contractions (4 in 10 minutes), cervical dilatation of 3 cm with 80% effacement, intact membranes, no bleeding, and a reactive fetal heart rate. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Nifedipine tocolysis plus betamethasone plus magnesium sulfate","Betamethasone alone without tocolysis","Immediate cesarean delivery","Nifedipine and betamethasone but no magnesium sulfate"],"a":0,"ex":"At 31 weeks with threatened preterm labor, the trio is: tocolysis (nifedipine or atosiban) for up to 48 hours to allow corticosteroid effect (betamethasone 12 mg ×2, indicated <34 weeks), plus magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection (indicated <32 weeks). Tocolysis without steroids serves no purpose, immediate delivery forfeits steroid benefit, and omitting MgSO4 loses proven cerebral-palsy reduction at <32 weeks.","tp":"Preterm labor","s":"OBG","qid":"p145","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes routine cervical cancer screening: the HPV DNA test is positive for HPV 16 and her cervical cytology is negative (NILM). Per current ASCCP 2019 risk-based guidelines, what is the appropriate next step?","o":["Repeat co-testing in 1 year","Colposcopy","Repeat HPV DNA test in 5 years","Immediate loop electrosurgical excision procedure"],"a":1,"ex":"HPV 16/18 genotypes carry high risk (CIN 3+ risk >4%, i.e., colposcopy threshold), so a positive result warrants immediate colposcopy regardless of cytology. Repeat testing in 1 year applies to positive non-16/18 genotypes with negative cytology (risk 1-year threshold), and 5-year repeat is for negative screening. LEEP requires a confirmed lesion at colposcopy, not a screening result.","tp":"Cervical cancer screening","s":"OBG","qid":"p146","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old postmenopausal woman presents with 3 months of abdominal distension. Transvaginal sonography shows a complex left adnexal mass of 9 cm with solid areas and ascites. Serum CA-125 is 420 U/mL. She has no significant comorbidities. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Neoadjuvant chemotherapy with interval debulking","Serial ultrasound surveillance every 3 months","Staging laparotomy with surgical cytoreduction","Laparoscopic ovarian cystectomy"],"a":2,"ex":"The high RMI (postmenopausal + CA-125 >200 + complex mass with solid areas) strongly suggests ovarian malignancy; primary surgical staging/cytoreduction via laparotomy is the definitive approach and is also therapeutic. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy is reserved for stage IV disease, unresectable disease, or poor performance status; surveillance and cystectomy are inappropriate when malignancy is suspected.","tp":"Ovarian cancer","s":"OBG","qid":"p147","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with two episodes of postmenopausal bleeding. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an endometrial thickness of 9 mm (double-layer measurement). What is the most appropriate next investigation?","o":["Repeat transvaginal ultrasound after 6 months","Oral progestogen for 3 months with reassessment","CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis","Endometrial sampling"],"a":3,"ex":"Postmenopausal bleeding with endometrial thickness ≥4–5 mm warrants histological sampling (office endometrial biopsy, or hysteroscopy-guided curettage) to exclude endometrial carcinoma. Repeat imaging only delays diagnosis, progestogen is therapeutic not diagnostic, and CT cannot rule out endometrial cancer. Endometrial thickness <4 mm with adequate visualization allows conservative follow-up.","tp":"Postmenopausal bleeding","s":"OBG","qid":"p148","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman at 26 weeks is confirmed to have monochorionic–monoamniotic twins with no structural anomalies and no growth discordance. Serial surveillance is initiated. What is the recommended timing and route of delivery?","o":["Cesarean delivery at 37 weeks","Planned cesarean delivery at 32–34 weeks","Vaginal delivery at 39 weeks","Serial amniodrainage from 26 weeks onward"],"a":1,"ex":"Monoamniotic twins have a high risk of cord entanglement and acute demise, so current guidelines (ACOG) recommend planned cesarean delivery at 32–34 weeks after antenatal surveillance (NST/BPP), with earlier delivery if complications arise. Waiting to 37 weeks risks in-utero death from cord entanglement, vaginal delivery is not recommended in monoamniotic twins, and amniodrainage treats twin–twin transfusion syndrome, which is not present here (no growth discordance or hemodynamic signs; TTTS can occur even in monoamniotic twins, diagnosis is just harder in a shared sac).","tp":"Multiple pregnancy","s":"OBG","qid":"p149","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p150","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A 10-year-old boy presents with symptoms including fever, joint pain, and a notable skin condition on his hand, as shown in the provided image. Based on the clinical findings, what is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Gottron's papules - Juvenile dermatomyositis","Rheumatoid nodules - Juvenile rheumatic arthritis","Sclerodactyly - Systemic sclerosis","Mechanic's hand-Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"a":0,"ex":"The image displays erythematous to violaceous papules located over the extensor surfaces of the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and interphalangeal (IP) joints of the hand. This specific skin finding is known as Gottron's papules, which are considered pathognomonic for dermatomyositis. When this condition occurs in a child, it is termed juvenile dermatomyositis (JDM). JDM is an autoimmune inflammatory myopathy that affects the skin and muscles. In addition to the characteristic skin rashes (which also include a heliotrope rash on the eyelids), patients present with systemic symptoms like fever and joint pain, as well as the hallmark feature of progressive, symmetrical proximal muscle weakness. Rheumatoid nodules (B) are subcutaneous and firm. Sclerodactyly (C) involves skin tightening. Mechanic's hands are hyperkeratotic fissures on the fingers.","tp":"Perinatal Transmission","s":"Paediatrics","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 3-month-old infant has 2 days of coryza, cough and laboured breathing. Temperature 37.9°C, respiratory rate 62/min, SpO2 93% on room air, mild subcostal retractions, and bilateral fine crackles with wheeze. The baby feeds well and passes urine normally; RSV antigen test is positive. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Nebulised salbutamol every 6 hours with home observation","Nebulised adrenaline and oral prednisolone for 5 days","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for 7 days","Supportive care: nasal suction, small frequent feeds and monitoring, without routine bronchodilators or steroids"],"a":3,"ex":"Typical viral bronchiolitis is self-limited and is managed with supportive care — nasal suction, feeding support and monitoring; oxygen is started when SpO2 falls below 90% (93% here does not qualify). Meta-analyses show no benefit from bronchodilators or systemic corticosteroids in typical bronchiolitis, so neither is routine. Antibiotics are reserved for proven bacterial co-infection or complications. Memory hook: 'no routine' in bronchiolitis — bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, chest physiotherapy and ribavirin.","tp":"Bronchiolitis","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p151","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy has had fever for 7 days (max 39.8°C) with bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection, cracked lips and strawberry tongue, a polymorphous truncal rash, a right-sided 1.5 cm cervical lymph node, and oedema of the hands and feet. CRP 12 mg/dL, ESR 70 mm/h. Echocardiography shows normal coronary arteries. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","o":["IVIG 2 g/kg as a single dose plus aspirin 80-100 mg/kg/day","Low-dose aspirin 3-5 mg/kg/day alone","Oral prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks","IV infliximab 5 mg/kg as a single dose"],"a":0,"ex":"Fever ≥5 days plus 4 of 5 mucocutaneous criteria (conjunctival injection, oral changes, rash, cervical lymphadenopathy, extremity changes) is complete Kawasaki disease; treatment must start within 10 days of fever onset (ideally before day 7). Standard first-line therapy is IVIG 2 g/kg as a single dose with high-dose aspirin (80-100 mg/kg/day) during the acute phase; low-dose aspirin is convalescent-phase antiplatelet therapy, not acute treatment. Steroids and infliximab are reserved for IVIG-refractory disease (persistent fever ≥36 h after IVIG). Normal coronary arteries on initial echo do not alter treatment.","tp":"Kawasaki disease","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p152","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy (BMI 24 kg/m², >95th percentile) has BP of 128/86 mmHg on three separate occasions using an appropriately sized cuff on the right arm. His 95th percentile BP for age, sex and height is 118/76 mmHg. He is asymptomatic with normal urine and renal function. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Start amlodipine 2.5 mg once daily immediately","Refer for 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring before any intervention","Lifestyle modification (weight reduction, diet, exercise) with re-evaluation in 3-6 months","Start enalapril 0.2 mg/kg/day and refer to paediatric nephrology"],"a":2,"ex":"This is stage 1 hypertension (BP ≥95th to <95th percentile + 12 mmHg (2017 AAP; stage 2 begins at 95th + 12 mmHg) — both systolic and diastolic exceed the 95th percentile of 118/76). Per the 2017 AAP guideline, asymptomatic stage 1 hypertension without target-organ damage or comorbidity is managed with lifestyle modification (weight loss, dietary change, exercise) with reassessment in 3-6 months; pharmacotherapy is added if it persists beyond 6 months, or immediately in stage 2 hypertension, symptomatic patients, CKD, diabetes or target-organ damage. ABPM is for suspected white-coat or masked hypertension, not first-line evaluation here. Memory hook: 'stage 1, asymptomatic → lifestyle 3-6 months before drugs; ACE inhibitor/ARB first-line in children.'","tp":"Paediatric hypertension","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p153","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has 2 weeks of polyuria, polydipsia and 3 kg weight loss, and is now drowsy with Kussmaul breathing. Capillary glucose 480 mg/dL; pH 7.05, bicarbonate 8 mEq/L, serum sodium 132 mEq/L (corrected 139), potassium 5.2 mEq/L. After a 10 mL/kg bolus of 0.9% saline, what is the most appropriate immediate next step per ISPAD 2022/2024 guidelines?","o":["Give sodium bicarbonate 1-2 mEq/kg to correct the acidosis","Start IV regular insulin at 0.05-0.1 U/kg/h about 1 hour after starting fluids, without an insulin bolus","Give an IV insulin bolus of 0.1 U/kg followed by an infusion","Give a 25% dextrose bolus to prevent hypoglycaemia"],"a":1,"ex":"ISPAD recommends initial fluid therapy first (10 mL/kg bolus only if shock is present, otherwise deficit replacement over 48 h) and starting IV insulin at 0.05-0.1 U/kg/h approximately 1 hour after fluids begin — an insulin bolus is not recommended. Bicarbonate is not given when pH ≥6.9 because it risks hypokalaemia and paradoxical CSF acidosis; potassium is added now — serum K is already below 5.5 mmol/L — once urine output is confirmed (ISPAD 2022/2024). Memory hook: 'fluids first, insulin later, potassium always, bicarbonate almost never (only if pH <6.9).'","tp":"Diabetic ketoacidosis","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p154","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"Regarding RSV prophylaxis and prevention, which statement reflects the current (2023-2025) international recommendation (AAP/CDC)?","o":["Palivizumab is recommended for all healthy term infants entering their first RSV season","RSV vaccination is recommended for all infants at 6 weeks of age","Maternal RSV vaccination during pregnancy is contraindicated","A single dose of nirsevimab is recommended for all infants younger than 8 months entering their first RSV season"],"a":3,"ex":"Nirsevimab, a long-acting monoclonal antibody, is recommended as a single intramuscular dose for all infants <8 months of age entering their first RSV season; infants 8-19 months with risk conditions receive a second-season dose. Palivizumab is limited to high-risk infants (≤29-week prematurity, chronic lung disease, haemodynamically significant congenital heart disease). Maternal RSVpreF vaccine given at 32-36 weeks gestation is recommended in some countries to protect the newborn — it is not contraindicated — and no RSV vaccine is licensed for infants. In India, palivizumab remains the option for high-risk neonates while nirsevimab is the 2023-2025 global standard.","tp":"RSV prophylaxis","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p155","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy with fever and vomiting for 2 days is lethargic and mottled, with capillary refill 4 s, heart rate 170/min, BP 78/48 mmHg, and SpO2 96% on room air. WBC 21,000/mm³, CRP 18 mg/dL. He has received 40 mL/kg normal saline. Per the 2024 Surviving Sepsis Campaign paediatric guidelines, what is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Give further 20-40 mL/kg fluid boluses until blood pressure normalises","Start an epinephrine infusion and reassess within 15-30 minutes","Administer hydrocortisone 2 mg/kg as the next step","Obtain a CT head before any further treatment"],"a":1,"ex":"SSC 2024: after 40 mL/kg of balanced crystalloid (in 10-20 mL/kg boluses), a child with persistent shock should receive a vasoactive infusion within 15-30 minutes — epinephrine for cold shock (mottling, prolonged capillary refill) or norepinephrine for warm shock; dopamine is no longer recommended. Continued fluid beyond 40-60 mL/kg without ongoing losses increases fluid overload and mortality. Hydrocortisone is reserved for catecholamine-refractory shock with adrenal insufficiency risk, and imaging must never delay resuscitation. Memory hook: '40 mL/kg → pressor; epinephrine for cold, norepinephrine for warm.'","tp":"Septic shock","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p156","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl has had fever for 4 days and is now afebrile. Platelets 45,000/mm³, haematocrit 46% (baseline 36%); she has persistent abdominal pain and one episode of haematemesis. Dengue NS1 antigen is positive. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Discharge home with paracetamol and advice to return if bleeding occurs","Admit and start isotonic crystalloid at 5-7 mL/kg/h with close monitoring","Start oral doxycycline for possible scrub typhus","Transfuse 1 unit of random-donor platelets because the count is below 50,000"],"a":1,"ex":"This is dengue with warning signs — rising haematocrit with rapid platelet fall, persistent abdominal pain and mucosal bleeding — which per the WHO dengue guidelines (2011 classification, retained in the 2023 update and NVBDCP) mandates hospital admission and isotonic crystalloid at 5-7 mL/kg/h with close monitoring for progression to shock. Platelet transfusion is NOT given on a count-based threshold; it is reserved for significant bleeding or refractory shock, and prophylactic transfusion is actively discouraged. Antibiotics are not indicated without a suspected bacterial co-infection. Memory hook: 'warning signs = admit + crystalloid; count-based platelet transfusion is a classic misconception.'","tp":"Dengue fever","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p157","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has periorbital and pedal oedema with frothy urine. Urine albumin 3+, serum albumin 1.9 g/dL, cholesterol 320 mg/dL, normal complement, BP 90/60 mmHg. Prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day is started. After 4 weeks of daily prednisolone, urine albumin is still 3+ and oedema persists. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Continue prednisolone 2 mg/kg/day for a further 4 weeks","Add oral cyclophosphamide pulses to prednisolone","Diagnose steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome and plan renal biopsy with a calcineurin inhibitor","Switch to alternate-day prednisolone and reassess after 6 months"],"a":2,"ex":"Failure to achieve remission (urine albumin negative or trace for 3 consecutive days) after 4 weeks of daily prednisolone defines steroid-resistant nephrotic syndrome. The next step is renal biopsy — minimal change disease is still possible (about 20-25%) but focal segmental glomerulosclerosis becomes likely — with introduction of a calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus or cyclosporine) plus ACE inhibition. Continuing the same dose or switching to alternate-day dosing does not address resistance; cyclophosphamide is used in steroid-dependent/frequent-relapse disease, not as first-line treatment for steroid resistance. Memory hook: '4 weeks of daily prednisolone without remission = resistant → biopsy + calcineurin inhibitor.'","tp":"Nephrotic syndrome","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p158","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 4-month-old breastfed girl has had loose stools with blood streaks for 3 weeks, perianal erythema and facial eczema. She is afebrile, thriving, with normal weight gain. Stool microscopy shows no pus cells, ova or cysts. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Advise the mother to eliminate cow's milk and dairy products from her own diet","Start oral metronidazole for 7 days","Switch the infant to a soy-based formula","Refer for colonoscopy under general anaesthesia"],"a":0,"ex":"A thriving breastfed infant with blood-streaked stools and eczema is the classic picture of cow's milk protein allergy (CMPA). Per ESPGHAN, the first-line intervention in a breastfed infant is elimination of cow's milk protein from the mother's diet, with expected response in 2-4 weeks. Soy formula is not first-line under 6 months due to high cross-reactivity and phytoestrogen concerns; an amino-acid formula is used if symptoms persist on strict elimination. Metronidazole treats giardiasis/amoebiasis — no pathogens were seen — and colonoscopy is not indicated for suspected CMPA. Memory hook: 'breastfed baby with blood in stool = maternal dairy-free diet first.'","tp":"Cow's milk protein allergy","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p159","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p160","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"What is the role of the secretions produced by the region indicated by the arrow in the given histology image?","o":["Sexual function","Maintenance of glucose level","Electrolyte balance","Emergency response"],"a":2,"ex":"The provided histology image shows a cross-section of the adrenal gland. The arrow is pointing to the zona glomerulosa, the outermost layer of the adrenal cortex. This zone is responsible for synthesizing and secreting mineralocorticoids, with aldosterone being the most significant. The primary function of aldosterone is the regulation of blood pressure and electrolyte balance. It acts on the distal tubules and collecting ducts of the kidneys to increase the reabsorption of sodium and water, and to enhance the excretion of potassium. Sexual function (Option A) is primarily influenced by androgens secreted from the zona reticularis. Maintenance of glucose levels (Option B) is a key function of glucocorticoids, such as cortisol, which are secreted by the zona fasciculata. The emergency \"fight-or-flight\" response (Option D) is mediated by catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) secreted by the adrenal medulla, not the cortex. 2. Which type of joint is NOT associated with the bone illustrated in the diagram? (A)\tSyndesmosis (B)\t Symphysis (C)\tPlane synovial joint (D)\tSynchondrosis AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (D) Synchondrosis The bone shown in the image is a rib, identifiable by its elongated, curved shape and posterior articulation features. Ribs are associated with the following joint types: 1. Plane synovial joints (C) → At the costovertebral and costotransverse joints between the rib and vertebrae. 2. Syndesmosis (A) → The interosseous ligaments between rib and vertebrae contribute to fibrous union. 3. Symphysis (B) → Indirectly associated via the manubriosternal joint (sternal angle) and some costal cartilage interactions. However, a synchondrosis (D) is a primary cartilaginous joint where bones are united by hyaline cartilage, such as the epiphyseal plate or first sternocostal joint (sometimes classified differently). Ribs themselves do not form true synchondroses directly in typical articulation patterns, except in early developmental stages. Thus, among the options listed, the joint type not typically associated with the rib is : (D) Synchondrosis. 3. The spinal sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve sends its second-order neurons to transmit which type of sensation? (A)\tTouch (B)\t Pain (C)\tVibration (D)\tProprioception AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (B) Pain The sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve in the brainstem is divided into three parts. The principal (or chief) sensory nucleus processes discriminative touch and pressure. The mesencephalic nucleus handles proprioception from the muscles of mastication. The spinal sensory nucleus is primarily responsible for processing protopathic sensations, which include pain and temperature, as well as crude touch from the ipsilateral side of the face. First-order neurons from the trigeminal ganglion synapse in the spinal nucleus. The second-order neurons then decussate (cross the midline) and ascend in the ventral two-dimensionality tract to the contralateral thalamus (VPM nucleus). From there, third-order neurons project to the somatosensory cortex. Fine touch (Option A) is primarily handled by the principal nucleus, while vibration (Option C) and proprioception (Option D) are carried by other pathways. 4. The substantia nigra has functional connections with which component of the basal ganglia? (A)\tStriatum (B)\t Pallidum (C)\tSubthalamic nucleus (D)\tThalamus AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (A) Striatum The substantia nigra, located in the midbrain, is a crucial component of the basal ganglia motor circuitry. It is most famously and extensively connected to the striatum (which consists of the caudate nucleus and the putamen). This connection forms the nigrostriatal pathway, where dopaminergic neurons from the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) project to the striatum to modulate its activity. This pathway is essential for the initiation and smooth execution of voluntary movements, and its degeneration is the primary pathology in Parkinson's disease. While the substantia nigra pars reticulata receives input from the striatum and projects to the thalamus (Option D), its most prominent functional connection is its dopaminergic input to the striatum. The pallidum (Option B) and subthalamic nucleus (Option C) are other key nodes within the basal ganglia circuit but are not the primary connection site for the substantia nigra. 5. Which structure is NOT present in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle? (A)\tHippocampus (B)\t Tail of the caudate nucleus (C)\tFimbria (D)\tCollateral eminence AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (B) Tail of the caudate nucleus The inferior (temporal) horn of the lateral ventricle extends into the temporal lobe. Its floor is a complex surface formed by several structures. These include the hippocampus (Option A), a key structure for memory formation, which creates a prominent bulge in the floor. The fimbria of the hippocampus (Option C), a band of efferent fibers, runs along its medial edge. The most lateral part of the floor is the collateral eminence (Option D), a ridge formed by the underlying collateral sulcus. In contrast, the tail of the caudate nucleus is a component of the roof of the inferior horn. It arches over the thalamus and follows the curve of the lateral ventricle, terminating anteriorly at the amygdala. Therefore, it is not found in the floor of the inferior horn. 6. Which structures pass through the area indicated by the arrow? (A)\tGlossopharyngeal nerve and stylopharyngeus muscle (B)\t Superior laryngeal artery and internal branch of the superior laryngeal nerve Click Here to Buy book on Amazon 81=== page  79 INICET Examination Paper (C)\tRecurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior laryngeal artery (D)\tLingual artery and hypoglossal nerve AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (A) Glossopharyngeal nerve and stylopharyngeus muscle The image illustrates the posterior view of the pharynx, with the arrow pointing to the gap located between the superior and middle pharyngeal constrictor muscles. This anatomical interval serves as a passageway for specific neurovascular structures. The structures that traverse this gap are the stylopharyngeus muscle and the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which innervates it. The superior laryngeal artery and internal laryngeal nerve (Option B) pierce the thyrohyoid membrane above the superior constrictor. The recurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior laryngeal artery (Option C) pass deep to the inferior constrictor muscle to enter the larynx from below. The lingual artery and hypoglossal nerve (Option D) are located more anteriorly and superiorly in the submandibular region. 7. Cross section at which level? (A)\tT10 (B)\t T12 (C)\tT11 (D)\tT9 AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (A) T10 The axial cross-section image displays structures from both the lower thorax and upper abdomen, indicating a plane near the diaphragm. Key anatomical landmarks visible include the spleen on the left, the liver on the right, the fundus of the stomach containing air, and the aorta anterior to a thoracic vertebral body. The superior pole of the spleen is typically located at the level of the 9th to 11th ribs posteriorly. The structures shown are consistent with a slice passing through the dome of the diaphragm and the upper abdominal organs. This anatomical plane corresponds to the vertebral level of approximately T10. The T12 level (Option B) would be lower, showing more of the kidneys and the aortic hiatus. The T9 level (Option D) would be higher, typically showing more of the heart and less of the sub-diaphragmatic abdominal organs like the spleen and stomach fundus. 8. Which artery supplies the part of the heart marked with the arrow? (A)\tAnterior interventricular artery (B)\t Left circumflex artery (C)\tLeft marginal artery (D)\tRight coronary artery (RCA) AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (A) Anterior interventricular artery The image displays the anterior (sternocostal) surface of the heart, with the arrow pointing to the region of the anterior interventricular sulcus. This area, which encompasses the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the adjacent right ventricular wall, and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum, receives its blood supply from the anterior interventricular artery. This artery, also commonly known as the left anterior descending (LAD) artery, is one of the two main branches of the left coronary artery. The left circumflex artery (Option B) supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle, while the right coronary artery (Option D) supplies the right atrium, right ventricle, and the posterior aspect of the heart. The left marginal artery (Option C) is a branch of the circumflex artery. 9. Which of the following statements accurately describes the anatomy of the breast? (A)\tThe superior medial quadrant has more tissue (B)\t There are 10 lobules present (C)\tThe axillary tail of Spence crosses the anterior axillary fold (D)\tThe nipple is located at the level of the fourth intercostal space in most women AAns EXAMINATION PAPER MAY-2024 (C) The axillary tail of Spence crosses the anterior axillary fold The axillary tail of Spence is a superolateral extension of breast tissue from the upper outer quadrant of the breast into the axilla. It passes through an opening in the deep fascia known as the foramen of Langer. As it extends toward the axilla, it runs along and effectively crosses the region of the anterior axillary fold, which is formed by the lower border of the pectoralis major muscle. Clinically, this is important because breast tissue in the axillary tail can develop lumps or malignancy and may be mistaken for axillary lymph nodes. 1. Option (A) is incorrect because the upper outer quadrant, not the superior medial quadrant, contains the greatest amount of glandular tissue and is the most common site of breast carcinoma. 2. Option (B) is incorrect since the breast typically contains 15–20 lobes, each drained by a lactiferous duct. 3. Option (D) states that the nipple lies at the fourth intercostal space; although often used as a landmark, its position varies with age and body habitus. Therefore, the most accurate anatomical statement is option (C).","tp":"Alcohol Withdrawal","s":"Anatomy","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy with a febrile-triggered seizure has been in generalised convulsive status epilepticus for 12 minutes. IV access has been secured. What is the most appropriate first medication?","o":["IV levetiracetam 60 mg/kg","IV phenytoin 20 mg/kg","Rectal paracetamol 15 mg/kg","IV lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg"],"a":3,"ex":"First-line therapy for generalised convulsive status epilepticus is a benzodiazepine — IV lorazepam 0.1 mg/kg (or IV/IM midazolam). If seizures continue 5 minutes after the first-line drug, second-line therapy is given; per the 2022 IPNSS/NSET guidelines, IV levetiracetam 60 mg/kg is preferred in children over phenytoin/fosphenytoin for its better safety profile and lack of cardiac risk. Paracetamol treats fever, not seizures. Memory hook: 'benzo first, levetiracetam second, phenytoin third in children.'","tp":"Status epilepticus","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p161","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 3-week-old breastfed infant has poor feeding, constipation, prolonged jaundice and an umbilical hernia. Newborn screening at day 3 reported TSH 85 mIU/L with low free T4 (0.3 ng/dL). Skeletal radiograph shows delayed ossification of the distal femoral epiphysis. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Start oral levothyroxine 10-15 mcg/kg/day immediately","Repeat TSH and free T4 after 4 weeks before starting any therapy","Start liothyronine (T3) 5 mcg/kg/day","Perform a thyroid scan and uptake study before initiating treatment"],"a":0,"ex":"Clinical features plus a screening TSH >40 mIU/L with low free T4 confirm congenital hypothyroidism; treatment is levothyroxine 10-15 mcg/kg/day started immediately, because delay beyond 2 weeks of age worsens neurodevelopmental outcome. Repeating tests before treatment or waiting for a thyroid scan is unnecessary — a scan, if aetiology is desired, can be performed after starting therapy. T3 (liothyronine) monotherapy is not used for maintenance. Memory hook: 'TSH high + T4 low = treat now, scan later; LT4 10-15 mcg/kg/day.'","tp":"Congenital hypothyroidism","s":"Paediatrics","qid":"p162","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 72-year-old man, 2 days after surgery for fracture neck of femur, develops fluctuating consciousness over 24 hours. He is disoriented to time and place, cannot maintain attention, picks at his IV line, and reports seeing 'small insects' on the wall. He alternates between drowsiness and agitated restlessness. Serum sodium 126 mEq/L; urine shows pyuria. He is now severely agitated and at risk of pulling out his IV line. Which is the MOST appropriate management for his agitation?","o":["Haloperidol 0.5-1 mg orally/IM with repeated low-dose reassessment","Lorazepam 2 mg IV stat and 6-hourly","Physical restraints until the acute episode resolves","Rapid IV bolus of 3% saline to correct the hyponatremia"],"a":0,"ex":"This is postoperative delirium (positive features of inattention, fluctuating course, visual hallucinations) precipitated by UTI and mild hyponatremia. The priority is treating the cause and using non-pharmacological measures; antipsychotics in low doses (haloperidol 0.5-1 mg, or atypical agents) are reserved for severe agitation or psychosis, making A correct. Benzodiazepines (B) worsen delirium and are first-line only for alcohol or benzodiazepine-withdrawal delirium. Restraints (C) are a last resort that increase injury and agitation. Rapid saline correction (D) risks osmotic demyelination; mild hyponatremia must be corrected slowly. Memory hook: 'Delirium agitation = low-dose antipsychotic, not benzo.'","tp":"Delirium","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p163","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder discontinued lithium 3 weeks ago. For the past week she has been almost completely mute, sits motionless with her arm held in an awkward raised position (waxy flexibility), and actively resists attempts to move her limbs (negativism). She eats and drinks minimally. She is afebrile; HR 86/min, BP 118/72 mm Hg. An IV lorazepam challenge test produces dramatic but transient improvement. What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Start high-dose parenteral haloperidol for the psychosis","Start dantrolene and cooling measures","Proceed directly to bilateral ECT","Continue scheduled oral lorazepam and treat the underlying mood episode"],"a":3,"ex":"The picture is catatonia (Bush-Francis criteria: mutism, waxy flexibility, negativism, posturing). A positive lorazepam challenge (IV 1-2 mg with response within 30 minutes) is both diagnostic and therapeutic; the next step is scheduled oral lorazepam (up to 8-24 mg/day) along with treatment of the underlying bipolar episode, so D is correct. ECT is reserved for lorazepam failure or malignant catatonia (fever, autonomic instability, rigidity) - which is absent here, ruling out C and B (dantrolene is for NMS). Antipsychotics (A) can precipitate malignant catatonia in catatonic patients and must be avoided. Memory hook: 'Lorazepam first, ECT next.'","tp":"Catatonia","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p164","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder, maintained on lithium 900 mg/day (serum level 0.8 mmol/L), presents with a 4-week depressive episode: low mood, anhedonia, hypersomnia, 3 kg weight gain, and poor concentration (PHQ-9 score 22). No rapid cycling or psychotic features; she denies suicidal ideation. Which is the MOST appropriate treatment to add now?","o":["Fluoxetine 20 mg/day","Methylphenidate 10 mg twice daily","Quetiapine, starting 50 mg and titrating toward 300 mg at bedtime","Lamotrigine with rapid titration to 200 mg within 10 days"],"a":2,"ex":"Quetiapine is a first-line agent for acute bipolar depression (along with lurasidone and cariprazine), and is correct here. Antidepressant monotherapy such as fluoxetine (A) can induce a switch to mania or rapid cycling and has modest efficacy in bipolar depression. Lamotrigine (D) is effective for depression prophylaxis but requires slow titration over 4-8 weeks to avoid Stevens-Johnson syndrome, making it unsuitable for acute treatment. Stimulants (B) are not indicated for bipolar depression. Lithium is already optimised in this patient. Memory hook: 'Bipolar depression: quetiapine first, avoid SSRI monotherapy - switch risk.'","tp":"Bipolar disorder","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p165","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with a 15-year history of daily alcohol use (~12 standard drinks/day) presents 14 hours after abrupt cessation with tremulous hands, sweating, nausea, tachycardia (HR 112/min), BP 150/96 mm Hg, agitation, and mild disorientation. He had one generalised seizure during a previous withdrawal episode. CIWA-Ar score is 18. What is the MOST appropriate first-line treatment?","o":["Intravenous phenytoin loading","Symptom-triggered benzodiazepine protocol (diazepam or lorazepam)","Carbamazepine monotherapy 400 mg/day","IV thiamine 100 mg only, with reassessment after 6 hours"],"a":1,"ex":"Benzodiazepines are first-line for alcohol withdrawal because they are GABA-A agonists and treat all withdrawal features, including seizure prophylaxis; symptom-triggered dosing guided by CIWA-Ar (treat when score >= 10-15) is preferred, so B is correct. Phenytoin (A) does not prevent alcohol-withdrawal seizures, unlike benzodiazepines. Carbamazepine (C) is only an alternative for mild outpatient withdrawal, not first-line. Thiamine (D) is an essential adjunct - given IV before any glucose to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy - but does not treat the withdrawal state itself. Memory hook: 'Withdrawal seizures need benzos, not phenytoin.'","tp":"Alcohol use disorders","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p166","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with schizophrenia (diagnosed 6 years) failed adequate trials of risperidone 6 mg/day for 10 weeks and olanzapine 20 mg/day for 10 weeks, with persistent delusions and auditory hallucinations, so clozapine was initiated and titrated to 300 mg/day. Three weeks later he presents with abdominal distension, absent bowel sounds, and no bowel movement for 4 days. CT abdomen shows a dilated large bowel without a transition point. What is the MOST likely diagnosis and the appropriate next step?","o":["Clozapine-induced ileus (gastrointestinal hypomotility); withhold clozapine, nil by mouth, laxatives and supportive decompression","Acute appendicitis; urgent appendicectomy","Mesenteric ischaemia; emergency laparotomy","Irritable bowel syndrome; add loperamide"],"a":0,"ex":"This is treatment-resistant schizophrenia (two adequate antipsychotic trials failed), correctly treated with clozapine. Clozapine's potent antimuscarinic and anti-serotonergic effects cause GI hypomotility affecting the entire bowel - constipation in up to 60% of patients, with ileus a potentially fatal complication. Management is stopping clozapine, decompression, and aggressive laxative therapy (A). Appendicitis (B) and mesenteric ischaemia (C) lack fever, peritonism, and CT transition point, and laparotomy would be disastrous. Loperamide (D) worsens hypomotility. Memory hook: 'Clozapine + constipation = stop and decompress; prophylactic laxatives always.'","tp":"Antipsychotic pharmacovigilance","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p167","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 19-year-old medical student has, for 14 months, experienced intrusive contamination thoughts ('germs are on my hands') that cause severe anxiety, which he neutralises by washing his hands more than 20 times daily (about 3 hours/day). He recognises the thoughts are excessive ('I know it is irrational, but I cannot stop') and avoids doorknobs and public washrooms. Y-BOCS score 28. He took sertraline 200 mg/day for 8 weeks with only 30% improvement. What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Increase sertraline to 300 mg/day","Alprazolam 0.25 mg three times daily","Add risperidone 0.5-2 mg/day as augmentation, along with exposure and response prevention","Switch to imipramine 75 mg/day"],"a":2,"ex":"The diagnosis is OCD with good insight; first-line treatment is exposure and response prevention (ERP) plus a full-dose SSRI (sertraline max 200 mg/day, fluoxetine 80, fluvoxamine 300). After a partial response at 8-12 weeks, evidence-based options are ERP intensification, switching SSRI, or augmentation with risperidone or aripiprazole, making C correct. A exceeds the sertraline maximum. Benzodiazepines (B) are ineffective in OCD and carry dependence risk. Clomipramine, not imipramine (D), is the TCA used in OCD and is second-line due to anticholinergic and arrhythmic side effects. Memory hook: 'SSRI at max dose + ERP; still stuck, add risperidone.'","tp":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p168","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old man presents with an 8-week history of depressed mood, anhedonia, 6 kg weight loss, early-morning awakening, psychomotor retardation, and guilt. He reports a voice saying 'you are worthless, end your life.' PHQ-9 score 26; he has passive death wishes but no plan. What is the MOST appropriate initial pharmacological treatment?","o":["Fluoxetine 20 mg/day monotherapy","Sertraline 100 mg/day with olanzapine 5-10 mg/day","Lithium carbonate monotherapy","Lorazepam 1 mg as needed only"],"a":1,"ex":"The mood-congruent auditory hallucination ('you are worthless') makes this major depressive disorder with psychotic features, which requires an antidepressant combined with an antipsychotic (the fluoxetine-olanzapine combination is FDA-approved; olanzapine, quetiapine, or aripiprazole are commonly used), so B is correct. Antidepressant monotherapy (A) is insufficient in psychotic depression and carries a higher relapse risk. Lithium (C) is an augmentation/relapse-prevention agent, not first-line monotherapy for MDD. Lorazepam alone (D) provides no antidepressant effect. ECT is the most effective option overall and should be considered early, especially for melancholic features, poor intake, or high suicide risk. Memory hook: 'Psychotic depression = antidepressant + antipsychotic (or ECT).'","tp":"Depressive disorders","s":"Psychiatry","qid":"p169","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p170","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Identify the process shown in the image.","o":["Marbling","Tattooing","Mummification","Embalming"],"a":0,"ex":"The image shows a characteristic branching, greenish-black discoloration pattern over the skin, resembling a marble-like network. This is known as marbling, a postmortem change seen during decomposition. Marbling occurs due to the breakdown of hemoglobin in blood vessels by bacteria during putrefaction. Hydrogen sulfide produced by bacterial action reacts with hemoglobin to form sulfhemoglobin, which stains the superficial veins. This produces a distinct reticular (branching) pattern along the course of subcutaneous veins. It is an early sign of putrefaction and commonly appears over the abdomen and chest. The other options are incorrect: 1. Tattooing refers to punctate abrasions caused by unburnt gunpowder. 2. Mummification is drying and preservation of the body in arid conditions. 3. Embalming is an artificial preservation process. Since the image clearly shows venous pattern discoloration typical of decomposition, the process is marbling.","tp":"AMTSL","s":"Forensic Medicine","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 68-year-old man (72 kg) undergoes right shoulder arthroscopy under an interscalene brachial plexus block. During injection of 0.5% ropivacaine 40 mL he complains of perioral numbness and tinnitus; within a minute he develops a generalised tonic-clonic seizure, then wide-complex tachycardia with hypotension (BP 70/40 mmHg). The injection is stopped immediately. What is the MOST appropriate next step in management?","o":["IV adrenaline 1 mg as a rapid push","IV amiodarone 300 mg over 10 minutes","IV 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 1 minute, followed by an infusion at 0.25 mL/kg/min","IV diazepam 10 mg and close observation"],"a":2,"ex":"The sequence of CNS signs (perioral numbness, tinnitus, seizures) followed by cardiovascular collapse indicates local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) from accidental intravascular injection of ropivacaine. Definitive treatment is 20% lipid emulsion: bolus 1.5 mL/kg over 1 minute (repeatable twice at 5-minute intervals) followed by an infusion of 0.25 mL/kg/min until haemodynamic stability; benzodiazepines are used only to control seizures and do not treat the cardiotoxicity. High-dose adrenaline impairs lipid resuscitation and should be avoided — only small titrated doses (10–20 mcg) are used if the patient arrests; amiodarone is reserved for arrhythmias refractory to lipid therapy. Memory hook: lipid emulsion '1.5 bolus, 0.25 infusion'.","tp":"Local anaesthetic toxicity","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p171","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 42-year-old man (110 kg, BMI 36) with acute pancreatitis requires emergency laparotomy. After preoxygenation, rapid sequence induction is performed with propofol and rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg. Direct laryngoscopy is Cormack–Lehane grade III; two further attempts with a bougie and a video laryngoscope fail. A second-generation supraglottic airway device is inserted but ventilation is impossible, and SpO2 falls from 98% to 82% within 2 minutes. What is the NEXT best step?","o":["Wake the patient and postpone surgery","Emergency front-of-neck access (scalpel cricothyrotomy)","Repeat direct laryngoscopy with a different blade","IV sugammadex 16 mg/kg followed by mask ventilation"],"a":1,"ex":"This is a cannot-intubate, cannot-oxygenate (CICO) situation — three failed intubation attempts plus failed supraglottic ventilation with desaturation. Per the 2022 ASA Difficult Airway Algorithm, CICO mandates immediate emergency front-of-neck access (scalpel cricothyrotomy is the preferred technique); further laryngoscopy attempts waste time and worsen hypoxia. Sugammadex is only useful when neuromuscular paralysis is the suspected cause of ventilation failure and still requires time to act, and waking the patient is impossible once SpO2 is falling. Memory hook: 'CICO → scalpel, now, not later'.","tp":"Difficult airway","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p172","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman (65 kg) undergoes laparoscopic cholecystectomy. Neuromuscular blockade is maintained with rocuronium; at the end of surgery the train-of-four (TOF) count is 4 and she receives neostigmine 2.5 mg with glycopyrrolate 0.5 mg. In the post-anaesthesia care unit she has a weak hand grip, cannot sustain a 5-second head lift, and SpO2 drops to 88% on air; the TOF ratio is 0.5. What is the MOST appropriate management?","o":["Observation with oxygen supplementation until the TOF ratio recovers spontaneously","Immediate tracheal reintubation and ventilation","A further dose of neostigmine 2.5 mg","IV sugammadex 2 mg/kg"],"a":3,"ex":"A TOF ratio below 0.9 indicates clinically significant residual neuromuscular blockade, an independent risk factor for upper airway obstruction, aspiration and hypoxaemia, so observation alone is unsafe. Sugammadex 2 mg/kg rapidly and completely reverses moderate rocuronium blockade (4 mg/kg for deep block with TOF 1–2, 16 mg/kg for immediate reversal), and is the correct choice here. Neostigmine has a ceiling effect and cannot be relied on for reversal at this depth, and reintubation is unnecessary when pharmacological reversal is available. Memory hook: 'TOF <0.9 never discharge; sugammadex 2/4/16'.","tp":"Neuromuscular blockade","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p173","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old primigravida received epidural analgesia during labour; accidental dural puncture with a 16G Tuohy needle was recognised at the time. She delivered vaginally 6 hours later. On the second postnatal day she develops a severe frontal headache that worsens on sitting or standing and is completely relieved by lying flat. She is afebrile, neck movements are full, and neurological examination is normal. Despite bed rest, fluids, paracetamol and caffeine, the headache persists at 72 hours and interferes with breastfeeding. What is the MOST appropriate definitive treatment?","o":["MRI of the brain before any intervention","Autologous epidural blood patch","Sumatriptan nasal spray","Diagnostic lumbar puncture to document low opening pressure"],"a":1,"ex":"Post-dural puncture headache (PDPH) is diagnosed by the classic postural headache after a wet tap, with fever and neck stiffness absent (which would otherwise raise suspicion of meningitis or cortical vein thrombosis). If symptoms persist beyond 24–48 hours despite conservative measures, the definitive treatment is an autologous epidural blood patch (approximately 20 mL of blood injected at the level of the puncture), which succeeds in 70–90% of cases. Sumatriptan is not established treatment, a diagnostic lumbar puncture would worsen the leak, and neuroimaging is not required before the blood patch when the presentation is typical. Memory hook: 'Postural headache + dural puncture → blood patch'.","tp":"Obstetric anaesthesia","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p174","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman (60 kg) with no known drug allergies receives IV cefazolin 1 g in the pre-operative ward 30 minutes before scheduled hysterectomy. Within 10 minutes she develops generalised urticaria, wheeze with bronchospasm, and hypotension (BP 74/42 mmHg, HR 128/min). The drug is stopped and oxygen is administered. What is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["IV hydrocortisone 200 mg","IV chlorpheniramine 10 mg","IV adrenaline 5 mg as a slow bolus","IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1 in 1000) into the anterolateral thigh"],"a":3,"ex":"Perioperative anaphylaxis to an IV antibiotic is an emergency: adrenaline is the first-line drug and the correct initial dose is 0.5 mg of 1 in 1000 (1 mg/mL) given intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh, repeated every 5 minutes as needed, along with fluids. Hydrocortisone and antihistamines are only adjuncts that do not reverse acute hypotension or bronchospasm. Intravenous adrenaline, if used, must be given in small titrated doses (50–100 mcg); a 5 mg IV bolus is lethal and is a classic trap option. Memory hook: 'Anaphylaxis: IM 0.5 mg 1:1000, repeat q5 min'.","tp":"Perioperative anaphylaxis","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p175","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on clopidogrel 75 mg/day (started 6 months ago after drug-eluting coronary stent placement) is scheduled for elective total knee replacement under spinal anaesthesia. Per current ASRA/ESRA guidelines, for how long should clopidogrel be withheld before neuraxial anaesthesia?","o":["24 hours","5–7 days","48–72 hours","Clopidogrel need not be stopped as the bleeding risk is low"],"a":1,"ex":"Clopidogrel irreversibly inhibits platelet P2Y12 receptors, and normal platelet function returns only as new platelets are produced, so it must be withheld for 5–7 days (ASRA recommends 5 days; AAGBI 5–7 days) before neuraxial blockade to minimise the risk of spinal epidural haematoma. Shorter intervals carry unacceptable bleeding risk, and there is no setting in which an antiplatelet agent need not be stopped for elective neuraxial anaesthesia. Memory hook: 'Clopidogrel 5–7 days, ticagrelor 3–5 days, prasugrel 7 days' (reversible agent ticagrelor needs less time than irreversible clopidogrel).","tp":"Regional anaesthesia","s":"Anaesthesia","qid":"p176","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old hypertensive man presents with sudden-onset 'thunderclap' headache 3 hours ago, followed by vomiting and neck stiffness. Non-contrast CT head shows diffuse hyperdense blood in the basal cisterns and sylvian fissures. What is the next best investigation?","o":["Repeat non-contrast CT head after 12 hours","CT angiography of the cerebral vessels","Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia","MRI brain with FLAIR sequences"],"a":1,"ex":"CT angiography is correct: once subarachnoid hemorrhage is confirmed on non-contrast CT, urgent CTA localizes the ruptured saccular aneurysm (usually circle of Willis) to guide intervention. Non-contrast CT within 6 hours of onset is ~97-100% sensitive, so repeating it adds nothing; lumbar puncture for xanthochromia is reserved for CT-negative suspected SAH and is best done ≥12 hours after the ictus, not when blood is already visible. MRI is less sensitive than CT for acute subarachnoid blood in the first hours, and DSA is used when CTA is negative/nondiagnostic or for treatment planning, not as the routine first test. Hook: 'CT first for blood, CTA next for the aneurysm, LP only when CT is negative.'","tp":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","s":"Radiology","qid":"p177","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with compensated HBV cirrhosis (Child-Pugh A) is found on surveillance ultrasound to have a 3.5 cm liver lesion. Contrast-enhanced MRI shows arterial phase hyperenhancement of the lesion with washout on the portal venous phase and an enhancing rim (capsule). Serum AFP is 320 ng/mL. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Follow-up MRI after 6 months","Diagnose hepatocellular carcinoma non-invasively and proceed to staging; no biopsy required","Percutaneous liver biopsy is mandatory before any treatment","PET-CT to confirm the diagnosis"],"a":1,"ex":"In a cirrhotic patient, arterial phase hyperenhancement plus washout on the portal venous/delayed phase with an enhancing capsule in a lesion ≥1 cm is diagnostic of HCC (LI-RADS-5), and biopsy is not required before staging or treatment. Repeat imaging would be wrong because the lesion already meets non-invasive diagnostic criteria; a high AFP supports but does not substitute for these imaging criteria. PET-CT is not part of the HCC diagnostic algorithm, and biopsy carries tract-seeding risk and is reserved for atypical lesions. Hook: 'Arterial hyperenhancement + washout + capsule in cirrhosis = HCC by imaging alone.'","tp":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","s":"Radiology","qid":"p178","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman with SLE on long-term corticosteroids presents with progressive right hip pain of 3 months duration. MRI of the right hip shows a serpentine 'double line sign' in the right femoral head with no collapse of the articular surface. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Core decompression of the femoral head","Total hip arthroplasty","Conservative management with protected weight-bearing","Bisphosphonate therapy"],"a":0,"ex":"The double line sign — an inner T2 hyperintense rim (granulation tissue) with an outer hypointense sclerotic rim — is pathognomonic of avascular necrosis; steroid use is the key risk factor here. In pre-collapse disease (as in this patient), core decompression is the appropriate surgical option to preserve the femoral head. Total hip arthroplasty is reserved for post-collapse or advanced disease, protected weight-bearing alone is inadequate once necrosis is established, and bisphosphonates are not standard therapy for AVN. Hook: 'Double line = double trouble — decompress before the head collapses.'","tp":"Avascular necrosis of femoral head","s":"Radiology","qid":"p179","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p180","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"In the diagram illustrating bacterial cell wall synthesis, at which step do beta-lactam antibiotics exert their action?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":3,"ex":"The diagram illustrates the synthesis of peptidoglycan, the structural component of the bacterial cell wall. The final and crucial step in this process is transpeptidation, which is indicated by label 4 in a typical diagram of this process. This step involves the formation of peptide cross-links between adjacent polysaccharide chains, a reaction catalyzed by enzymes known as transpeptidases (also called penicillin-binding proteins, or PBPs). This cross-linking gives the cell wall its strength and rigidity. Beta-lactam antibiotics, such as penicillins and cephalosporins, are structural analogues of the D-alanyl-D-alanine substrate of these enzymes. They bind to the active site of the PBPs and irreversibly inhibit them, blocking the transpeptidation step. This leads to the formation of a defective cell wall and subsequent cell lysis. The other steps, such as precursor synthesis (1), membrane transport (2), and transglycosylation (3), are targets for other classes of antibiotics.","tp":"Ossification Age Estimation","s":"Pharmacology","img":true,"imgdata":["","","",""]},{"q":"A 52-year-old asymptomatic woman undergoing screening mammography shows a 2 cm segmental cluster of pleomorphic microcalcifications in the right upper outer quadrant with no associated mass. What is the most appropriate next step?","o":["Stereotactic-guided core biopsy","Ultrasound-guided core biopsy","Contrast-enhanced MRI of the breast","Short-interval follow-up mammogram after 6 months"],"a":0,"ex":"Pleomorphic (granular, heterogeneous) microcalcifications in a segmental distribution are highly suspicious (BI-RADS 4C) and warrant stereotactic (or tomosynthesis-guided) vacuum-assisted core biopsy, which is the only reliable way to target microcalcifications. Ultrasound often cannot visualize microcalcifications without an associated mass, so US-guided biopsy is inappropriate; a 6-month follow-up is inadequate for suspicious calcifications, and MRI assesses disease extent but is not the primary biopsy strategy. Hook: 'Pleomorphic + segmental = biopsy — and stereotaxis, not ultrasound, aims at calcium.'","tp":"Breast microcalcifications","s":"Radiology","qid":"p181","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman, 48 hours after elective knee arthroplasty, develops sudden severe dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain. Blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg, pulse 130/min, SpO2 88% on room air. CT pulmonary angiography shows a saddle embolus. What is the most appropriate immediate management?","o":["Systemic intravenous thrombolysis","Therapeutic-dose IV heparin infusion alone","Retrievable inferior vena cava filter","Surgical pulmonary embolectomy"],"a":0,"ex":"The ESC 2019 guideline classifies this as high-risk (massive) PE — shock or hypotension with a saddle embolus — mandating immediate primary reperfusion with systemic intravenous thrombolysis. Heparin alone is first-line only in intermediate-risk (hemodynamically stable) PE; surgical or catheter-directed embolectomy is reserved for when thrombolysis is contraindicated or fails; and an IVC filter prevents future emboli but does not treat the existing obstructing embolus. Hook: 'Shock with PE = thrombolyze, don't heparinize.'","tp":"Acute pulmonary embolism","s":"Radiology","qid":"p182","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with known prostate cancer (PSA 150 ng/mL) undergoes a Tc-99m MDP bone scan. The scan shows diffusely increased, symmetric tracer uptake in the axial skeleton with absent renal activity. What is the most likely interpretation?","o":["Metabolic bone disease such as renal osteodystrophy","Extensive skeletal metastases from prostate cancer","Normal age-related scan","Failed injection of the radiopharmaceutical"],"a":1,"ex":"Diffuse symmetric skeletal uptake with absent renal and soft-tissue activity is a 'superscan' pattern; in the context of advanced prostate cancer with a very high PSA, it indicates extensive skeletal metastases. Metabolic bone disease (renal osteodystrophy, hyperparathyroidism) and myelofibrosis can produce a similar pattern, so clinical context decides — but metastases are overwhelmingly the most likely cause here. A normal scan would show visible kidneys with heterogeneous, not uniformly increased, uptake, and a failed injection would show soft-tissue extravasation without a skeletal distribution. Hook: 'No kidneys visible + bright bones = superscan; in prostate cancer read it as diffuse mets.'","tp":"Bone scintigraphy","s":"Radiology","qid":"p183","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"An 8-month-old boy presents with episodes of colicky crying, vomiting and a 'currant jelly' stool. Abdominal ultrasound shows a 'target sign' in the right upper quadrant. The child is hemodynamically stable with no features of peritonitis. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Pneumatic (air) enema reduction under fluoroscopic or ultrasound guidance","Immediate laparotomy","Intravenous fluids and antibiotics with observation","Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy with retrieval"],"a":0,"ex":"The target (doughnut) sign on transverse ultrasound is the classic sonographic finding of intussusception. In a stable child without peritonitis or perforation, pneumatic (air) enema reduction is the standard first-line non-operative treatment, successful in about 80-90% of cases. Immediate laparotomy is reserved for peritonitis, perforation or failed enema reduction; observation and antibiotics are not therapeutic, and endoscopy is not part of the standard approach. Hook: 'Target sign, stable child → air in; peritonitis or failed reduction → scalpel.'","tp":"Intussusception","s":"Radiology","qid":"p184","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 26-year-old male construction worker fell on his outstretched hand 4 months ago and was diagnosed with a scaphoid waist fracture, treated in a below-elbow thumb spica cast for 12 weeks. He now has persistent radial-side wrist pain. X-ray shows a clear gap at the fracture site, cystic changes, and increased density (sclerosis) of the proximal fragment. What is the BEST next step in management?","o":["Continue cast immobilization for another 8 weeks","Open reduction with non-vascularized corticocancellous (Russe) bone graft and K-wire fixation","Vascularized bone graft based on the 1,2-intercompartmental supraretinacular artery (1,2-ICSRA) with internal fixation","Proximal row carpectomy"],"a":2,"ex":"Correct: vascularized bone graft. The scaphoid receives 70-80% of its blood supply retrograde via the dorsal carpal branch of the radial artery, which enters at the dorsal ridge — a waist fracture therefore devascularizes the proximal pole. Sclerosis of the proximal fragment on X-ray is the radiographic sign of avascular necrosis. Established nonunion with proximal-pole AVN will not heal with a non-vascularized Russe graft (choice B); continued casting (A) is futile at 4 months, and proximal row carpectomy (D) is a salvage procedure for scaphoid nonunion advanced collapse (SNAC) arthritis, not a nonunion without arthritis. Memory hook: 'Retrograde blood supply → proximal pole dies first.'","tp":"Scaphoid fractures","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p185","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 45-year-old farmer sustains a crush injury to his right leg when a tractor falls on it. There is a 9 cm wound over the middle third of the tibia with exposed bone and periosteal stripping, severe muscle contusion, and no active bleeding; dorsalis pedis pulse is palpable. He reaches the emergency department 2 hours after injury. Which of the following describes the correct classification and immediate management?","o":["Gustilo IIIB; urgent wound debridement with copious saline lavage, IV co-amoxiclav/cefuroxime started immediately along with tetanus prophylaxis, provisional skeletal stabilization, and delayed primary closure","Gustilo IIIA; wound washout with immediate primary skin closure and oral antibiotics","Gustilo IIIC; urgent vascular exploration and repair before wound debridement","Gustilo IIIB; IV antibiotics alone with definitive debridement deferred for 72 hours"],"a":0,"ex":"Correct: Gustilo IIIB with immediate antibiotics and urgent debridement. Periosteal stripping with exposed bone and severe soft tissue damage qualifies as IIIB, regardless of wound size; IIIC (choice C) requires arterial injury needing repair — the palpable pulse excludes it. IIIA (choice B) implies adequate soft tissue coverage, and primary closure of an open fracture is wrong — wounds are left open with delayed primary closure at 48-72 hours after a 'second look'. IV antibiotics must start within 1 hour of arrival (co-amoxiclav/cefuroxime; add an aminoglycoside for gross contamination). Memory hook: 'Antibiotics in 1 hour, debridement without delay, close it later.'","tp":"Open fractures","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p186","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy fell from a swing onto his outstretched left hand 4 hours ago. The left elbow is swollen with an 'S'-shaped deformity; X-ray shows a Gartland type III extension-type supracondylar fracture. The radial pulse is not palpable, but the hand is pink and warm with brisk capillary refill. What is the best next step?","o":["Observe for 12 hours with limb elevation and serial pulse checks","Immediate brachial arteriography","Urgent closed reduction with percutaneous K-wire fixation, then reassess distal perfusion","Open exploration of the brachial artery before fracture fixation"],"a":2,"ex":"Correct: urgent closed reduction and pinning, then reassess. A 'pink, pulseless' hand after supracondylar fracture is usually due to arterial entrapment or spasm at the fracture site; the pulse commonly returns once the displaced Gartland III fragment is reduced, so reduction and K-wiring come first. Arteriography (B) and immediate open exploration (D) delay definitive reduction and are reserved for a hand that is pale/cold (or remains pulseless with poor perfusion) after reduction. Elevation alone (A) is unacceptable for a completely displaced fracture with absent pulse. Memory hook: 'Pink and pulseless = reduce and pin; pale and pulseless = explore.'","tp":"Supracondylar humerus fractures","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p187","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman, cognitively intact and independently mobile in the community, slipped and fell at home. She has right hip pain with a shortened, externally rotated leg. X-ray shows a displaced subcapital (Garden type IV) fracture of the neck of the femur. She is medically optimized. As per current guidelines (NICE), what is the recommended surgical treatment?","o":["Hemiarthroplasty","Total hip replacement (THR)","Internal fixation with cannulated screws","Dynamic hip screw (DHS)"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: total hip replacement. NICE recommends THR for displaced intracapsular (Garden III-IV) fractures in patients who are cognitively intact and independently mobile pre-fracture, as it gives better function and lower reoperation rates than hemiarthroplasty (A). Hemiarthroplasty is preferred in frail, cognitively impaired, or low-demand patients. Cannulated screws (C) are for undisplaced (Garden I-II) intracapsular fractures, and DHS (D) is for extracapsular (intertrochanteric) fractures. Surgery should be performed within 24-48 hours of admission. Memory hook: 'Displaced Garden III-IV in a fit patient → THR; unfit → hemiarthroplasty; undisplaced → fix.'","tp":"Neck of femur fractures","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p188","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman with a recent low-trauma vertebral compression fracture and a femoral neck T-score of −3.4 is classified as being at very high fracture risk. She was started on romosozumab 210 mg subcutaneously monthly. Twelve months later her bone mineral density has improved. According to current (Endocrine Society 2024) guidance, what should be done next?","o":["Continue romosozumab for another 12 months","Stop romosozumab and start alendronate","Stop all pharmacological therapy and rely on calcium and vitamin D alone","Switch to oral raloxifene"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: stop romosozumab and start an antiresorptive, preferably a bisphosphonate. Romosozumab (anti-sclerostin monoclonal antibody) is an anabolic agent approved for a maximum 12-month course in very-high-risk patients; bone gains are rapidly lost once it is stopped, so sequential therapy with an antiresorptive (alendronate) is mandatory to maintain the improvement. Continuing beyond 12 months (A) is not recommended (rare ONJ/atypical femoral fracture risk and cardiovascular caution). Calcium with vitamin D (C) is an adjunct, never sufficient after anabolic therapy; raloxifene (D) has weaker efficacy and is not the preferred sequential agent. Memory hook: 'Anabolic first in very high risk, then hand over to a bisphosphonate.'","tp":"Osteoporosis","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p189","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p190","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Interpret the following arterial blood gas (ABG) values: pH=7.5, pCO2=50 mm Hg, HCO3-=30 mEq/L","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"a":1,"ex":"To interpret an ABG, we follow a systematic approach. First, check the pH. A pH of 7.5 is above the normal range (7.35-7.45), indicating alkalosis. Second, determine if the primary cause is respiratory or metabolic. The pCO2 is 50 mm Hg, which is higher than the normal range (35-45 mm Hg). An elevated pCO2 causes acidosis, which is opposite to the patient's alkalotic state, so the primary problem is not respiratory. The HCO3- is 30 mEq/L, which is higher than the normal range (22-26 mEq/L). An elevated HCO3- causes alkalosis. Therefore, the primary disorder is metabolic alkalosis. The elevated pCO2 represents a respiratory compensation, where the body hypoventilates to retain CO2 (an acid) to counteract the metabolic alkalosis.","tp":"Male Pelvic Anatomy","s":"Biochemistry","img":true,"imgdata":[""]},{"q":"A 32-year-old man sustains a closed both-bone fracture of the right forearm and a cast is applied at a district hospital. Over 6 hours he develops progressively severe pain out of proportion to the injury, requiring escalating analgesia. The forearm is tense and exquisitely tender; passive extension of the fingers causes severe pain, and there is paresthesia in the median nerve territory. Compartment pressure measures 45 mmHg; diastolic BP is 70 mmHg. What is the best next step?","o":["Elevate the limb, apply ice and continue analgesia with observation","Split the cast and reassess in 2 hours","Immediate fasciotomy of the volar and dorsal forearm compartments","IV mannitol and furosemide"],"a":2,"ex":"Correct: immediate fasciotomy. Diagnosis of compartment syndrome is primarily clinical — pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, paresthesia and a tense compartment. Pressure measurement supports it: fasciotomy is indicated when the compartment pressure is within 30 mmHg of diastolic (ΔP = 70 − 45 = 25 mmHg < 30). Elevation (A) lowers arterial perfusion pressure and worsens ischaemia; cast splitting alone (B) is insufficient once the syndrome is established (the skin itself can become a constricting layer); mannitol (D) is unproven. Irreversible muscle and nerve damage occurs within hours — the result is Volkmann ischaemic contracture. Memory hook: 'ΔP < 30 mmHg or pain on passive stretch → cut fascia now.'","tp":"Compartment syndrome","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p191","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy presents with 3 months of progressive pain over the right distal thigh, worse at night, with visible swelling. X-ray shows a mixed lytic-sclerotic lesion with a sunburst periosteal reaction, Codman's triangle and an associated soft tissue mass. Serum alkaline phosphatase is elevated. What is the best next step in management?","o":["Above-knee amputation","Core needle biopsy planned along the line of the future definitive surgical incision, performed by the treating surgeon","Start neoadjuvant chemotherapy without tissue diagnosis","Radiotherapy as the primary modality"],"a":1,"ex":"Correct: biopsy performed by the treating orthopaedic oncology surgeon, with the biopsy tract planned to lie within the future resection field. Age, distal femoral location, sunburst reaction and Codman's triangle strongly suggest osteosarcoma, but chemotherapy and resection cannot begin without histological confirmation (choice C is wrong). The biopsy tract is excised en bloc at definitive surgery, so a poorly placed biopsy can ruin limb-salvage options. Amputation (A) without staging and neoadjuvant chemotherapy (methotrexate, doxorubicin, cisplatin) is obsolete; osteosarcoma is radioresistant (D) — radiosensitivity is a feature of Ewing's sarcoma (onion-peel reaction, t(11;22)). Memory hook: 'The biopsy tract belongs to the surgeon's future incision.'","tp":"Bone tumours","s":"Orthopaedics","qid":"p192","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy presents with a 3-week history of a painful, boggy, purulent scalp swelling with patchy hair loss and tender regional lymphadenopathy. KOH mount of plucked hairs shows ectothrix spores and fungal hyphae. What is the most appropriate treatment?","o":["Oral griseofulvin 20 mg/kg/day for 8-12 weeks","Oral terbinafine 250 mg/day for 2 weeks","Topical clotrimazole cream twice daily for 4 weeks","Incision and drainage of the scalp swelling followed by topical miconazole"],"a":0,"ex":"This is kerion, an inflammatory (boggy, purulent) form of tinea capitis, usually due to Microsporum or Trichophyton species. Oral griseofulvin remains the drug of choice for tinea capitis in children because it is highly effective against Microsporum and safe in this age group; 8-12 weeks of therapy is required. Terbinafine is less active against Microsporum and 2 weeks is far too short. Topical antifungals cannot penetrate the hair follicle and never suffice for tinea capitis. The kerion is a fungal hypersensitivity reaction, not an abscess — incision and drainage is contraindicated and may cause scarring alopecia. Hook: 'Kerion = inflammatory tinea capitis — treat with oral griseofulvin, never incise.'","tp":"Tinea capitis","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p193","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 40-year-old man presents with large, ill-defined, erythematous plaques with central clearing and peripheral pustules over the trunk and groin, present for 6 months. He has been applying a 'Candid' combination cream (clobetasol + terbinafine + ofloxacin) purchased from a pharmacy. KOH mount shows septate hyphae; culture grows Trichophyton indotineae. What is the most appropriate management?","o":["Continue the same combination cream for 2 more months","Oral terbinafine 250 mg/day for 2 weeks","Stop the combination cream and start oral itraconazole 200 mg/day for 4 weeks, extending if needed","Topical luliconazole 1% cream twice daily alone"],"a":2,"ex":"This is steroid-modified tinea (tinea incognito) caused by irrational fixed-dose combination creams (clobetasol + antifungal + antibiotic), which are banned in India; the potent steroid suppresses inflammation while the fungus spreads, producing ill-defined borders and pustules. Trichophyton indotineae, the dominant species in Indian dermatophytosis, shows high terbinafine resistance (squatinate mutation in squalene epoxidase gene), so IADVL guidelines recommend oral itraconazole 200 mg/day — typically 4 weeks minimum, extended until clinical and mycological clearance (often 6-8 weeks). Continuing the combination cream worsens the disease; topical therapy alone is inadequate for widespread involvement; terbinafine is the wrong agent here. Hook: 'Indian tinea = itraconazole-first; terbinafine resistance is endemic.'","tp":"Dermatophytosis (tinea incognito)","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p194","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p195","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"Identify the visual field defect shown in the image.","o":["Central scotoma","Roennes Nasal step","Blind spot enlargement","Arcuate scotoma"],"a":2,"ex":"Blind spot enlargement The image shows a visual field test (perimetry chart) with a darkened area located temporally in each eye, corresponding to the location of the physiological blind spot. The abnormal finding here is that this area is larger than normal, indicating enlargement of the blind spot. The blind spot represents the point where the optic nerve exits the retina, known as the optic disc. Since this area lacks photoreceptors, it normally appears as a small scotoma in the temporal visual field of each eye. Blind spot enlargement commonly occurs in conditions that cause swelling of the optic disc, such as papilledema due to raised intracranial pressure. When the optic disc becomes swollen, the area without photoreceptors expands, leading to a larger blind spot on visual field testing. The other options show different patterns:","tp":"Haemorrhagic Disease of Newborn","s":"Ophthalmology","img":true,"imgdata":["",""]},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has severe plaque psoriasis (PASI 24, DLQI 25) with psoriatic arthritis and a 3-year history of moderately active Crohn's disease on mesalamine. He is biologic-naive. Which is the most appropriate systemic therapy?","o":["Secukinumab","Guselkumab","Apremilast","Methotrexate"],"a":1,"ex":"IL-17 inhibitors (secukinumab, ixekizumab) can exacerbate or unmask inflammatory bowel disease and are contraindicated in patients with active Crohn's disease. IL-23p19 inhibitors (guselkumab, risankizumab) and ustekinumab are IBD-safe and highly effective for both skin and joint disease, making guselkumab the correct first-line choice. Apremilast is too weak for PASI 24 disease, and methotrexate has modest efficacy in psoriasis and is not adequate therapy for active Crohn's disease. Hook: 'Crohn's + psoriasis → anti-IL-23 (guselkumab/risankizumab) or ustekinumab; never anti-IL-17.'","tp":"Psoriasis (biologics)","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p196","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman develops fever and a widespread erythematous rash with flaccid blisters and epidermal detachment involving 30% of her body surface area, 12 days after starting carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia. Nikolsky sign is positive. Besides withdrawing the drug and supportive care in a burns unit, a randomized trial has shown improved survival with which of the following?","o":["IVIG 2 g/kg over 2 days","Methylprednisolone 1 g/day for 3 days","Etanercept 50 mg as a single early subcutaneous dose","Plasmapheresis"],"a":2,"ex":"This is toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS/TEN overlap with 30% detachment) — a severe drug reaction best managed in a burn unit with SCORTEN-based prognostication. The NEJM 2018 randomized trial by Wang et al. showed that a single early dose of etanercept 50 mg reduced observed mortality compared with prednisolone; current guidelines (IADVL 2023) endorse anti-TNF agents (etanercept/infliximab) or cyclosporine as preferred systemic options. High-dose systemic corticosteroids remain controversial, IVIG has failed to show a mortality benefit in meta-analyses, and plasmapheresis lacks evidence. Hook: 'SJS/TEN: stop the drug, supportive care, etanercept or cyclosporine — NOT high-dose steroids or IVIG as first line.'","tp":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome / TEN","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p197","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with severe hidradenitis suppurativa (Hurley stage III, lesions in both axillae and groins) has failed adalimumab 40 mg weekly after 6 months. Surgery is not feasible at present. Which biologic uniquely provides dual blockade of both IL-17A and IL-17F and received US FDA approval for hidradenitis suppurativa in 2024?","o":["Bimekizumab","Secukinumab","Infliximab","Ustekinumab"],"a":0,"ex":"Bimekizumab is the first IL-17 inhibitor that neutralizes both IL-17A and IL-17F; it was FDA-approved for hidradenitis suppurativa in November 2024 and is an appropriate choice after anti-TNF (adalimumab) failure. Secukinumab (approved for HS in 2023) blocks only IL-17A, not IL-17F, so it does not satisfy the dual-blockade criterion. Infliximab is another anti-TNF and is used off-label but represents the same pathway that already failed. Ustekinumab (IL-12/23 inhibitor) has failed to show benefit in phase 2 HS trials. Hook: 'Bimekizumab = dual IL-17A/IL-17F blockade — HS, psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis.'","tp":"Hidradenitis suppurativa","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p198","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"q":"A 55-year-old man undergoes wide local excision of a 3.5 mm Breslow thickness, ulcerated melanoma on his back; sentinel lymph node biopsy is negative. Staging is IIB (T3bN0M0) and the tumour is BRAF V600E wild-type. What is the most appropriate adjuvant therapy?","o":["Observation only","Adjuvant pembrolizumab 200 mg every 3 weeks for up to 17 cycles (about 1 year)","Dabrafenib plus trametinib","High-dose interferon alfa-2b for 1 year"],"a":1,"ex":"The KEYNOTE-716 trial showed that adjuvant pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1) significantly improves recurrence-free survival in resected stage IIB and IIC melanoma, and it is now the standard of care for stage IIB and above, including after negative sentinel node biopsy. BRAF/MEK inhibitor combinations (dabrafenib + trametinib) require a BRAF V600 mutation, which is absent here, and observation is only appropriate for stage IIA and lower. High-dose interferon is obsolete due to poor tolerability and minimal survival benefit. Hook: 'Adjuvant anti-PD-1 starts at stage IIB melanoma — not just stage III/IV.'","tp":"Melanoma","s":"Dermatology","qid":"p199","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","img":false},{"qid":"p200","e":"NEET PG","y":"2026","src":"paper2026","q":"A 65-year-old patient presented with binasal visual field loss. Imaging revealed a space- occupying lesion in the brain, for which the patient is receiving treatment. What is the most probable location of this lesion?","o":["Chiasma","Rt. Optic tract","Lt. Optic tract","Lt. Visual cortex"],"a":0,"ex":"Chiasma The visual pathway has a precise organization. The optic chiasm is where the nerve fibers from the nasal (medial) half of each retina cross to the opposite side, while the temporal (lateral) fibers remain uncrossed. The nasal retinal fibers are responsible for vision in the temporal visual fields. A lesion that compresses the optic chiasm from the center (like a pituitary adenoma) will damage these crossing nasal fibers from both eyes. This results in the loss of vision in both temporal fields, a defect known as bitemporal hemianopia. The question describes binasal vision loss, which is the loss of vision in the fields closest to the nose. This rare defect is caused by bilateral lesions compressing the lateral, uncrossed fibers of the optic chiasm, for example, from bilateral internal carotid artery aneurysms. However, chiasmal lesions are the only single location that can produce bilateral field defects like this. The options likely contain an error, intending to say bitemporal, which points directly to the chiasm.","tp":"Local Anaesthetic Dosing","s":"Ophthalmology","img":true,"imgdata":["","","","",""]}];
